window.UNITY_DOCS_INDEX = [{"title": "ATLAS Analysis on Unity", "url": "index.html", "text": "UMass Unity provides the compute resources. CVMFS supplies the ATLAS software, and an Enterprise Linux 9 container provides the runtime for ATLAS releases on Unity's Ubuntu hosts. Pixi manages project dependencies and personal tools. \n This guide documents how to use Unity as an analysis facility for ATLAS, from an interactive shell to a reproducible analysis environment. \n \n Start here \n Setting up the environment → \n Create your work/project directories once, save  USER_PI  and the shell shortcuts, request CPU or GPU resources, enter an EL9 container, and load the software needed for your analysis. \n Grid certificates and authentication → \n Obtain and register a personal grid certificate, install its encrypted private key on Unity, and create a short-lived ATLAS proxy. Includes renewal and troubleshooting for credential and output-path errors. \n Submitting jobs with Slurm → \n Turn the interactive environment into a batch job. Includes downloadable scripts, a PyROOT smoke test, resource requests, logs, and troubleshooting. \n Running on datasets with Slurm → \n Request a dataset replica at NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK, freeze a complete Rucio file manifest, and read it directly through XRootD from Unity jobs. Includes worker proxies, bounded arrays, validated outputs, retries, and merging. \n Using Pixi → \n Install Pixi in your user area, manage personal command-line tools with  pixi global , and use  pixi install  and  pixi run  with an existing project's environment. \n Using VS Code → \n Connect to an allocated node, start a VS Code backend inside EL9, and configure integrated ATLAS Python execution, debugging, and notebook kernels. Includes SSH and tunnel methods. \n Using JupyterLab → \n Start a Pixi-based notebook server in your own Slurm session, optionally inside EL9, and connect from your browser through an SSH tunnel. Check the kernel, save notebooks on Unity, and reconnect or finish the session. \n Codex Agents → \n Run Codex with your ATLAS release inside EL9. Install personal Rucio and AMI MCP servers, authenticate with a grid proxy, and connect them to the agent for dataset discovery and metadata queries. \n \n How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Grid proxy \n Authenticate to ATLAS grid services using your registered certificate identity and VO attributes. \n \n \n Rucio and NET2 storage \n Arrange and retain dataset replicas; supply registered XRootD URLs for direct worker reads. \n \n \n Pixi environment \n Supply a project's own packages and command-line tools. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. A self-contained Pixi project that supports Ubuntu can also run directly on the allocated host, as described in the Pixi and JupyterLab chapters. \n The environment chapter defines the directory settings and shell helpers used throughout this guide. The grid-certificate chapter prepares the identity and proxy used for authenticated grid access, including the Codex MCP servers. The Pixi chapter supplies the package-management basics used by JupyterLab and Codex. Choose VS Code, JupyterLab, or Codex according to how you want to work; you do not need to start all three. \n About this guide \n The chapters cover interactive environment setup, grid certificates and authentication, Slurm jobs on NET2 datasets, Pixi, VS Code, JupyterLab, and Codex with authenticated catalog access. Further chapters will extend analysis workflows and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the page, command, error message, and compute-node hostname. Include the relevant software versions and whether you are on the Ubuntu host or inside EL9. Omit credentials, tokens, and private keys. \n"}, {"title": "How the environment fits together", "url": "index.html#how-the-environment-fits-together", "text": "How the environment fits together \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Its role \n \n \n \n \n Unity login node \n Connect, edit files, and request resources. \n \n \n Slurm allocation \n Reserve CPUs, memory, time, and optional GPUs on a compute node. \n \n \n Apptainer container \n Provide the EL9 userspace required by the ATLAS release. \n \n \n CVMFS \n Deliver centrally maintained software and container images. \n \n \n Grid proxy \n Authenticate to ATLAS grid services using your registered certificate identity and VO attributes. \n \n \n Rucio and NET2 storage \n Arrange and retain dataset replicas; supply registered XRootD URLs for direct worker reads. \n \n \n Pixi environment \n Supply a project's own packages and command-line tools. \n \n \n Your work directory \n Keep analysis code, builds, inputs, and outputs on writable Unity storage. \n \n \n \n Start the container  after  the allocation has started. The container runs within that allocation; it does not reserve additional resources. A self-contained Pixi project that supports Ubuntu can also run directly on the allocated host, as described in the Pixi and JupyterLab chapters. \n The environment chapter defines the directory settings and shell helpers used throughout this guide. The grid-certificate chapter prepares the identity and proxy used for authenticated grid access, including the Codex MCP servers. The Pixi chapter supplies the package-management basics used by JupyterLab and Codex. Choose VS Code, JupyterLab, or Codex according to how you want to work; you do not need to start all three. \n"}, {"title": "About this guide", "url": "index.html#about-this-guide", "text": "About this guide \n The chapters cover interactive environment setup, grid certificates and authentication, Slurm jobs on NET2 datasets, Pixi, VS Code, JupyterLab, and Codex with authenticated catalog access. Further chapters will extend analysis workflows and machine learning as those procedures are established. \n The  Unity documentation  is the reference for cluster access, storage, and scheduling. The  ATLAS Software Documentation  is the reference for ATLAS software and analysis procedures. \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the page, command, error message, and compute-node hostname. Include the relevant software versions and whether you are on the Ubuntu host or inside EL9. Omit credentials, tokens, and private keys. \n"}, {"title": "Setting up the environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html", "text": "Use a Slurm allocation for your work, then start an EL9 container on the allocated compute node. Inside it, ATLAS software is available through CVMFS without a local installation. \n \n Before you begin:  you need a Unity account, access to a group work directory, and a Bash shell. CVMFS must be accessible on the  compute node , not just the login node. \n \n 1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_USERNAME with your Unity username.\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n 2. Create your working directories once \n On the  Unity login node , set  USER_PI  to your PI's existing Unity directory name. Unity uses the convention  pi_[PI's username] : the prefix  pi_  followed by the PI's Unity username, for example  pi_rclsa_umass_edu . Substitute that complete directory name, including  pi_ , for  YOUR_PI .  $USER  is already your own Unity username after logging in, so leave it as a variable. \n # Unity login node — replace YOUR_PI, for example with pi_rclsa_umass_edu.\nexport USER_PI=YOUR_PI\nmkdir -p \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}\"\nmkdir -p \"/project/${USER_PI}/${USER}\"\n\n# The examples throughout this manual use this analysis subdirectory.\nmkdir -p \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" \n Create these directories only once , not for every allocation or container.  mkdir -p  also succeeds if they already exist. Your PI's storage allocation must already be provisioned and accessible; these commands create your own subdirectories, not a new storage allocation. \n Add your edited  export USER_PI=...  line to your existing  ~/.bashrc  so the choice persists across sessions. The complete  shell configuration below  includes this line and defines  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" ; keep a single definition of each when merging it. Directory creation stays outside  .bashrc . \n ATLAS_WORKDIR  is your common analysis work area. Later chapters use  \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"  for an individual analysis checkout; replace  PROJECT_NAME  with that repository's directory name. \n Throughout the manual,  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is your personal work area and  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is your personal area in your PI's project allocation. Use  /work  or Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas and retention. \n 3. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n 4. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\n# Forward the directory variables explicitly into Apptainer.\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER_PI=\"$USER_PI\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER=\"$USER\"\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Use the analysis subdirectory you created in the one-time setup: \n # Ubuntu compute node\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Subsequent chapters assume the  shell configuration below  is installed, so they reuse  ATLAS_WORKDIR  and the allocation/container shortcuts. \n 5. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment. Unity exposes  /work  inside the container without  -m /work , so no explicit mount is needed for the standard workflow. File permissions still apply. \n Use  -m  only when a directory you need is  not already visible  inside the container. For example, if  /scratch  is missing, leave the container and relaunch with  setupATLAS -c el9 -m /scratch . Check the paths you need after entering; automatic mounts can depend on the launcher and site configuration.  setupATLAS -c -h  describes additional mount options. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work\nprintf 'PI: %s\\nUser: %s\\n' \"$USER_PI\" \"$USER\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\" \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may initially map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. The explicit  cd  returns to the shared analysis directory. The  APPTAINERENV_  exports above preserve  USER_PI ,  USER , and  ATLAS_WORKDIR  inside Apptainer; other host variables need not be preserved. If you need project files, also check that  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is visible, using the mount guidance above if needed. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n 6. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . Throughout this manual,  ATLAS_RELEASE  means the complete  project,version  pair: for example,  AnalysisBase  followed by a comma and your exact release number. Enter that pair at the prompt: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Use an exact version rather than  latest ; the analysis determines the supported release.  ATLAS_RELEASE  is a session variable, so set it again when starting a new session or submitting from a different shell. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n 7. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Pass  --nv  explicitly to the container runtime through  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS . This exposes the host's NVIDIA utilities and driver libraries inside EL9. The following sequence preserves any existing container options and forwards Slurm's device selection: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node; module load apptainer/latest was done above.\n(\n    export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n    if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\n    if [[ ${CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=\"$CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER\"\n    fi\n    setupATLAS -c el9\n) \n The enclosing parentheses run the launch in a subshell. When you exit the container, these exports disappear and the host shell's previous container options are preserved. Set these options  before starting the container ; setting them inside an already running container cannot add the missing GPU mounts. \n Then check  inside  the container: \n command -v nvidia-smi\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output for  apptainer ... --nv ... . This guide passes the option explicitly through the ATLAS launcher;  APPTAINER_NV=true  is Apptainer's environment-variable equivalent. \n If  nvidia-smi  is still missing, try  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi  inside the container. If the full path works but the bare command does not, inspect  PATH . If the file is absent, check the container launch line and any mount warnings; changing  PATH  cannot expose an unmounted executable. Confirm that  nvidia-smi  works on the same GPU node outside the container first. See the  Apptainer GPU documentation  for runtime details. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. Verify device visibility and compatibility with the framework you intend to use. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. \n The next section puts this launch sequence in a reusable  .bashrc  function so you only need to type  atlas_gpu  on the allocated node. \n 8. Save your shell configuration \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity, replacing  YOUR_PI  with your actual PI directory, including the  pi_  prefix (for example,  pi_rclsa_umass_edu ). If you already added  USER_PI  above, keep just one edited definition. These lines save the directory settings and define commands; they do not create directories, allocate resources, or start software at login.  The remaining chapters assume this configuration is in place.  The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n # Add to your existing ~/.bashrc on Unity; do not replace that file.\n# This defines shortcuts only. It does not start a container or allocate a job.\n# Replace YOUR_PI with pi_<PI's Unity username>, e.g. pi_rclsa_umass_edu.\nexport USER_PI=YOUR_PI\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Pixi and personal tools installed with pixi global (see the Using Pixi chapter).\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\n\n# Keep these path variables available even when Apptainer cleans the environment.\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER_PI=\"$USER_PI\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER=\"$USER\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\n\n# Install this optional function as described in Grid certificates and authentication.\n# Loading its definition does not create a proxy or prompt for a passphrase.\nif [[ -r \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" ]]; then\n    source \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\nfi\n\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n\n    # Run on an allocated NVIDIA GPU node. The subshell keeps these\n    # container options local to this invocation.\n    atlas_gpu() (\n        module load apptainer/latest || return \"$?\"\n        export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n        if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n            export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n        fi\n        if [[ ${CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER+x} ]]; then\n            export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=\"$CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER\"\n        fi\n        source \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" -c el9 \"$@\"\n    )\nfi \n The  PATH  line makes Pixi and its personal command-line tools available after you follow  Using Pixi . It is safe to include before installing Pixi. The optional source block enables  atlas_proxy  after you  install the grid-proxy helper ; it is also safe to include before installation. The function creates or renews a proxy only when you invoke it inside a configured EL9 session. \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . The GPU workflow is then: \n # Unity login node: request a GPU allocation.\nunity_gpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu GPU compute node:\n# choose your work directory, then start the EL9 container.\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\natlas_gpu \n ATLAS_WORKDIR  is derived from your saved  USER_PI  and Unity  $USER . If your allocated shell has not loaded the settings or function yet, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. The  APPTAINERENV_  exports preserve the directory variables when the container starts. \n unity_gpu  requests resources;  atlas_gpu  loads Apptainer and starts EL9 with  --nv  on the allocated node. The function preserves existing  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS  options, passes the current allocation's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES , and confines the exports to its subshell. Adding the definition to  .bashrc  does not start a container at login. You can pass extra ATLAS container arguments, for example  atlas_gpu -m /another/path  if a required path is not already visible. \n For CPU work, continue to use  unity_cpu , then the host preparation and  setupATLAS -c el9  commands above. \n Keep module loading and software startup inside explicitly invoked commands or functions, rather than executing them automatically when  .bashrc  is read. This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. Batch scripts and SSH launchers still initialize their own environment explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n 9. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n nvidia-smi: command not found  inside EL9 \n Confirm it works on the host, restart with  --nv  through  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS , and check launcher warnings. Test  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi  to distinguish a missing mount from a  PATH  issue. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation,  --nv  in the launch command, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage  and  benchmarking examples  using  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Next:   Grid certificates and authentication  prepares your identity for ATLAS grid services. Then  Submitting jobs with Slurm  recreates this environment automatically on a batch worker. \n"}, {"title": "1. Connect to Unity", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#connect", "text": "1. Connect to Unity \n From your own computer, connect using your Unity username: \n # Your computer — replace YOUR_USERNAME with your Unity username.\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n Follow Unity's  SSH guide  for account and SSH-key setup. Use the login node to prepare and submit work; run builds, event processing, and training on allocated compute nodes. \n"}, {"title": "2. Create your working directories once", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#directories", "text": "2. Create your working directories once \n On the  Unity login node , set  USER_PI  to your PI's existing Unity directory name. Unity uses the convention  pi_[PI's username] : the prefix  pi_  followed by the PI's Unity username, for example  pi_rclsa_umass_edu . Substitute that complete directory name, including  pi_ , for  YOUR_PI .  $USER  is already your own Unity username after logging in, so leave it as a variable. \n # Unity login node — replace YOUR_PI, for example with pi_rclsa_umass_edu.\nexport USER_PI=YOUR_PI\nmkdir -p \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}\"\nmkdir -p \"/project/${USER_PI}/${USER}\"\n\n# The examples throughout this manual use this analysis subdirectory.\nmkdir -p \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" \n Create these directories only once , not for every allocation or container.  mkdir -p  also succeeds if they already exist. Your PI's storage allocation must already be provisioned and accessible; these commands create your own subdirectories, not a new storage allocation. \n Add your edited  export USER_PI=...  line to your existing  ~/.bashrc  so the choice persists across sessions. The complete  shell configuration below  includes this line and defines  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" ; keep a single definition of each when merging it. Directory creation stays outside  .bashrc . \n ATLAS_WORKDIR  is your common analysis work area. Later chapters use  \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"  for an individual analysis checkout; replace  PROJECT_NAME  with that repository's directory name. \n Throughout the manual,  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is your personal work area and  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is your personal area in your PI's project allocation. Use  /work  or Unity's  scratch service  for active job I/O. Consult the  storage guide  for quotas and retention. \n"}, {"title": "3. Request compute resources", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#allocate", "text": "3. Request compute resources \n Choose  one  of the following commands on the login node. Adjust the resource request to your workload. \n CPU session \n # Unity login node\nsalloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n GPU session \n # Unity login node — alternative to the CPU request\nsalloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 \\\n       --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Option \n Meaning in these examples \n \n \n \n \n --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 \n One node and one task. \n \n \n --cpus-per-task=4 \n Four CPU cores for that task. Configure your application's threads accordingly. \n \n \n --mem=16G \n 16 GB of host memory for the node allocation; this is not GPU memory. \n \n \n --time=02:00:00 \n A two-hour wall-time limit. \n \n \n --gpus=1 \n One GPU, only in the GPU example. \n \n \n \n Unity's documented  salloc  workflow opens a shell on the compute node once resources are available. Confirm your location: \n # After the allocation is granted\nhostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\nNodes: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$SLURM_JOB_NODELIST\"\nuname -m \n The hostname should be a compute node in your allocation. If you are still on a login node with an active allocation, use  srun --pty bash -l  to start a shell on the allocated node. Do not run a second  salloc  inside the first one. \n This guide targets  x86_64  nodes. Containers do not translate CPU architectures; ARM and POWER partitions need compatible software and images.  gpu-preempt  is an alternative when interruption is acceptable. Consult the current  partition list  and  GPU guide  before choosing constraints or preemptible resources. \n"}, {"title": "4. Prepare the host environment", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#prepare-host", "text": "4. Prepare the host environment \n Run the following in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell , before entering a container: \n module load apptainer/latest\napptainer --version\n\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nalias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n\n# Forward the directory variables explicitly into Apptainer.\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER_PI=\"$USER_PI\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER=\"$USER\"\n\nls -l \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n The last command must find the setup script. Stop here if it fails: an empty  /cvmfs  directory alone does not establish that the ATLAS repository is available. Report the compute-node hostname and the missing path to Unity support. The first access to a CVMFS path may take longer while content is fetched. \n Start from a clean host shell. Deactivate any Conda or Python virtual environment and avoid loading Ubuntu ROOT, Python, or compiler modules for use inside the EL9 environment. Load the Apptainer runtime on the host; select analysis tools inside the container. \n Choose a work directory \n Use the analysis subdirectory you created in the one-time setup: \n # Ubuntu compute node\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\npwd -P \n Keep shell configuration in your home directory. Subsequent chapters assume the  shell configuration below  is installed, so they reuse  ATLAS_WORKDIR  and the allocation/container shortcuts. \n"}, {"title": "5. Enter the EL9 container", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#container", "text": "5. Enter the EL9 container \n For a CPU session, run: \n # Ubuntu compute node, in your analysis directory\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n -c el9  selects the ATLAS EL9 environment. Unity exposes  /work  inside the container without  -m /work , so no explicit mount is needed for the standard workflow. File permissions still apply. \n Use  -m  only when a directory you need is  not already visible  inside the container. For example, if  /scratch  is missing, leave the container and relaunch with  setupATLAS -c el9 -m /scratch . Check the paths you need after entering; automatic mounts can depend on the launcher and site configuration.  setupATLAS -c -h  describes additional mount options. \n The launcher initializes the ATLAS user interface in the container. Check it before selecting a release: \n # Inside the EL9 container\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\npwd\ntype lsetup\ntype asetup\nls /cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo\nls /work\nprintf 'PI: %s\\nUser: %s\\n' \"$USER_PI\" \"$USER\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\" \n Expect an EL9 distribution and  x86_64 . A container shares the host kernel, so  uname -r  is  not  a test of its Linux distribution. The launcher may initially map the working directory to  /srv  and use a different home path. The explicit  cd  returns to the shared analysis directory. The  APPTAINERENV_  exports above preserve  USER_PI ,  USER , and  ATLAS_WORKDIR  inside Apptainer; other host variables need not be preserved. If you need project files, also check that  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  is visible, using the mount guidance above if needed. \n If  lsetup  is unavailable despite a successful container launch, initialize the interface explicitly  inside  that container: \n export ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \n \n Two separate choices.  The container selects the operating-system environment.  asetup  selects an ATLAS software release. Entering EL9 does not by itself load AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena. \n \n"}, {"title": "6. Load analysis software", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#software", "text": "6. Load analysis software \n Choose the environment needed by your analysis. Use a fresh container session when switching between unrelated software stacks. \n A standalone ROOT check \n In an EL9 session with no analysis release loaded: \n lsetup root\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n This checks a basic ROOT/PyROOT environment. For reproducible work, select an explicit tool version using the choices shown by  lsetup \"root --help\" , and record it with your analysis. \n AnalysisBase, AthAnalysis, or Athena \n For an analysis release, begin in a  fresh EL9 session  and select the exact project and version required by your analysis, following the ATLAS  release setup guide . Throughout this manual,  ATLAS_RELEASE  means the complete  project,version  pair: for example,  AnalysisBase  followed by a comma and your exact release number. Enter that pair at the prompt: \n # Inside a fresh EL9 container, in your analysis directory\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Use  AthAnalysis  or  Athena  in place of  AnalysisBase  when that is what your workflow requires. Check the setup output for a successful release selection and an EL9-compatible platform. Use an exact version rather than  latest ; the analysis determines the supported release.  ATLAS_RELEASE  is a session variable, so set it again when starting a new session or submitting from a different shell. \n After a successful setup: \n printf 'Project: %s\\nVersion: %s\\nPlatform: %s\\n' \\\n       \"$AtlasProject\" \"$AtlasVersion\" \"$BINARY_TAG\"\ncommand -v python\nroot-config --version\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Use the ROOT and Python supplied by the release. Keep standalone  lsetup root  and release setup in separate sessions. Record the project, version, platform, and container information with your code; record the resolved image from the launcher output if you need to reproduce the precise container as well. \n"}, {"title": "7. Enable GPU access", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#gpu", "text": "7. Enable GPU access \n For an  NVIDIA GPU allocation , use this launch sequence in place of the CPU container command. First check the host: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node, after salloc and host preparation\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Pass  --nv  explicitly to the container runtime through  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS . This exposes the host's NVIDIA utilities and driver libraries inside EL9. The following sequence preserves any existing container options and forwards Slurm's device selection: \n # Ubuntu GPU compute node; module load apptainer/latest was done above.\n(\n    export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n    if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\n    if [[ ${CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=\"$CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER\"\n    fi\n    setupATLAS -c el9\n) \n The enclosing parentheses run the launch in a subshell. When you exit the container, these exports disappear and the host shell's previous container options are preserved. Set these options  before starting the container ; setting them inside an already running container cannot add the missing GPU mounts. \n Then check  inside  the container: \n command -v nvidia-smi\nnvidia-smi\nprintf 'CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=%s\\n' \"${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:-<unset>}\" \n Check the launcher's output for  apptainer ... --nv ... . This guide passes the option explicitly through the ATLAS launcher;  APPTAINER_NV=true  is Apptainer's environment-variable equivalent. \n If  nvidia-smi  is still missing, try  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi  inside the container. If the full path works but the bare command does not, inspect  PATH . If the file is absent, check the container launch line and any mount warnings; changing  PATH  cannot expose an unmounted executable. Confirm that  nvidia-smi  works on the same GPU node outside the container first. See the  Apptainer GPU documentation  for runtime details. \n GPU visibility does not install a CUDA-enabled analysis framework, or make ordinary ATLAS code use the GPU. Verify device visibility and compatibility with the framework you intend to use. Do not override Slurm's device selection or set it to  all ;  nvidia-smi  may enumerate more devices than your application is assigned. \n The next section puts this launch sequence in a reusable  .bashrc  function so you only need to type  atlas_gpu  on the allocated node. \n"}, {"title": "8. Save your shell configuration", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#bashrc", "text": "8. Save your shell configuration \n Add the following to  your existing   ~/.bashrc  on Unity, replacing  YOUR_PI  with your actual PI directory, including the  pi_  prefix (for example,  pi_rclsa_umass_edu ). If you already added  USER_PI  above, keep just one edited definition. These lines save the directory settings and define commands; they do not create directories, allocate resources, or start software at login.  The remaining chapters assume this configuration is in place.  The same snippet is available as  atlas-bashrc.sh . \n # Add to your existing ~/.bashrc on Unity; do not replace that file.\n# This defines shortcuts only. It does not start a container or allocate a job.\n# Replace YOUR_PI with pi_<PI's Unity username>, e.g. pi_rclsa_umass_edu.\nexport USER_PI=YOUR_PI\nexport ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\n\n# Pixi and personal tools installed with pixi global (see the Using Pixi chapter).\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\n\n# Keep these path variables available even when Apptainer cleans the environment.\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER_PI=\"$USER_PI\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_USER=\"$USER\"\nexport APPTAINERENV_ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\n\n# Install this optional function as described in Grid certificates and authentication.\n# Loading its definition does not create a proxy or prompt for a passphrase.\nif [[ -r \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" ]]; then\n    source \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\nfi\n\nif [[ $- == *i* ]]; then\n    alias setupATLAS='source ${ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE}/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh'\n    alias unity_cpu='salloc --partition=cpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n    alias unity_gpu='salloc --partition=gpu --nodes=1 --ntasks=1 --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00'\n\n    # Run on an allocated NVIDIA GPU node. The subshell keeps these\n    # container options local to this invocation.\n    atlas_gpu() (\n        module load apptainer/latest || return \"$?\"\n        export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n        if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n            export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n        fi\n        if [[ ${CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER+x} ]]; then\n            export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER=\"$CUDA_DEVICE_ORDER\"\n        fi\n        source \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" -c el9 \"$@\"\n    )\nfi \n The  PATH  line makes Pixi and its personal command-line tools available after you follow  Using Pixi . It is safe to include before installing Pixi. The optional source block enables  atlas_proxy  after you  install the grid-proxy helper ; it is also safe to include before installation. The function creates or renews a proxy only when you invoke it inside a configured EL9 session. \n Reload it in the  host shell  with  source ~/.bashrc . The GPU workflow is then: \n # Unity login node: request a GPU allocation.\nunity_gpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu GPU compute node:\n# choose your work directory, then start the EL9 container.\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\natlas_gpu \n ATLAS_WORKDIR  is derived from your saved  USER_PI  and Unity  $USER . If your allocated shell has not loaded the settings or function yet, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. The  APPTAINERENV_  exports preserve the directory variables when the container starts. \n unity_gpu  requests resources;  atlas_gpu  loads Apptainer and starts EL9 with  --nv  on the allocated node. The function preserves existing  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS  options, passes the current allocation's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES , and confines the exports to its subshell. Adding the definition to  .bashrc  does not start a container at login. You can pass extra ATLAS container arguments, for example  atlas_gpu -m /another/path  if a required path is not already visible. \n For CPU work, continue to use  unity_cpu , then the host preparation and  setupATLAS -c el9  commands above. \n Keep module loading and software startup inside explicitly invoked commands or functions, rather than executing them automatically when  .bashrc  is read. This avoids unexpected containers or incompatible software environments when starting new shells. Batch scripts and SSH launchers still initialize their own environment explicitly; non-interactive Bash does not normally expand aliases. \n"}, {"title": "9. End or reconnect to a session", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#sessions", "text": "9. End or reconnect to a session \n Run  exit  inside the container to return to the Ubuntu compute-node shell. Run  exit  again to leave the interactive allocation and release its resources. A new allocation requires a new container and software setup; files in your work directory remain. \n For a session that survives an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas   on the login node before  salloc . Detach with  Ctrl+B , then  D . Reconnect to the same login node and use  tmux attach -t atlas . Record that login hostname before starting.  tmux  does not extend the Slurm wall-time limit or prevent preemption. See Unity's  interactive-job guidance . \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n setupATLAS: command not found \n Define the export and alias in the current Bash shell, or source the setup script explicitly. \n \n \n Missing  atlasLocalSetup.sh \n Test its full CVMFS path on the allocated compute node; ask Unity support if unavailable. \n \n \n apptainer: command not found \n Load  apptainer/latest  on the host. Use  module avail apptainer  if the module name changes. \n \n \n Container fails with namespace or mount errors \n Save the launcher output and hostname for support; node/runtime configuration may need adjustment. \n \n \n Work files disappear inside the container \n Add the correct host path with  -m , including a symlink's real target; verify with  pwd -P  before launch. \n \n \n Container still reports Ubuntu \n Check  /etc/os-release ; the container may have failed to start, leaving you in the host shell. \n \n \n ROOT/Python errors after setup \n Try a fresh container, choose one software stack, and check the release's platform. \n \n \n nvidia-smi: command not found  inside EL9 \n Confirm it works on the host, restart with  --nv  through  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS , and check launcher warnings. Test  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi  to distinguish a missing mount from a  PATH  issue. \n \n \n GPU works on host but not in container \n Confirm an NVIDIA allocation,  --nv  in the launch command, and preservation of Slurm's device selection. \n \n \n Allocation is pending \n Inspect  squeue -u \"$USER\"  on the host; reduce an unnecessarily large request or consult current partition availability. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "setting-up-environment/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n ATLAS Software Documentation :  environment prerequisites  and  user-interface setup . \n Unity documentation :  interactive CLI jobs ,  container support ,  partitions , and  storage . \n US ATLAS Analysis Facilities: Apptainer usage  and  benchmarking examples  using  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS . \n Apptainer GPU support  and  environment-variable reference . \n \n Next:   Grid certificates and authentication  prepares your identity for ATLAS grid services. Then  Submitting jobs with Slurm  recreates this environment automatically on a batch worker. \n"}, {"title": "Grid certificates and authentication", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html", "text": "Use a personal grid certificate to establish your identity, register that identity with ATLAS, and create a short-lived VOMS proxy for command-line access. The proxy can then be used by Rucio, compatible storage clients, and the Rucio/AMI MCP servers described later in this guide. \n Obtaining and installing the certificate is a one-time setup that you repeat when renewing or replacing it. Creating a proxy is a session task. Complete  Setting up the environment  first; this chapter uses its  unity_cpu ,  setupATLAS , and  ATLAS_WORKDIR  settings. \n 1. Understand the credentials \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Item \n Purpose and lifetime \n \n \n \n \n Personal grid certificate \n Identifies you to grid services; issued by a trusted certificate authority and renewed before its expiry. \n \n \n Encrypted private key \n Proves that the certificate belongs to you. Its passphrase is needed when creating a proxy. \n \n \n ATLAS VO membership \n Associates your identity with the ATLAS virtual organization and its approved groups. Managed through ATLAS IAM. \n \n \n VOMS proxy \n A short-lived credential containing your delegated identity and ATLAS attributes. Clients read it from the path in  X509_USER_PROXY . \n \n \n Unity account \n Lets you log in and run jobs on Unity. It is separate from your ATLAS grid identity and Rucio account. \n \n \n \n A  .p12  or  .pfx  file is a PKCS#12 bundle containing the certificate and private key. This guide extracts that bundle into  usercert.pem  and an encrypted  userkey.pem , then creates the session proxy in a private directory under  /tmp . \n 2. Obtain a personal grid certificate \n If you already have a valid personal grid certificate and its private key, reuse them and continue to  ATLAS registration . You do not need a separate certificate for Unity. \n On  your own computer , using your browser: \n \n Open the  CERN Certification Authority  and sign in with your CERN account. \n Under  Grid User certificates , choose  New Grid User certificate  and follow the request process. This must be a personal grid-user certificate. \n Save the certificate and private key as a password-protected PKCS#12 bundle ( .p12  or  .pfx ). Keep the original downloaded bundle if the issuance workflow supplies one. \n Keep a protected backup and retain its password. You will need both to install the credential on Unity. \n \n CERN checks account and registration eligibility. Its  eligibility guidance  explains the requirements and alternatives if you cannot obtain a CERN certificate. A Unity account alone does not make you eligible. \n If your certificate is already installed in a browser, export or back up  the certificate together with its private key  as a password-protected PKCS#12 bundle. Exporting only a public  .cer  file is insufficient. CERN provides  browser import/export help  and  macOS instructions . \n 3. Register with ATLAS and link the certificate \n Certificate issuance and ATLAS membership are separate steps. Use the browser on your own computer for registration: \n \n Import the PKCS#12 bundle into your browser's personal-certificate store. In Firefox, open the certificate manager, choose  Your Certificates , and select  Import . Enter the bundle password when prompted. \n Open  ATLAS IAM  with that browser. Select your grid certificate if the browser asks. \n If you already have an ATLAS IAM account, sign in with  CERN SSO . Otherwise choose  Apply for an account , complete the registration and usage-policy steps, confirm the email request, and wait for approval. \n Check that your grid certificate is linked to your account. If  Link Certificate  is offered, select it and confirm the certificate identity. The link option requires the browser to have presented a valid, trusted certificate. \n \n Use your existing IAM account when adding a replacement certificate. Request additional groups only when needed for your work. The  ATLAS VO guide  and  US ATLAS registration instructions  provide further details. \n 4. Install the credentials on Unity \n Keep credentials in your private home directory. Do not put the bundle, private key, or proxy in your analysis repository or a shared project directory. \n For a  first installation , create the destination from your own computer. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  with your Unity username, and adjust the local bundle filename: \n # Your computer\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \\\n  'umask 077; mkdir -p \"$HOME/.globus\"; chmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"'\nscp \"$HOME/Downloads/grid-certificate.p12\" \\\n  YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org:.globus/usercred.p12 \n If you already have the working  usercert.pem  and  userkey.pem  pair on Unity, skip the bundle transfer and conversion. For a certificate replacement, preserve the existing pair until you have checked the replacement; the conversion commands below write those filenames. \n Enter the container \n Connect to Unity and use the environment chapter's allocation and container commands: \n # Your computer\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Check the credential directory  inside EL9 : \n printf 'Container home: %s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -ld \"$HOME/.globus\" \n The container may expose your home at a different path than the Ubuntu host. Use the paths visible inside the container throughout this chapter. If the files are missing, check the host path and the environment chapter's  mount guidance  before continuing. A  .globus  directory under the current working directory is not necessarily  $HOME/.globus . \n Convert a PKCS#12 bundle to PEM \n For the first installation, run these commands  inside EL9 . The private key remains encrypted: \n umask 077\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\"\n\nopenssl pkcs12 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\" \\\n  -clcerts -nokeys -out \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\"\n\nopenssl pkcs12 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\" \\\n  -nocerts -aes256 -out \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\n\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n OpenSSL asks for the bundle's import password. When extracting the key, it also asks you to choose and confirm a PEM passphrase. That PEM passphrase is what  voms-proxy-init  will request. Do not add  -nodes  or  -noenc , which would remove key encryption. \n Check that the files are readable and that the certificate has not expired: \n ls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\nopenssl x509 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n  -noout -subject -issuer -dates\nopenssl pkey -in \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" -check -noout \n The last command prompts for the PEM passphrase and checks the private key without printing it. These commands check the local files; the following VOMS request checks your grid credential and ATLAS membership. \n 5. Create a proxy for this session \n Inside a fresh EL9 session, initialize the ATLAS grid client environment: \n lsetup rucio\ncommand -v voms-proxy-init \n No analysis release or Pixi environment is needed for this step. The  Codex chapter  runs the same proxy commands through its isolated MCP launcher instead. \n Choose the proxy output path  before creating it . Use explicit paths for all three files: the certificate, private key, and output proxy. Run this once for a new session;  renew an existing proxy  at its current path when needed. \n umask 077\nif ATLAS_PROXY_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d /tmp/atlas-proxy.XXXXXXXX)\"; then\n    export ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\n    export X509_USER_PROXY=\"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR/x509up\"\n\n    voms-proxy-init \\\n        -voms atlas -valid 12:00 \\\n        -cert \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n        -key \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \\\n        -out \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft\nelse\n    echo 'Could not create a private proxy directory; stop here.' >&2\nfi \n Substitute your actual certificate/key filenames if needed. Enter the  PEM private-key passphrase  when prompted. Continue only after proxy creation succeeds and the remaining lifetime is positive. Inspect the identity, ATLAS attributes, and their expiry with: \n voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -all \n The requested 12 hours can be shortened by certificate expiry or server policy. Inspect both the proxy and VOMS-attribute lifetimes. The exported path remains available to commands started in this terminal. \n Do not set  X509_USER_PROXY  by running  voms-proxy-info -path  before a proxy exists.  If that lookup fails, assigning its output can export an empty string. The explicit  -out  recipe above avoids that failure. \n Recommended shortcut:  atlas_proxy \n For routine sessions, define a Bash function so that creating or renewing a proxy takes one command. This creates the  short-lived session proxy  from your installed certificate and encrypted key; obtaining or renewing the personal certificate itself still follows the earlier CA and registration steps. \n Install the  atlas-proxy.sh helper   once , on the Unity login node: \n mkdir -p \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas\"\ncurl --fail --location --output \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/atlas-proxy.sh \n Add these lines to your existing  .bashrc  if they are not already present. The environment chapter's updated  shell configuration  includes them: \n if [[ -r \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" ]]; then\n    source \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\nfi \n Sourcing this file only defines  atlas_proxy ; it does not contact VOMS or prompt at login. In a  fresh EL9 session , initialize the grid tools and invoke it explicitly: \n lsetup rucio\n# Also load the function if this container did not read your .bashrc.\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy \n It requests 12 hours by default. To request another lifetime, use  atlas_proxy 24:00 ; the server or certificate may shorten the request, so inspect the lifetimes printed by the helper. \n The helper uses  $HOME/.globus/usercert.pem  and  $HOME/.globus/userkey.pem  with explicit  -cert  and  -key  arguments. It creates a private session directory, supplies a nonempty  -out  path, checks the proxy and ATLAS attributes, and exports  X509_USER_PROXY  and  ATLAS_PROXY_DIR  in your current shell only after success. If your certificate/key filenames differ, adjust the two paths in your installed helper. \n Calling  atlas_proxy  again in the same session renews at the same path. The replacement is validated before it replaces the old file, so failed authentication does not discard an existing proxy. Enter the private-key passphrase at the terminal; it is not stored in  .bashrc . Run the function directly, not as  $(atlas_proxy)  or in a separate shell, because its exports must remain in the current shell. \n For Codex's isolated grid tools, the optional second argument selects its launcher:  atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" . The  Codex chapter  sets up that launcher. The helper does not run  lsetup  itself or change your analysis software environment. \n 6. Use the proxy with your tools \n For Rucio, set the account associated with your ATLAS identity and select proxy authentication: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nexport RUCIO_AUTH_TYPE=x509_proxy \n Your Rucio account may differ from Unity's  $USER . These exports select an existing account; they do not create or register it. Compatible XRootD clients use the same  X509_USER_PROXY  path. The Codex MCP launcher forwards that path to its clients, which have their own authentication configuration. \n Keep the proxy readable by the process that needs it. A new node or container may have a different  /tmp , and a new terminal does not automatically inherit your exports. Recreate the proxy in the session where you will use it if the existing file is unavailable. In a Slurm job, a proxy made on another node is not automatically accessible merely because its filename is exported.  Running on datasets with Slurm  shows how to publish a private shared copy and validate a separate proxy on each worker. \n Put the helper definition in  .bashrc , and invoke  atlas_proxy  when you need it. Do not save a temporary proxy path or invoke proxy creation automatically at login. Proxy creation establishes your identity and ATLAS attributes; an individual service still applies its account and resource permissions. \n 7. Renew and finish \n Renew a session proxy \n With the recommended helper, renew in the same container and terminal with: \n atlas_proxy \n Without the helper, use the explicit commands below. Renew at the same filename so tools already configured with that path can use it: \n if [[ -n ${X509_USER_PROXY:-} && -d ${ATLAS_PROXY_DIR:-} ]]; then\n    voms-proxy-init \\\n        -voms atlas -valid 12:00 \\\n        -cert \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n        -key \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \\\n        -out \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft\nelse\n    echo 'Create a new session proxy using the earlier recipe.' >&2\nfi \n Restart clients that retain an expired credential in memory. For Codex, follow its  renew/resume instructions . \n Renew the personal certificate \n Renew through your certificate authority before the personal certificate expires. Install the new certificate  and its matching private key , check their validity, and confirm the certificate identity is still linked to your existing ATLAS IAM account. A changed subject or issuer may need a new link. Then create a fresh proxy. Certificate renewal, VO membership renewal, and proxy renewal are separate operations. \n Finish the session \n After all clients using this proxy have finished: \n voms-proxy-destroy -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && rmdir \"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\" \\\n    && unset X509_USER_PROXY ATLAS_PROXY_DIR \n Keep the personal certificate and encrypted key for future sessions. Destroying this proxy removes the local copy; it does not revoke other credentials or tokens already issued. \n Troubleshooting \n No credentials found! \n Check  HOME , filenames, and readability  inside the container : \n printf 'HOME=%s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n Pass the paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key , as in the  creation recipe . If the files are visible and the error remains, inspect preceding messages about loading or decrypting the key. \n Error creating proxy certificate: : Invalid argument \n If VOMS reports a successful server contact followed by this error, check the output variable: \n printf 'X509_USER_PROXY=<%s>\\n' \"${X509_USER_PROXY-UNSET}\" \n <>  means the variable is set but empty;  <UNSET>  means it is absent. An empty exported value can make VOMS try to save to an invalid filename. Follow the  creation recipe  to choose a private directory and pass a nonempty  -out  path. Do not derive the path from a failed  voms-proxy-info  invocation. \n If it still fails with a valid, writable output path, add  -debug  to the  voms-proxy-init  command and inspect the final exception. The brief error alone is not enough to diagnose every possible failure. \n Other common problems \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n atlas_proxy: command not found \n Install the helper once, then source  $HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh  inside this container. The  .bashrc  source block defines it for shells that load that configuration. \n \n \n ATLAS_PROXY_DIR is not a private owned session directory \n Do not reuse that directory. Run  unset ATLAS_PROXY_DIR , then invoke  atlas_proxy  to create a new private directory; the old file is not removed. \n \n \n OpenSSL cannot read an older PKCS#12 bundle and reports an unsupported algorithm \n Add  -legacy  to the affected  openssl pkcs12  extraction command. Keep encryption on the resulting PEM key. \n \n \n Cannot decrypt the private key \n Use the PEM passphrase chosen during conversion, which may differ from the bundle password. Check the file with  openssl pkey -check -noout . \n \n \n Browser does not offer the certificate or IAM does not show a link option \n Import the personal certificate and its key, check expiry, and ensure the browser presents the intended certificate. Consult CERN's browser help if CA trust needs configuring. \n \n \n VOMS rejects the identity or ATLAS attributes \n Check certificate validity, approved VO membership, and the certificate link in ATLAS IAM. Newly approved changes can take time to propagate. \n \n \n Proxy has disappeared or expired \n Check its explicit filename and lifetime in the current container; recreate it on a new node or renew it at the existing path. \n \n \n Rucio authenticates to an unexpected account \n Check  RUCIO_ACCOUNT ; it is the ATLAS Rucio account, not automatically Unity  $USER . \n \n \n \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the command, error, software version, and compute-node hostname. Omit private keys, proxy contents, passwords, and tokens. \n References \n \n CERN:  certificate authority ,  grid-user eligibility , and  browser help . \n ATLAS:  obtaining a certificate ,  VO membership , and  ATLAS IAM . \n US ATLAS:  joining the ATLAS VO . \n INDIGO IAM:  linking an X.509 certificate . \n OpenSSL:  PKCS#12 conversion . \n VOMS:  client guide  and  proxy-init manual . \n Rucio:  client authentication configuration . \n \n Next:   Submitting jobs with Slurm  runs ATLAS software on batch workers.  Codex Agents  uses your personal grid proxy for Rucio and AMI access. \n"}, {"title": "1. Understand the credentials", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#credentials", "text": "1. Understand the credentials \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Item \n Purpose and lifetime \n \n \n \n \n Personal grid certificate \n Identifies you to grid services; issued by a trusted certificate authority and renewed before its expiry. \n \n \n Encrypted private key \n Proves that the certificate belongs to you. Its passphrase is needed when creating a proxy. \n \n \n ATLAS VO membership \n Associates your identity with the ATLAS virtual organization and its approved groups. Managed through ATLAS IAM. \n \n \n VOMS proxy \n A short-lived credential containing your delegated identity and ATLAS attributes. Clients read it from the path in  X509_USER_PROXY . \n \n \n Unity account \n Lets you log in and run jobs on Unity. It is separate from your ATLAS grid identity and Rucio account. \n \n \n \n A  .p12  or  .pfx  file is a PKCS#12 bundle containing the certificate and private key. This guide extracts that bundle into  usercert.pem  and an encrypted  userkey.pem , then creates the session proxy in a private directory under  /tmp . \n"}, {"title": "2. Obtain a personal grid certificate", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#obtain", "text": "2. Obtain a personal grid certificate \n If you already have a valid personal grid certificate and its private key, reuse them and continue to  ATLAS registration . You do not need a separate certificate for Unity. \n On  your own computer , using your browser: \n \n Open the  CERN Certification Authority  and sign in with your CERN account. \n Under  Grid User certificates , choose  New Grid User certificate  and follow the request process. This must be a personal grid-user certificate. \n Save the certificate and private key as a password-protected PKCS#12 bundle ( .p12  or  .pfx ). Keep the original downloaded bundle if the issuance workflow supplies one. \n Keep a protected backup and retain its password. You will need both to install the credential on Unity. \n \n CERN checks account and registration eligibility. Its  eligibility guidance  explains the requirements and alternatives if you cannot obtain a CERN certificate. A Unity account alone does not make you eligible. \n If your certificate is already installed in a browser, export or back up  the certificate together with its private key  as a password-protected PKCS#12 bundle. Exporting only a public  .cer  file is insufficient. CERN provides  browser import/export help  and  macOS instructions . \n"}, {"title": "3. Register with ATLAS and link the certificate", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#register", "text": "3. Register with ATLAS and link the certificate \n Certificate issuance and ATLAS membership are separate steps. Use the browser on your own computer for registration: \n \n Import the PKCS#12 bundle into your browser's personal-certificate store. In Firefox, open the certificate manager, choose  Your Certificates , and select  Import . Enter the bundle password when prompted. \n Open  ATLAS IAM  with that browser. Select your grid certificate if the browser asks. \n If you already have an ATLAS IAM account, sign in with  CERN SSO . Otherwise choose  Apply for an account , complete the registration and usage-policy steps, confirm the email request, and wait for approval. \n Check that your grid certificate is linked to your account. If  Link Certificate  is offered, select it and confirm the certificate identity. The link option requires the browser to have presented a valid, trusted certificate. \n \n Use your existing IAM account when adding a replacement certificate. Request additional groups only when needed for your work. The  ATLAS VO guide  and  US ATLAS registration instructions  provide further details. \n"}, {"title": "4. Install the credentials on Unity", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#install", "text": "4. Install the credentials on Unity \n Keep credentials in your private home directory. Do not put the bundle, private key, or proxy in your analysis repository or a shared project directory. \n For a  first installation , create the destination from your own computer. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  with your Unity username, and adjust the local bundle filename: \n # Your computer\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \\\n  'umask 077; mkdir -p \"$HOME/.globus\"; chmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"'\nscp \"$HOME/Downloads/grid-certificate.p12\" \\\n  YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org:.globus/usercred.p12 \n If you already have the working  usercert.pem  and  userkey.pem  pair on Unity, skip the bundle transfer and conversion. For a certificate replacement, preserve the existing pair until you have checked the replacement; the conversion commands below write those filenames. \n Enter the container \n Connect to Unity and use the environment chapter's allocation and container commands: \n # Your computer\nssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.unityhpc.org \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Check the credential directory  inside EL9 : \n printf 'Container home: %s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -ld \"$HOME/.globus\" \n The container may expose your home at a different path than the Ubuntu host. Use the paths visible inside the container throughout this chapter. If the files are missing, check the host path and the environment chapter's  mount guidance  before continuing. A  .globus  directory under the current working directory is not necessarily  $HOME/.globus . \n Convert a PKCS#12 bundle to PEM \n For the first installation, run these commands  inside EL9 . The private key remains encrypted: \n umask 077\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\"\n\nopenssl pkcs12 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\" \\\n  -clcerts -nokeys -out \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\"\n\nopenssl pkcs12 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercred.p12\" \\\n  -nocerts -aes256 -out \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\n\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n OpenSSL asks for the bundle's import password. When extracting the key, it also asks you to choose and confirm a PEM passphrase. That PEM passphrase is what  voms-proxy-init  will request. Do not add  -nodes  or  -noenc , which would remove key encryption. \n Check that the files are readable and that the certificate has not expired: \n ls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\nopenssl x509 -in \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n  -noout -subject -issuer -dates\nopenssl pkey -in \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" -check -noout \n The last command prompts for the PEM passphrase and checks the private key without printing it. These commands check the local files; the following VOMS request checks your grid credential and ATLAS membership. \n"}, {"title": "5. Create a proxy for this session", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#create-proxy", "text": "5. Create a proxy for this session \n Inside a fresh EL9 session, initialize the ATLAS grid client environment: \n lsetup rucio\ncommand -v voms-proxy-init \n No analysis release or Pixi environment is needed for this step. The  Codex chapter  runs the same proxy commands through its isolated MCP launcher instead. \n Choose the proxy output path  before creating it . Use explicit paths for all three files: the certificate, private key, and output proxy. Run this once for a new session;  renew an existing proxy  at its current path when needed. \n umask 077\nif ATLAS_PROXY_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d /tmp/atlas-proxy.XXXXXXXX)\"; then\n    export ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\n    export X509_USER_PROXY=\"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR/x509up\"\n\n    voms-proxy-init \\\n        -voms atlas -valid 12:00 \\\n        -cert \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n        -key \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \\\n        -out \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft\nelse\n    echo 'Could not create a private proxy directory; stop here.' >&2\nfi \n Substitute your actual certificate/key filenames if needed. Enter the  PEM private-key passphrase  when prompted. Continue only after proxy creation succeeds and the remaining lifetime is positive. Inspect the identity, ATLAS attributes, and their expiry with: \n voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -all \n The requested 12 hours can be shortened by certificate expiry or server policy. Inspect both the proxy and VOMS-attribute lifetimes. The exported path remains available to commands started in this terminal. \n Do not set  X509_USER_PROXY  by running  voms-proxy-info -path  before a proxy exists.  If that lookup fails, assigning its output can export an empty string. The explicit  -out  recipe above avoids that failure. \n Recommended shortcut:  atlas_proxy \n For routine sessions, define a Bash function so that creating or renewing a proxy takes one command. This creates the  short-lived session proxy  from your installed certificate and encrypted key; obtaining or renewing the personal certificate itself still follows the earlier CA and registration steps. \n Install the  atlas-proxy.sh helper   once , on the Unity login node: \n mkdir -p \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas\"\ncurl --fail --location --output \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/atlas-proxy.sh \n Add these lines to your existing  .bashrc  if they are not already present. The environment chapter's updated  shell configuration  includes them: \n if [[ -r \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" ]]; then\n    source \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\nfi \n Sourcing this file only defines  atlas_proxy ; it does not contact VOMS or prompt at login. In a  fresh EL9 session , initialize the grid tools and invoke it explicitly: \n lsetup rucio\n# Also load the function if this container did not read your .bashrc.\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy \n It requests 12 hours by default. To request another lifetime, use  atlas_proxy 24:00 ; the server or certificate may shorten the request, so inspect the lifetimes printed by the helper. \n The helper uses  $HOME/.globus/usercert.pem  and  $HOME/.globus/userkey.pem  with explicit  -cert  and  -key  arguments. It creates a private session directory, supplies a nonempty  -out  path, checks the proxy and ATLAS attributes, and exports  X509_USER_PROXY  and  ATLAS_PROXY_DIR  in your current shell only after success. If your certificate/key filenames differ, adjust the two paths in your installed helper. \n Calling  atlas_proxy  again in the same session renews at the same path. The replacement is validated before it replaces the old file, so failed authentication does not discard an existing proxy. Enter the private-key passphrase at the terminal; it is not stored in  .bashrc . Run the function directly, not as  $(atlas_proxy)  or in a separate shell, because its exports must remain in the current shell. \n For Codex's isolated grid tools, the optional second argument selects its launcher:  atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" . The  Codex chapter  sets up that launcher. The helper does not run  lsetup  itself or change your analysis software environment. \n"}, {"title": "6. Use the proxy with your tools", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#use-proxy", "text": "6. Use the proxy with your tools \n For Rucio, set the account associated with your ATLAS identity and select proxy authentication: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nexport RUCIO_AUTH_TYPE=x509_proxy \n Your Rucio account may differ from Unity's  $USER . These exports select an existing account; they do not create or register it. Compatible XRootD clients use the same  X509_USER_PROXY  path. The Codex MCP launcher forwards that path to its clients, which have their own authentication configuration. \n Keep the proxy readable by the process that needs it. A new node or container may have a different  /tmp , and a new terminal does not automatically inherit your exports. Recreate the proxy in the session where you will use it if the existing file is unavailable. In a Slurm job, a proxy made on another node is not automatically accessible merely because its filename is exported.  Running on datasets with Slurm  shows how to publish a private shared copy and validate a separate proxy on each worker. \n Put the helper definition in  .bashrc , and invoke  atlas_proxy  when you need it. Do not save a temporary proxy path or invoke proxy creation automatically at login. Proxy creation establishes your identity and ATLAS attributes; an individual service still applies its account and resource permissions. \n"}, {"title": "7. Renew and finish", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#renew", "text": "7. Renew and finish \n Renew a session proxy \n With the recommended helper, renew in the same container and terminal with: \n atlas_proxy \n Without the helper, use the explicit commands below. Renew at the same filename so tools already configured with that path can use it: \n if [[ -n ${X509_USER_PROXY:-} && -d ${ATLAS_PROXY_DIR:-} ]]; then\n    voms-proxy-init \\\n        -voms atlas -valid 12:00 \\\n        -cert \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \\\n        -key \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \\\n        -out \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft\nelse\n    echo 'Create a new session proxy using the earlier recipe.' >&2\nfi \n Restart clients that retain an expired credential in memory. For Codex, follow its  renew/resume instructions . \n Renew the personal certificate \n Renew through your certificate authority before the personal certificate expires. Install the new certificate  and its matching private key , check their validity, and confirm the certificate identity is still linked to your existing ATLAS IAM account. A changed subject or issuer may need a new link. Then create a fresh proxy. Certificate renewal, VO membership renewal, and proxy renewal are separate operations. \n Finish the session \n After all clients using this proxy have finished: \n voms-proxy-destroy -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \\\n    && rmdir \"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\" \\\n    && unset X509_USER_PROXY ATLAS_PROXY_DIR \n Keep the personal certificate and encrypted key for future sessions. Destroying this proxy removes the local copy; it does not revoke other credentials or tokens already issued. \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n No credentials found! \n Check  HOME , filenames, and readability  inside the container : \n printf 'HOME=%s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n Pass the paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key , as in the  creation recipe . If the files are visible and the error remains, inspect preceding messages about loading or decrypting the key. \n Error creating proxy certificate: : Invalid argument \n If VOMS reports a successful server contact followed by this error, check the output variable: \n printf 'X509_USER_PROXY=<%s>\\n' \"${X509_USER_PROXY-UNSET}\" \n <>  means the variable is set but empty;  <UNSET>  means it is absent. An empty exported value can make VOMS try to save to an invalid filename. Follow the  creation recipe  to choose a private directory and pass a nonempty  -out  path. Do not derive the path from a failed  voms-proxy-info  invocation. \n If it still fails with a valid, writable output path, add  -debug  to the  voms-proxy-init  command and inspect the final exception. The brief error alone is not enough to diagnose every possible failure. \n Other common problems \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n atlas_proxy: command not found \n Install the helper once, then source  $HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh  inside this container. The  .bashrc  source block defines it for shells that load that configuration. \n \n \n ATLAS_PROXY_DIR is not a private owned session directory \n Do not reuse that directory. Run  unset ATLAS_PROXY_DIR , then invoke  atlas_proxy  to create a new private directory; the old file is not removed. \n \n \n OpenSSL cannot read an older PKCS#12 bundle and reports an unsupported algorithm \n Add  -legacy  to the affected  openssl pkcs12  extraction command. Keep encryption on the resulting PEM key. \n \n \n Cannot decrypt the private key \n Use the PEM passphrase chosen during conversion, which may differ from the bundle password. Check the file with  openssl pkey -check -noout . \n \n \n Browser does not offer the certificate or IAM does not show a link option \n Import the personal certificate and its key, check expiry, and ensure the browser presents the intended certificate. Consult CERN's browser help if CA trust needs configuring. \n \n \n VOMS rejects the identity or ATLAS attributes \n Check certificate validity, approved VO membership, and the certificate link in ATLAS IAM. Newly approved changes can take time to propagate. \n \n \n Proxy has disappeared or expired \n Check its explicit filename and lifetime in the current container; recreate it on a new node or renew it at the existing path. \n \n \n Rucio authenticates to an unexpected account \n Check  RUCIO_ACCOUNT ; it is the ATLAS Rucio account, not automatically Unity  $USER . \n \n \n \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the command, error, software version, and compute-node hostname. Omit private keys, proxy contents, passwords, and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "grid-certificates/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n CERN:  certificate authority ,  grid-user eligibility , and  browser help . \n ATLAS:  obtaining a certificate ,  VO membership , and  ATLAS IAM . \n US ATLAS:  joining the ATLAS VO . \n INDIGO IAM:  linking an X.509 certificate . \n OpenSSL:  PKCS#12 conversion . \n VOMS:  client guide  and  proxy-init manual . \n Rucio:  client authentication configuration . \n \n Next:   Submitting jobs with Slurm  runs ATLAS software on batch workers.  Codex Agents  uses your personal grid proxy for Rucio and AMI access. \n"}, {"title": "Submitting jobs with Slurm", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html", "text": "A batch job needs the same environment as an interactive session. Slurm starts the job on an allocated  Ubuntu compute node ; the job loads Apptainer, enters  EL9 , selects an ATLAS release, and runs the analysis there. \n You submit with  sbatch  from a Unity login node. There is no preceding  salloc , and you can disconnect after submission. The batch allocation and its wall-time limit are managed by Slurm. \n \n Prerequisite:  first complete  Setting up the environment , including the one-time directories and  .bashrc  settings , and choose an exact ATLAS release that works for your analysis. This chapter uses your saved  USER_PI , Unity  $USER , and  ATLAS_WORKDIR . \n \n 1. Separate scheduling from the analysis \n The example uses two files: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n File \n Where it runs \n What it does \n \n \n \n \n atlas-job.sbatch \n Ubuntu worker node \n Requests resources through  #SBATCH  directives, loads the container runtime, and starts the EL9 command. \n \n \n run-atlas.sh \n Inside the EL9 container \n Initializes ATLAS, selects the requested release, and runs a small PyROOT test. \n \n \n \n The key ATLAS option is  -r , which runs a command inside the container without opening an interactive prompt. The wrapper invokes the setup script directly: \n # Pattern used in the batch script; the variables are defined there.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\" \n This is the scripted equivalent of  setupATLAS -c el9 , followed by commands inside the container. Simply writing  setupATLAS -c el9  on one line and your analysis on the next does not put that next line inside the container. Keep the analysis in the command passed to  -r . \n The  US ATLAS benchmarking examples  use this non-interactive interface. Our wrapper adapts it to Unity's module and Slurm configuration. \n 2. Prepare a shared job directory \n Start from a  fresh Ubuntu login shell , outside an interactive ATLAS container. Your  .bashrc  already defines  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" ; create a job subdirectory there: \n # Unity login node\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\nmkdir -p logs\n\ncurl --fail --location --output atlas-job.sbatch \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/atlas-job.sbatch\ncurl --fail --location --output run-atlas.sh \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/run-atlas.sh \n You can also save the files directly:  atlas-job.sbatch  and  run-atlas.sh . Both belong in the directory from which you will submit. No executable permission is required here: Slurm reads the submission script, and the container runs the payload with  bash . \n Create  logs/  before submitting.  Slurm opens the output files before the script runs, so creating that directory inside the job would be too late. \n Slurm transfers the submission script, but it does  not  transfer your payload, analysis code, input files, or software build. They must remain accessible on shared storage. Avoid editing  run-atlas.sh  or its dependencies while jobs using them are queued or running; keep a separate copy for a new version. \n 3. The worker-node script \n atlas-job.sbatch  requests one CPU, 4 GB of memory, and 15 minutes for the small test. Its login Bash shell initializes the host's module environment. It creates a separate  runs/JOB_ID/  directory for every submission. \n #!/bin/bash -l\n#SBATCH --job-name=atlas-test\n#SBATCH --partition=cpu\n#SBATCH --nodes=1\n#SBATCH --ntasks=1\n#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1\n#SBATCH --mem=4G\n#SBATCH --time=00:15:00\n#SBATCH --output=logs/%x-%j.out\n#SBATCH --error=logs/%x-%j.err\n#SBATCH --export=NONE\n\n# This script runs on the allocated Ubuntu worker node.\nif [[ $# -ne 2 || $1 != *,* || -z $2 ]]; then\n    echo 'Usage: sbatch atlas-job.sbatch AnalysisBase,VERSION \"$USER_PI\"' >&2\n    exit 2\nfi\nunity_atlas_release=$1\nunity_atlas_pi=$2\nunity_atlas_user=${SLURM_JOB_USER:?Missing Slurm username}\ncd \"${SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR:?Submit from your shared job directory}\" || exit 1\nunity_atlas_payload=\"$PWD/run-atlas.sh\"\nif [[ ! -r \"$unity_atlas_payload\" ]]; then\n    echo \"Cannot read payload: $unity_atlas_payload\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\nunity_atlas_run_dir=\"$PWD/runs/${SLURM_JOB_ID:?Missing Slurm job ID}\"\nmkdir -p \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# Load the container runtime on the host, before entering EL9.\nmodule load apptainer/latest || exit \"$?\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nif [[ ! -r \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" ]]; then\n    echo \"ATLASLocalRootBase is unavailable on $(hostname)\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\n# Enable NVIDIA passthrough only when this job has allocated GPUs.\nif [[ -n ${SLURM_JOB_GPUS:-} ]]; then\n    export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n    if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\nfi\n\n# Pass arguments explicitly; do not depend on host variables surviving\n# the container's environment filtering. %q quotes each Bash argument.\nprintf -v unity_atlas_command 'bash %q %q %q %q %q %q' \\\n    \"$unity_atlas_payload\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" \\\n    \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\" \"$unity_atlas_pi\" \"$unity_atlas_user\"\nprintf 'Job %s on %s; release %s; output %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$(hostname)\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\"\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# -r runs the payload inside the container and then leaves the container.\n# Unity exposes /work inside the container; no -m is needed for it.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nprintf 'Container command exited with status %s\\n' \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nexit \"$unity_atlas_status\" \n The wrapper sources ATLASLocalRootBase explicitly instead of depending on the interactive  setupATLAS  alias in  .bashrc . The  --export=NONE  directive avoids importing the submission shell's full environment; Slurm still supplies its job variables and may initialize the user's login environment. This is not a completely empty environment. The submission commands pass your saved  USER_PI  as an argument; the wrapper obtains your username from  SLURM_JOB_USER  and passes both into the payload. This keeps  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  and  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  usable in scripts without relying on  .bashrc  being read there. Keep software and container startup out of your shell startup files, as described in the  shell-configuration section . \n Unity exposes  /work  inside the container, so the standard command needs no  -m /work . For another filesystem that is missing inside EL9, use the  mount guidance in the environment chapter  and add the corresponding  -m /actual/path  to the container launch. \n This is a  single-node, single-process  job. Slurm already runs the batch script on the worker, so it does not need an additional  srun  for this example. MPI and multiple simultaneous tasks require a different launch pattern. If you later introduce  srun , note that this script uses  --export=NONE : use  srun --export=ALL ...  when the step needs the environment you have prepared. \n 4. The script inside EL9 \n run-atlas.sh  starts a fresh Bash process inside the container. It initializes the ATLAS command definitions, runs  asetup  for the release passed at submission, and creates  hist.root  without requiring any input data or credentials. \n #!/bin/bash\n# This script runs inside the EL9 container.\nunity_atlas_release=${1:?Missing ATLAS project and version}\nunity_atlas_run_dir=${2:?Missing output directory}\nunity_atlas_cpus=${3:-1}\n# These were passed explicitly, not inherited from an interactive .bashrc.\nexport USER_PI=${4:?Missing Unity PI directory}\nexport USER=${5:?Missing Unity username}\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n# Do not let a sourced setup script interpret the payload's arguments.\nset --\n\n# A new Bash process needs its own ATLAS command definitions.\n# ALRB defines aliases such as asetup; Bash scripts must enable them.\nshopt -s expand_aliases\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\nasetup \"$unity_atlas_release\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\n\n# Enable strict error handling after the external setup scripts finish.\nset -euo pipefail\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=\"$unity_atlas_cpus\"\nprintf 'Container OS:\\n'\ncat /etc/os-release\nprintf '\\nWorking directory: %s\\n' \"$PWD\"\n\n# Replace this smoke test with your analysis command.\npython -u - <<'PY'\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nROOT.gRandom.SetSeed(12345)\noutput = ROOT.TFile.Open(\"hist.root\", \"RECREATE\")\nif not output or output.IsZombie():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Cannot create hist.root\")\nhist = ROOT.TH1D(\"example\", \"ATLAS on Unity; x; Entries\", 50, -5, 5)\nhist.FillRandom(\"gaus\", 10000)\nhist.Write()\noutput.Close()\nprint(\"ROOT version:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"Wrote hist.root with 10000 entries\")\nPY\ntest -s hist.root\nprintf 'Payload finished successfully.\\n' \n shopt -s expand_aliases  lets this non-interactive Bash script use the aliases defined by ATLASLocalRootBase, including  asetup . The setup and  asetup  commands are on separate lines so those definitions are available before the release command is parsed. \n External setup scripts run before strict Bash error handling is enabled; their return codes are checked explicitly. Once setup finishes,  set -euo pipefail  makes a failed analysis command stop the payload. The wrapper returns the container command's status to Slurm. When adapting the example, keep this error handling: a final successful  echo  must not hide a failed analysis. \n 5. Submit and inspect the result \n Use the  same project and exact release version  selected in the  environment chapter , after confirming it works for your analysis.  ATLAS_RELEASE  has the same  project,version  format here. Do not enter a literal placeholder or a moving  latest  version. The prompt below sets it in your login shell; the batch job itself never prompts for input. \n # Still in the directory containing both scripts and logs/.\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\"); then\n        JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n        printf 'Submitted job: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\n    fi\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Record the returned job ID. In a later login session, set  JOB_ID  to that number before using these commands: \n # Queued/running jobs; a completed job will disappear from squeue.\nsqueue --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\"\n\n# Accounting, including the batch step; may take a short time to update.\nsacct --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\" \\\n      --format=JobID,JobName,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\n\n# These files appear once the job starts.\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.err\"\nls -lh \"runs/${JOB_ID}/hist.root\" \n For a successful smoke test, check  all three : \n \n Slurm reports  COMPLETED  with  ExitCode  equal to  0:0 . \n The output log identifies EL9, reports the ROOT version, and ends with the successful payload message and container status zero. \n runs/JOB_ID/hist.root  exists and is nonempty. In an interactive session with the same release, you can open it and inspect the histogram  example , which should have 10,000 entries. \n \n MaxRSS  is generally reported for a step such as  JOB_ID.batch ; keep those rows in the accounting output. Check both logs when a job fails. For Slurm, the first number in  ExitCode  is the return code and the second is the terminating signal. \n To cancel a queued or running job  when you want to stop it : \n scancel \"$JOB_ID\" \n 6. Replace the test with your analysis \n Replace the Python block and the  test -s hist.root  check in  run-atlas.sh  with your analysis command and an appropriate output check. Keep the release setup and run-directory change. Relative output paths then land in  runs/JOB_ID/ ; use absolute paths for shared inputs and code. \n For example, use the analysis checkout under your work area and choose an input file from your  /project  allocation. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with your repository's directory name and use its actual script/input paths.  USER_PI  and  USER  are set by the supplied payload from the arguments passed by the worker script: \n # Inside run-atlas.sh, after asetup and set -euo pipefail.\npython -u \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas/PROJECT_NAME/run.py\" \\\n    --input \"/project/${USER_PI}/${USER}/input.root\" \\\n    --output result.root\ntest -s result.root \n If your analysis uses a compiled ATLAS package, build it in an EL9 allocation with the same release and platform, then source that build's exact  setup.sh  after  asetup  in the payload. Keep the build accessible on shared storage. Do not build the same directory concurrently from multiple jobs. \n Remote data access and authentication need their own setup; having software on CVMFS does not grant access to protected data. Continue with  Running on datasets with Slurm  for Rucio replication to NET2, worker credentials, XRootD inputs, and retryable arrays. \n Adjust CPU, memory, and time \n Command-line  sbatch  options override the corresponding  #SBATCH  directives. For example, once your analysis is configured to use four CPUs: \n sbatch --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \\\n    atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\" \n Requesting four CPUs does not automatically parallelize Python or ROOT. The example passes the CPU allocation into the container and sets  OMP_NUM_THREADS ; configure your application's own thread or worker count as needed. Keep that count within the allocation.  --mem  here is memory per node, not per CPU. \n Keep directives at the top of the file, before shell commands. Slurm reads them before executing Bash, so  #SBATCH --output=$HOME/...  does not expand  $HOME . Use a literal path, a Slurm filename token such as  %j  (job ID), or an option on the  sbatch  command line. \n Request an NVIDIA GPU \n The same wrapper detects an allocated GPU through  SLURM_JOB_GPUS , appends  --nv  to  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS , and forwards Slurm's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES  selection when present. This uses the same passthrough option as the  interactive GPU recipe . The batch script sets it itself;  atlas_gpu  remains the shortcut for interactive shells. Request the GPU at submission: \n sbatch --partition=gpu --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 \\\n    --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\" \n Add  nvidia-smi  after setup inside  run-atlas.sh  to inspect device visibility, then use a CUDA-capable application and compatible software environment. The provided histogram test runs on the CPU even when a GPU is allocated; it does not validate a GPU framework. Do not replace Slurm's device selection with a hard-coded GPU number. \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n No log files yet \n A pending job has not started. Check  squeue ; also confirm  logs/  existed before submission and is writable. \n \n \n module: command not found \n Keep the login-shell shebang  #!/bin/bash -l ; check host shell initialization and Unity's module instructions. \n \n \n setupATLAS  or  asetup  is not found \n Use the supplied explicit setup and alias-expansion sequence; an interactive  .bashrc  alias is not enough for batch scripts. \n \n \n Script or input missing inside the container \n Submit from the shared job directory, keep the payload there, and verify its absolute path inside EL9. Add  -m  only for paths not already visible. \n \n \n Software setup fails \n Confirm the exact project, release, and platform worked in a fresh interactive EL9 session. Check compute-node CVMFS access. \n \n \n FAILED ,  OUT_OF_MEMORY , or  TIMEOUT \n Read both logs and the  sacct  state; fix the command or adjust the measured memory/time requirement. \n \n \n COMPLETED  but output is missing \n Check that the payload actually ran, did not hide a failed command, and wrote to the intended run directory. Keep explicit output checks. \n \n \n GPU unavailable inside the payload \n Check the GPU resource request,  --nv  in the container launch line, Slurm's device selection, and the application's CUDA support. If  nvidia-smi  is missing, inspect startup mount warnings. \n \n \n \n References \n \n Unity: batch jobs with sbatch  and  partitions . \n US ATLAS analysis-facility benchmarking examples , including ATLASLocalRootBase container commands and batch wrappers. \n ATLAS Software Documentation . \n Slurm sbatch ,  sacct , and  job exit codes . \n Apptainer NVIDIA GPU support . \n \n Next:   Running on datasets with Slurm  extends this setup to a complete ATLAS dataset at NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK. \n"}, {"title": "1. Separate scheduling from the analysis", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#workflow", "text": "1. Separate scheduling from the analysis \n The example uses two files: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n File \n Where it runs \n What it does \n \n \n \n \n atlas-job.sbatch \n Ubuntu worker node \n Requests resources through  #SBATCH  directives, loads the container runtime, and starts the EL9 command. \n \n \n run-atlas.sh \n Inside the EL9 container \n Initializes ATLAS, selects the requested release, and runs a small PyROOT test. \n \n \n \n The key ATLAS option is  -r , which runs a command inside the container without opening an interactive prompt. The wrapper invokes the setup script directly: \n # Pattern used in the batch script; the variables are defined there.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\" \n This is the scripted equivalent of  setupATLAS -c el9 , followed by commands inside the container. Simply writing  setupATLAS -c el9  on one line and your analysis on the next does not put that next line inside the container. Keep the analysis in the command passed to  -r . \n The  US ATLAS benchmarking examples  use this non-interactive interface. Our wrapper adapts it to Unity's module and Slurm configuration. \n"}, {"title": "2. Prepare a shared job directory", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#prepare", "text": "2. Prepare a shared job directory \n Start from a  fresh Ubuntu login shell , outside an interactive ATLAS container. Your  .bashrc  already defines  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" ; create a job subdirectory there: \n # Unity login node\nmkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/atlas-test\"\nmkdir -p logs\n\ncurl --fail --location --output atlas-job.sbatch \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/atlas-job.sbatch\ncurl --fail --location --output run-atlas.sh \\\n    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/slurm/run-atlas.sh \n You can also save the files directly:  atlas-job.sbatch  and  run-atlas.sh . Both belong in the directory from which you will submit. No executable permission is required here: Slurm reads the submission script, and the container runs the payload with  bash . \n Create  logs/  before submitting.  Slurm opens the output files before the script runs, so creating that directory inside the job would be too late. \n Slurm transfers the submission script, but it does  not  transfer your payload, analysis code, input files, or software build. They must remain accessible on shared storage. Avoid editing  run-atlas.sh  or its dependencies while jobs using them are queued or running; keep a separate copy for a new version. \n"}, {"title": "3. The worker-node script", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#batch-script", "text": "3. The worker-node script \n atlas-job.sbatch  requests one CPU, 4 GB of memory, and 15 minutes for the small test. Its login Bash shell initializes the host's module environment. It creates a separate  runs/JOB_ID/  directory for every submission. \n #!/bin/bash -l\n#SBATCH --job-name=atlas-test\n#SBATCH --partition=cpu\n#SBATCH --nodes=1\n#SBATCH --ntasks=1\n#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1\n#SBATCH --mem=4G\n#SBATCH --time=00:15:00\n#SBATCH --output=logs/%x-%j.out\n#SBATCH --error=logs/%x-%j.err\n#SBATCH --export=NONE\n\n# This script runs on the allocated Ubuntu worker node.\nif [[ $# -ne 2 || $1 != *,* || -z $2 ]]; then\n    echo 'Usage: sbatch atlas-job.sbatch AnalysisBase,VERSION \"$USER_PI\"' >&2\n    exit 2\nfi\nunity_atlas_release=$1\nunity_atlas_pi=$2\nunity_atlas_user=${SLURM_JOB_USER:?Missing Slurm username}\ncd \"${SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR:?Submit from your shared job directory}\" || exit 1\nunity_atlas_payload=\"$PWD/run-atlas.sh\"\nif [[ ! -r \"$unity_atlas_payload\" ]]; then\n    echo \"Cannot read payload: $unity_atlas_payload\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\nunity_atlas_run_dir=\"$PWD/runs/${SLURM_JOB_ID:?Missing Slurm job ID}\"\nmkdir -p \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# Load the container runtime on the host, before entering EL9.\nmodule load apptainer/latest || exit \"$?\"\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nif [[ ! -r \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" ]]; then\n    echo \"ATLASLocalRootBase is unavailable on $(hostname)\" >&2\n    exit 1\nfi\n\n# Enable NVIDIA passthrough only when this job has allocated GPUs.\nif [[ -n ${SLURM_JOB_GPUS:-} ]]; then\n    export ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS=\"${ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS:+$ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS }--nv\"\n    if [[ ${CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES+x} ]]; then\n        export APPTAINERENV_CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=\"$CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"\n    fi\nfi\n\n# Pass arguments explicitly; do not depend on host variables surviving\n# the container's environment filtering. %q quotes each Bash argument.\nprintf -v unity_atlas_command 'bash %q %q %q %q %q %q' \\\n    \"$unity_atlas_payload\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" \\\n    \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\" \"$unity_atlas_pi\" \"$unity_atlas_user\"\nprintf 'Job %s on %s; release %s; output %s\\n' \\\n    \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \"$(hostname)\" \"$unity_atlas_release\" \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\"\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n\n# -r runs the payload inside the container and then leaves the container.\n# Unity exposes /work inside the container; no -m is needed for it.\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\" \\\n    -c el9 -r \"$unity_atlas_command\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nprintf 'Container command exited with status %s\\n' \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nexit \"$unity_atlas_status\" \n The wrapper sources ATLASLocalRootBase explicitly instead of depending on the interactive  setupATLAS  alias in  .bashrc . The  --export=NONE  directive avoids importing the submission shell's full environment; Slurm still supplies its job variables and may initialize the user's login environment. This is not a completely empty environment. The submission commands pass your saved  USER_PI  as an argument; the wrapper obtains your username from  SLURM_JOB_USER  and passes both into the payload. This keeps  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  and  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  usable in scripts without relying on  .bashrc  being read there. Keep software and container startup out of your shell startup files, as described in the  shell-configuration section . \n Unity exposes  /work  inside the container, so the standard command needs no  -m /work . For another filesystem that is missing inside EL9, use the  mount guidance in the environment chapter  and add the corresponding  -m /actual/path  to the container launch. \n This is a  single-node, single-process  job. Slurm already runs the batch script on the worker, so it does not need an additional  srun  for this example. MPI and multiple simultaneous tasks require a different launch pattern. If you later introduce  srun , note that this script uses  --export=NONE : use  srun --export=ALL ...  when the step needs the environment you have prepared. \n"}, {"title": "4. The script inside EL9", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#payload", "text": "4. The script inside EL9 \n run-atlas.sh  starts a fresh Bash process inside the container. It initializes the ATLAS command definitions, runs  asetup  for the release passed at submission, and creates  hist.root  without requiring any input data or credentials. \n #!/bin/bash\n# This script runs inside the EL9 container.\nunity_atlas_release=${1:?Missing ATLAS project and version}\nunity_atlas_run_dir=${2:?Missing output directory}\nunity_atlas_cpus=${3:-1}\n# These were passed explicitly, not inherited from an interactive .bashrc.\nexport USER_PI=${4:?Missing Unity PI directory}\nexport USER=${5:?Missing Unity username}\ncd \"$unity_atlas_run_dir\" || exit 1\n# Do not let a sourced setup script interpret the payload's arguments.\nset --\n\n# A new Bash process needs its own ATLAS command definitions.\n# ALRB defines aliases such as asetup; Bash scripts must enable them.\nshopt -s expand_aliases\nexport ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE=/cvmfs/atlas.cern.ch/repo/ATLASLocalRootBase\nsource \"$ATLAS_LOCAL_ROOT_BASE/user/atlasLocalSetup.sh\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\nasetup \"$unity_atlas_release\"\nunity_atlas_status=$?\nif (( unity_atlas_status != 0 )); then\n    exit \"$unity_atlas_status\"\nfi\n\n# Enable strict error handling after the external setup scripts finish.\nset -euo pipefail\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=\"$unity_atlas_cpus\"\nprintf 'Container OS:\\n'\ncat /etc/os-release\nprintf '\\nWorking directory: %s\\n' \"$PWD\"\n\n# Replace this smoke test with your analysis command.\npython -u - <<'PY'\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nROOT.gRandom.SetSeed(12345)\noutput = ROOT.TFile.Open(\"hist.root\", \"RECREATE\")\nif not output or output.IsZombie():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"Cannot create hist.root\")\nhist = ROOT.TH1D(\"example\", \"ATLAS on Unity; x; Entries\", 50, -5, 5)\nhist.FillRandom(\"gaus\", 10000)\nhist.Write()\noutput.Close()\nprint(\"ROOT version:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"Wrote hist.root with 10000 entries\")\nPY\ntest -s hist.root\nprintf 'Payload finished successfully.\\n' \n shopt -s expand_aliases  lets this non-interactive Bash script use the aliases defined by ATLASLocalRootBase, including  asetup . The setup and  asetup  commands are on separate lines so those definitions are available before the release command is parsed. \n External setup scripts run before strict Bash error handling is enabled; their return codes are checked explicitly. Once setup finishes,  set -euo pipefail  makes a failed analysis command stop the payload. The wrapper returns the container command's status to Slurm. When adapting the example, keep this error handling: a final successful  echo  must not hide a failed analysis. \n"}, {"title": "5. Submit and inspect the result", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#submit", "text": "5. Submit and inspect the result \n Use the  same project and exact release version  selected in the  environment chapter , after confirming it works for your analysis.  ATLAS_RELEASE  has the same  project,version  format here. Do not enter a literal placeholder or a moving  latest  version. The prompt below sets it in your login shell; the batch job itself never prompts for input. \n # Still in the directory containing both scripts and logs/.\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\"); then\n        JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n        printf 'Submitted job: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\n    fi\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi \n Record the returned job ID. In a later login session, set  JOB_ID  to that number before using these commands: \n # Queued/running jobs; a completed job will disappear from squeue.\nsqueue --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\"\n\n# Accounting, including the batch step; may take a short time to update.\nsacct --jobs=\"$JOB_ID\" \\\n      --format=JobID,JobName,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\n\n# These files appear once the job starts.\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-test-${JOB_ID}.err\"\nls -lh \"runs/${JOB_ID}/hist.root\" \n For a successful smoke test, check  all three : \n \n Slurm reports  COMPLETED  with  ExitCode  equal to  0:0 . \n The output log identifies EL9, reports the ROOT version, and ends with the successful payload message and container status zero. \n runs/JOB_ID/hist.root  exists and is nonempty. In an interactive session with the same release, you can open it and inspect the histogram  example , which should have 10,000 entries. \n \n MaxRSS  is generally reported for a step such as  JOB_ID.batch ; keep those rows in the accounting output. Check both logs when a job fails. For Slurm, the first number in  ExitCode  is the return code and the second is the terminating signal. \n To cancel a queued or running job  when you want to stop it : \n scancel \"$JOB_ID\" \n"}, {"title": "6. Replace the test with your analysis", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#analysis", "text": "6. Replace the test with your analysis \n Replace the Python block and the  test -s hist.root  check in  run-atlas.sh  with your analysis command and an appropriate output check. Keep the release setup and run-directory change. Relative output paths then land in  runs/JOB_ID/ ; use absolute paths for shared inputs and code. \n For example, use the analysis checkout under your work area and choose an input file from your  /project  allocation. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with your repository's directory name and use its actual script/input paths.  USER_PI  and  USER  are set by the supplied payload from the arguments passed by the worker script: \n # Inside run-atlas.sh, after asetup and set -euo pipefail.\npython -u \"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas/PROJECT_NAME/run.py\" \\\n    --input \"/project/${USER_PI}/${USER}/input.root\" \\\n    --output result.root\ntest -s result.root \n If your analysis uses a compiled ATLAS package, build it in an EL9 allocation with the same release and platform, then source that build's exact  setup.sh  after  asetup  in the payload. Keep the build accessible on shared storage. Do not build the same directory concurrently from multiple jobs. \n Remote data access and authentication need their own setup; having software on CVMFS does not grant access to protected data. Continue with  Running on datasets with Slurm  for Rucio replication to NET2, worker credentials, XRootD inputs, and retryable arrays. \n Adjust CPU, memory, and time \n Command-line  sbatch  options override the corresponding  #SBATCH  directives. For example, once your analysis is configured to use four CPUs: \n sbatch --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 \\\n    atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\" \n Requesting four CPUs does not automatically parallelize Python or ROOT. The example passes the CPU allocation into the container and sets  OMP_NUM_THREADS ; configure your application's own thread or worker count as needed. Keep that count within the allocation.  --mem  here is memory per node, not per CPU. \n Keep directives at the top of the file, before shell commands. Slurm reads them before executing Bash, so  #SBATCH --output=$HOME/...  does not expand  $HOME . Use a literal path, a Slurm filename token such as  %j  (job ID), or an option on the  sbatch  command line. \n Request an NVIDIA GPU \n The same wrapper detects an allocated GPU through  SLURM_JOB_GPUS , appends  --nv  to  ALRB_CONT_CMDOPTS , and forwards Slurm's  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES  selection when present. This uses the same passthrough option as the  interactive GPU recipe . The batch script sets it itself;  atlas_gpu  remains the shortcut for interactive shells. Request the GPU at submission: \n sbatch --partition=gpu --gpus=1 --cpus-per-task=4 \\\n    --mem=16G --time=02:00:00 atlas-job.sbatch \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$USER_PI\" \n Add  nvidia-smi  after setup inside  run-atlas.sh  to inspect device visibility, then use a CUDA-capable application and compatible software environment. The provided histogram test runs on the CPU even when a GPU is allocated; it does not validate a GPU framework. Do not replace Slurm's device selection with a hard-coded GPU number. \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n No log files yet \n A pending job has not started. Check  squeue ; also confirm  logs/  existed before submission and is writable. \n \n \n module: command not found \n Keep the login-shell shebang  #!/bin/bash -l ; check host shell initialization and Unity's module instructions. \n \n \n setupATLAS  or  asetup  is not found \n Use the supplied explicit setup and alias-expansion sequence; an interactive  .bashrc  alias is not enough for batch scripts. \n \n \n Script or input missing inside the container \n Submit from the shared job directory, keep the payload there, and verify its absolute path inside EL9. Add  -m  only for paths not already visible. \n \n \n Software setup fails \n Confirm the exact project, release, and platform worked in a fresh interactive EL9 session. Check compute-node CVMFS access. \n \n \n FAILED ,  OUT_OF_MEMORY , or  TIMEOUT \n Read both logs and the  sacct  state; fix the command or adjust the measured memory/time requirement. \n \n \n COMPLETED  but output is missing \n Check that the payload actually ran, did not hide a failed command, and wrote to the intended run directory. Keep explicit output checks. \n \n \n GPU unavailable inside the payload \n Check the GPU resource request,  --nv  in the container launch line, Slurm's device selection, and the application's CUDA support. If  nvidia-smi  is missing, inspect startup mount warnings. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "submitting-jobs/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Unity: batch jobs with sbatch  and  partitions . \n US ATLAS analysis-facility benchmarking examples , including ATLASLocalRootBase container commands and batch wrappers. \n ATLAS Software Documentation . \n Slurm sbatch ,  sacct , and  job exit codes . \n Apptainer NVIDIA GPU support . \n \n Next:   Running on datasets with Slurm  extends this setup to a complete ATLAS dataset at NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK. \n"}, {"title": "Running on datasets with Slurm", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html", "text": "Run a local Unity Slurm array over an ATLAS dataset stored at  NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK . Rucio arranges and protects the replica; the workers read its files directly through XRootD. No NFS mount or full dataset download to Unity is required. \n Complete  Grid certificates and authentication  and  Submitting jobs with Slurm  first. This chapter uses the environment chapter's  USER_PI ,  $USER ,  ATLAS_WORKDIR , and shell shortcuts. Unity's PI directory follows  pi_[PI's username] , for example  pi_rclsa_umass_edu . \n 1. Choose a reproducible unit of work \n The starting point is  one closed dataset, one frozen file manifest, and one file per array task . Limit the number of simultaneous tasks and start with a single-file pilot. This keeps retries small and gives every input a stable output location. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Stage \n Where it runs \n Result \n \n \n \n \n Request a replica \n Interactive EL9 session with Rucio \n A rule retaining one dataset copy at NET2. \n \n \n Freeze the input list \n The same authenticated session \n manifest.json , with one available NET2 XRootD URL per file. \n \n \n Submit \n Fresh Unity Ubuntu login shell \n A Slurm array with a concurrency limit. \n \n \n Process \n EL9 container on each worker \n One validated histogram and completion receipt per input. \n \n \n Check and retry \n Unity login shell \n A list of unfinished inputs, or a complete list for merging. \n \n \n \n The example reads a numeric branch and fills a ROOT histogram. It is an executable starting point for connecting your analysis to the workflow, not an ATLAS event selection. Later in the chapter we explain  how to replace it . \n 2. Prepare a campaign directory \n Use a new directory for each dataset/configuration/code version. Here the campaign name is  net2-susy15-v1 ; replace it consistently if you choose another name. On the  Ubuntu login node : \n mkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs\"\nif mkdir \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"; then\n    (\n        set -e\n        cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\n        mkdir logs\n        for name in make-manifest.py atlas-dataset.sbatch run-dataset.sh \\\n                    analyse-file.py dataset_status.py; do\n            curl --fail --location --output \"$name\" \\\n                \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/datasets/$name\"\n        done\n    )\nelse\n    echo 'Choose a new campaign name; the existing directory was not changed.' >&2\nfi \n Stop if directory creation or a download fails. Do not reuse a campaign containing old results. The five downloadable helpers are: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Helper \n Purpose \n \n \n \n \n make-manifest.py \n Check dataset completeness and select registered NET2 file URLs. \n \n \n atlas-dataset.sbatch \n Allocate a worker and enter EL9. \n \n \n run-dataset.sh \n Validate a worker proxy, load the release, and launch the analysis. \n \n \n analyse-file.py \n Read one file, validate the output, and publish its result atomically. \n \n \n dataset_status.py \n Validate results and produce retry or merge lists. \n \n \n \n Keep this directory on shared  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  storage. Slurm transfers the submission script, not the other files. Credentials will be stored separately in private shared home storage. \n 3. Request and retain the NET2 replica \n Start an interactive EL9 session using the existing helpers: \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu\n\n# Allocated Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Inside EL9, use the grid chapter's recommended  atlas_proxy  shortcut . It supplies the explicit certificate/key and proxy output paths. Install the helper once as described there; sourcing its file below also works when the container has not loaded your  .bashrc . \n Set your ATLAS Rucio account; it may differ from Unity's  $USER . The example DID below is a concrete SUSY15 dataset; replace it when processing another dataset: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nlsetup rucio\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy \n Continue only if  atlas_proxy  succeeds. The explicit  manual proxy recipe  remains available if you have not installed the shortcut. Then configure Rucio and the dataset: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nexport RUCIO_AUTH_TYPE=x509_proxy\nexport RSE=NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK\nDID='mc16_13TeV:mc16_13TeV.501261.MGPy8EG_s0zpzp2mu2e_500_100_10.deriv.DAOD_SUSY15.e8092_e7400_s3126_r11891_r11748_p4800_tid27118280_00'\n\nrucio --version\nrucio rse list --rses \"$RSE\"\nrucio did show \"$DID\"\nrucio rule list --did \"$DID\" \n Use the  full dataset DID , including scope, not a filename or an XRootD URL. This is the dataset containing the example file  DAOD_SUSY15.27118280._000001.pool.root.1 ; the manifest will include all of its files. Still check its current replica and rule state before requesting another transfer. A dataset is a set of files; a container is a set of datasets. For a container, inspect its contents with  rucio did content list \"$CONTAINER_DID\" , choose its closed child datasets, and use a separate campaign for each. Do not close an official production dataset yourself; wait for completion or select a closed dataset. \n Check existing rules before creating another one. An existing rule that retains this dataset at NET2 for your whole campaign may be sufficient. Otherwise request  one copy  at this exact RSE, for example for 14 days: \n rucio rule add --copies 1 --rses \"$RSE\" --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" \n Record the returned rule ID. This schedules a site-to-site transfer; it does not download the dataset to Unity. The example lifetime is in seconds and must cover the transfer, queueing, processing, and retries. It is not a statement of NET2 quota or retention policy. \n read -r -p 'Rule ID to monitor: ' RULE_ID\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$RULE_ID\" > rule-id.txt\nrucio rule show \"$RULE_ID\" \n Continue when the rule is  OK  and all file locks are satisfied. A rule ID alone is not evidence that the transfer has finished. If it is stuck, use  rucio rule show --examine \"$RULE_ID\"  and contact the relevant support team with the rule ID. Do not submit repeated rules to retry the same transfer. You can leave the allocation while waiting and return in a later authenticated session. \n ATLAS membership permitting reads does not automatically grant your Rucio account quota or permission to place data at this RSE. If the request is rejected, ask the NET2 storage contact for the appropriate quota, group account, or approval procedure. Do not change accounts or request approval flags without knowing the site's policy. Files present without a protecting rule can later be deleted by Rucio. \n Older Rucio clients \n The commands above use the newer grouped CLI. If the client supplied by  lsetup rucio  does not recognize it, use the equivalent legacy command below. Run  one form , not both: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Newer command \n Legacy equivalent \n \n \n \n \n rucio rse list --rses \"$RSE\" \n rucio list-rses --rses \"$RSE\" \n \n \n rucio did show \"$DID\" \n rucio stat \"$DID\" \n \n \n rucio did content list \"$CONTAINER_DID\" \n rucio list-content \"$CONTAINER_DID\" \n \n \n rucio rule list --did \"$DID\" \n rucio list-rules \"$DID\" \n \n \n rucio rule add --copies 1 --rses \"$RSE\" --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" \n rucio add-rule --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" 1 \"$RSE\" \n \n \n rucio rule show \"$RULE_ID\" \n rucio rule-info \"$RULE_ID\" \n \n \n rucio rule show --examine \"$RULE_ID\" \n rucio rule-info --examine \"$RULE_ID\" \n \n \n \n 4. Freeze a complete input manifest \n Still inside EL9 with  lsetup rucio  and a valid proxy, run: \n python make-manifest.py --did \"$DID\" --rse \"$RSE\" --output manifest.json \n The helper uses Rucio's Python client instead of parsing a printed table. It: \n \n Requires a closed, nonempty dataset and independently lists  all  its files. \n Queries available, directly readable  root://  replicas at  NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK , respecting the RSE's read-availability setting. \n Selects exactly one registered URL per file, sorts by file DID, and records scope, name, size, and catalogue checksums. \n Refuses duplicates, missing replicas, inconsistent metadata, or a changed dataset listing. A partial transfer cannot silently become a partial analysis. \n Publishes  manifest.json  only when complete, and refuses to overwrite an existing manifest. \n \n Inspect the snapshot and optionally export a simple URL list for other tools: \n python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('manifest.json') as stream:\n    manifest = json.load(stream)\nfiles = manifest['files']\nprint('Dataset:', manifest['did'])\nprint('RSE:', manifest['rse'])\nprint('Files:', len(files))\nprint('Total size: %.3f GB' % (sum(f['bytes'] for f in files) / 1e9))\nprint('First URL:', files[0]['url'])\nwith open('inputs.txt', 'x') as stream:\n    stream.writelines(f['url'] + '\\n' for f in files)\nPY \n The Slurm example uses  manifest.json , which preserves more information than  inputs.txt . Catalogue availability is checked at preparation time; worker reads can still fail later, which is why output validation and retries remain necessary. Stored catalogue checksums are provenance; selective ROOT reads do not recompute a whole-file checksum. \n Use the URLs Rucio returns.  Do not reconstruct hashed  /rucio/...  directories or replace hostnames with string substitutions.  root://xrootd.data.net2.mghpcc.org:1094//atlaslocalgroupdisk/...  is a NET2 access pattern, but the manifest must follow the registered read endpoints. If those endpoints are inaccessible from a worker, resolve that with NET2/Rucio configuration before scaling up. \n 5. Make the proxy available to workers \n The grid chapter's  /tmp  proxy belongs to its node/container. A batch worker cannot access it merely because its path was exported. Copy the valid proxy to a  private shared-home directory , outside the campaign. Run this in the authenticated EL9 session, from the campaign directory: \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nPROXY_DIR=\"$HOME/$(dirname \"$PROXY_REL\")\"\numask 077\nmkdir -p \"$PROXY_DIR\"\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\" \"$HOME/.globus/unity-atlas-proxies\" \"$PROXY_DIR\"\n\nif PROXY_NEW=$(mktemp \"$PROXY_DIR/.x509up.XXXXXXXX\"); then\n    if cp -- \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \"$PROXY_NEW\" \\\n        && chmod 600 \"$PROXY_NEW\" \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -exists \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -acexists atlas \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -all; then\n        mv -f -- \"$PROXY_NEW\" \"$HOME/$PROXY_REL\"\n    else\n        rm -f -- \"$PROXY_NEW\"\n        echo 'Proxy was not published; fix authentication before submitting.' >&2\n    fi\nfi \n Inspect  both  the proxy and VOMS-attribute lifetimes. Each worker requires enough remaining validity for its requested wall time plus 15 minutes. The default two-hour job therefore needs more than 2 hours 15 minutes remaining  when it starts , after queueing. \n The batch wrapper passes the relative proxy path explicitly because it uses  --export=NONE . Inside EL9, the payload resolves it against that container's  $HOME , copies it into a private worker  /tmp  directory, and validates the copy. It needs no private key, password prompt, or Rucio dataset query. It preserves the grid CA certificate-directory setting while keeping grid-tool Python and library setup separate from the analysis release. \n For renewal, run  atlas_proxy  again in an authenticated EL9 session, then repeat the copy block above from the same campaign directory only after it succeeds. The  grid chapter  also gives the manual commands. The temporary-file rename updates the shared source atomically.  Queued tasks can use the replacement; already-running tasks keep their original copy.  Do not request a wall time longer than the credential can cover, and do not extend a running job beyond its validated proxy lifetime. \n Leave the container and allocation with  exit  twice. The remaining preparation and  sbatch  commands run in a  fresh Ubuntu login shell . \n 6. Configure and freeze the analysis \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,exact-version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nread -r -p 'Numeric branch (for example MuonsAuxDyn.pt): ' ATLAS_BRANCH\n\npython3 - \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$ATLAS_BRANCH\" <<'PY'\nimport json\nimport sys\nconfig = dict(release=sys.argv[1], tree='CollectionTree', branch=sys.argv[2],\n              bins=100, xmin=0.0, xmax=200000.0)\nwith open('analysis-config.json', 'x') as stream:\n    json.dump(config, stream, indent=2)\n    stream.write('\\n')\nPY \n Choose the exact release that supports your input and analysis.  MuonsAuxDyn.pt  is an example, not a promise that every DAOD has that branch; inspect your file's schema and select an existing numeric scalar/vector branch. Adjust the tree name and histogram range in  analysis-config.json  before freezing it. The example range is suitable for a momentum expressed in MeV. \n The Python analysis opens the file, checks its size against the manifest, books a histogram and an event count, and runs the event loop over the selected branch. It checks the processed event count and reopens the output before publishing it. It does not treat a metadata-only file open as successful analysis. \n Freeze the input list, configuration, and code together: \n sha256sum manifest.json analysis-config.json make-manifest.py \\\n    atlas-dataset.sbatch run-dataset.sh analyse-file.py dataset_status.py \\\n    > campaign.sha256\nsha256sum --check --strict campaign.sha256 \n Do not edit these files or regenerate this checksum list while jobs are queued, running, or being retried. Both host and container check it. For a new analysis version, create a new campaign directory; keep the original manifest and results as provenance.  logs/  must already exist before submission. \n 7. Run one task, then scale out \n From the  Ubuntu login shell , submit a pilot for manifest index zero: \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nif PILOT_ID=$(sbatch --parsable --array=0 atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"); then\n    PILOT_ID=${PILOT_ID%%;*}\n    printf 'Pilot job: %s\\n' \"$PILOT_ID\"\nfi \n The defaults are one CPU, 4 GB, and two hours per task. Wait for completion and inspect: \n squeue --jobs=\"$PILOT_ID\"\nsacct --jobs=\"$PILOT_ID\" --format=JobID,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-dataset-${PILOT_ID}_0.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-dataset-${PILOT_ID}_0.err\"\npython3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" \n The status tool reports  1/N  complete after a successful pilot and returns nonzero while the whole dataset is unfinished. Inspect  results/task-000000/result.root  and  receipt.json , and require Slurm  COMPLETED  with exit code  0:0 . If configuration or analysis code needs changing, make a fresh campaign before launching the dataset. Adjust resources based on the pilot's time and memory, not on the bulk-transfer rate alone. \n Get the file count and inspect the cluster's array limits: \n NFILES=$(python3 -c 'import json; print(len(json.load(open(\"manifest.json\"))[\"files\"]))')\nscontrol show config | grep -E 'MaxArraySize|SchedulerParameters'\nprintf 'Dataset has %s tasks\\n' \"$NFILES\" \n MaxArraySize  limits the highest array index, and site policy can also limit task counts and submitted jobs. If the dataset fits those limits, submit with at most  four simultaneous tasks  initially: \n if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable --array=\"0-$((NFILES-1))%4\" \\\n    atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"); then\n    JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n    printf 'Dataset array: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\nfi \n The completed pilot result is validated and reused rather than counted twice. A task publishes to  results/task-INDEX/  only after its temporary output and receipt pass validation. Retries use the same manifest index. Interrupted temporary directories do not count as completed inputs. \n Use command-line options such as  --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=8G --time=01:00:00  to override resources. The sample enables ROOT threading only up to the CPU allocation. Increase  %4  gradually after measuring event throughput, memory, and the effect of concurrent readers. A fast single-file transfer does not establish the capacity available to every simultaneous job. \n Datasets larger than one array \n The optional third script argument is a  manifest offset . For example, a second window of 500 files uses local array indices  0-499  and offset  500 , which process manifest indices  500-999 : \n # Example only: requires at least 1000 files and a site limit permitting 500 tasks.\nsbatch --array=0-499%4 atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\" 500 \n Submit only windows within the manifest, shorten the final window, and finish one window before submitting the next if you want a global concurrency limit of four.  %4  is a limit  per array , not across all your arrays. Global manifest indices remain stable even though Slurm indices restart at zero. \n 8. Check completeness, retry, and merge \n Monitor arrays with  squeue  and  sacct  as above. After the relevant arrays have stopped, validate the published results: \n python3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" --retry-list retry-1.txt \n The helper checks every expected input, its code/configuration hashes, event counts, and the output's size and SHA-256. It returns nonzero for unfinished or invalid results and writes their manifest indices to  retry-1.txt . It refuses to overwrite old lists; use  retry-2.txt  for a later pass. A Slurm success flag or the mere existence of a ROOT file is insufficient. \n Inspect the failures, renew the shared proxy if needed, and fix operational causes before retrying. For a dataset using a single zero-offset array: \n RETRY=$(cat retry-1.txt)\nif [[ -n \"$RETRY\" ]]; then\n    sbatch --array=\"${RETRY}%4\" atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"\nfi \n For offset windows, the retry list contains  global  manifest indices, including files in future windows that have not run yet. First select only indices belonging to the window you are retrying, then subtract its offset to obtain local Slurm indices and pass that offset again. For example, global index  507  is  --array=7  with offset  500 . Keep retry arrays within the same site limits. \n A published result that fails validation is deliberately not overwritten. With its tasks stopped, preserve it under a separate  quarantine/  directory before retrying that index. Do not delete a valid result to force a rerun; changed analysis code belongs in a new campaign. \n Only when every file has a valid result should you produce the merge list: \n python3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" --outputs outputs.txt \n If anything is incomplete, no new merge list is written. The list contains exactly one result per input, in manifest order. Do not collect outputs with a broad wildcard over original jobs, retries, and temporary directories. \n For the example histograms, merge  inside an allocated EL9 session with the same release . Revalidate the complete manifest and merge those exact outputs, avoiding shell argument-length limits. Publish the merged file only after success: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nATLAS_RELEASE=$(python3 -c 'import json; print(json.load(open(\"analysis-config.json\"))[\"release\"])')\nasetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport os\nimport tempfile\nimport ROOT\nfrom dataset_status import load_campaign, inspect_campaign\n\ncampaign = load_campaign('.')\noutputs, failures = inspect_campaign(campaign)\nif failures:\n    raise RuntimeError('Dataset incomplete; do not merge')\ntarget = Path('merged.root')\nif target.exists():\n    raise RuntimeError('merged.root already exists; preserve the previous merge')\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix='.merge-', dir='.') as directory:\n    temporary = Path(directory) / 'merged.root'\n    merger = ROOT.TFileMerger()\n    if not merger.OutputFile(str(temporary), 'RECREATE'):\n        raise RuntimeError('Cannot create merge output')\n    for output in outputs:\n        if not merger.AddFile(str(output)):\n            raise RuntimeError(f'Cannot add {output}')\n    if not merger.Merge():\n        raise RuntimeError('Merge failed')\n    del merger\n    check = ROOT.TFile.Open(str(temporary), 'READ')\n    if not check or check.IsZombie() or not check.Get('example'):\n        raise RuntimeError('Merged histogram is missing or unreadable')\n    check.Close()\n    os.link(temporary, target)  # Atomic publication; refuses an existing target.\nprint('Merged', len(outputs), 'validated inputs into', target)\nPY \n This merges the example's histogram outputs,  not the original xAOD/DAOD files . Use your analysis framework's merge and metadata checks for other output formats. Preserve  manifest.json , configuration, code, receipts, and job IDs alongside the final result. Move durable results to your  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  area according to Unity storage policy. \n When all queued/running tasks and retries have finished, destroy the campaign's shared proxy from an EL9 session with  lsetup rucio : \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nvoms-proxy-destroy -file \"$HOME/$PROXY_REL\" \n Keep the Rucio rule alive through processing and recovery. In an authenticated Rucio session, reload its ID with  RULE_ID=$(cat rule-id.txt) . If you own it and need more time, use  rucio rule update --lifetime 1209600 \"$RULE_ID\"  (legacy:  rucio update-rule --lifetime 1209600 \"$RULE_ID\" ), subject to policy. Do not delete rules owned by someone else or remove replicas directly from dCache. \n 9. Adapt the example to your analysis \n Replace the histogram computation in  analyse-file.py  before freezing a new campaign. The selected input is  campaign.manifest['files'][index]['url'] ; pass that URL to ROOT, uproot, or your ATLAS analysis executable. Keep the following behavior: \n \n A read failure produces a nonzero exit; never silently skip a file. \n Output is written into the supplied temporary  staging  directory and validated before publication. \n The receipt identifies the input and exact configuration/code, and records a meaningful completeness check for your analysis. After event selections, distinguish events processed from events selected. \n Any extra scripts, configuration, or compiled-build identity are included in  campaign.sha256  and the receipt's provenance checks. The example's  INPUTS  list is in  dataset_status.py . \n \n The starting granularity is one file per task. If files are tiny compared with container/release startup time, group several files into a task in a later version and record/check every member. If one file exceeds the credential or wall-time budget, use explicit nonoverlapping event ranges and validate total coverage. Keep these partitions fixed for retries. \n Direct XRootD reads avoid a second full copy in Unity storage. If measurements show that repeated scans benefit from staging, download into job-local scratch, verify the catalogue checksum, and account for local capacity and cleanup. Keep dataset discovery out of each worker: catalogue queries are done once when preparing the manifest. \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n Rucio rejects the rule \n Exact RSE name, account quota, write permission, and site approval policy. \n \n \n Rule remains replicating or stuck \n Inspect the rule and transfer errors; do not start from an incomplete URL list. \n \n \n Manifest creation fails \n Closed DATASET rather than container, all files available, direct ROOT protocol, and readable RSE status. Existing manifests are never replaced. \n \n \n Worker cannot find the proxy \n Copy it into private shared home and pass the relative path; the interactive  /tmp  filename is not shared. \n \n \n Proxy expires too soon \n Renew and atomically republish the shared source, then retry; running tasks keep their original snapshot. \n \n \n campaign.sha256  fails \n A frozen input changed or is missing. Restore it or create a new campaign rather than mixing versions. \n \n \n ROOT branch/type error \n Inspect the actual DAOD schema and use a supported numeric branch and compatible release. \n \n \n Some tasks time out or run out of memory \n Use their logs and accounting to adjust resources; do not mark them successful. \n \n \n Existing published result is invalid \n Stop its tasks, preserve the directory in quarantine, investigate, then retry. \n \n \n Array rejected \n Check index/task/submission limits; use bounded windows with explicit offsets. \n \n \n All jobs completed, but the status tool reports missing inputs \n Check failed/retried tasks and manifest indices; do not merge until complete. \n \n \n \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the command, error, client/release versions, node hostname, and relevant task or rule ID. Omit proxy contents, private keys, and tokens. \n References \n \n Rucio:  replication rules ,  current CLI examples ,  DID client , and  replica client . \n Rucio CLI definitions:  rules  and  replicas . \n Unity:  batch jobs  and  storage . \n Slurm:  job arrays  and  sbatch . \n ROOT:  RDataFrame  and  TFileMerger . \n \n Next:   Using Pixi  covers personal tools and project package environments. \n"}, {"title": "1. Choose a reproducible unit of work", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#workflow", "text": "1. Choose a reproducible unit of work \n The starting point is  one closed dataset, one frozen file manifest, and one file per array task . Limit the number of simultaneous tasks and start with a single-file pilot. This keeps retries small and gives every input a stable output location. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Stage \n Where it runs \n Result \n \n \n \n \n Request a replica \n Interactive EL9 session with Rucio \n A rule retaining one dataset copy at NET2. \n \n \n Freeze the input list \n The same authenticated session \n manifest.json , with one available NET2 XRootD URL per file. \n \n \n Submit \n Fresh Unity Ubuntu login shell \n A Slurm array with a concurrency limit. \n \n \n Process \n EL9 container on each worker \n One validated histogram and completion receipt per input. \n \n \n Check and retry \n Unity login shell \n A list of unfinished inputs, or a complete list for merging. \n \n \n \n The example reads a numeric branch and fills a ROOT histogram. It is an executable starting point for connecting your analysis to the workflow, not an ATLAS event selection. Later in the chapter we explain  how to replace it . \n"}, {"title": "2. Prepare a campaign directory", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#prepare", "text": "2. Prepare a campaign directory \n Use a new directory for each dataset/configuration/code version. Here the campaign name is  net2-susy15-v1 ; replace it consistently if you choose another name. On the  Ubuntu login node : \n mkdir -p \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs\"\nif mkdir \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"; then\n    (\n        set -e\n        cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\n        mkdir logs\n        for name in make-manifest.py atlas-dataset.sbatch run-dataset.sh \\\n                    analyse-file.py dataset_status.py; do\n            curl --fail --location --output \"$name\" \\\n                \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/datasets/$name\"\n        done\n    )\nelse\n    echo 'Choose a new campaign name; the existing directory was not changed.' >&2\nfi \n Stop if directory creation or a download fails. Do not reuse a campaign containing old results. The five downloadable helpers are: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Helper \n Purpose \n \n \n \n \n make-manifest.py \n Check dataset completeness and select registered NET2 file URLs. \n \n \n atlas-dataset.sbatch \n Allocate a worker and enter EL9. \n \n \n run-dataset.sh \n Validate a worker proxy, load the release, and launch the analysis. \n \n \n analyse-file.py \n Read one file, validate the output, and publish its result atomically. \n \n \n dataset_status.py \n Validate results and produce retry or merge lists. \n \n \n \n Keep this directory on shared  /work/${USER_PI}/${USER}  storage. Slurm transfers the submission script, not the other files. Credentials will be stored separately in private shared home storage. \n"}, {"title": "3. Request and retain the NET2 replica", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#replicate", "text": "3. Request and retain the NET2 replica \n Start an interactive EL9 session using the existing helpers: \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu\n\n# Allocated Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n Inside EL9, use the grid chapter's recommended  atlas_proxy  shortcut . It supplies the explicit certificate/key and proxy output paths. Install the helper once as described there; sourcing its file below also works when the container has not loaded your  .bashrc . \n Set your ATLAS Rucio account; it may differ from Unity's  $USER . The example DID below is a concrete SUSY15 dataset; replace it when processing another dataset: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nlsetup rucio\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy \n Continue only if  atlas_proxy  succeeds. The explicit  manual proxy recipe  remains available if you have not installed the shortcut. Then configure Rucio and the dataset: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nexport RUCIO_AUTH_TYPE=x509_proxy\nexport RSE=NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK\nDID='mc16_13TeV:mc16_13TeV.501261.MGPy8EG_s0zpzp2mu2e_500_100_10.deriv.DAOD_SUSY15.e8092_e7400_s3126_r11891_r11748_p4800_tid27118280_00'\n\nrucio --version\nrucio rse list --rses \"$RSE\"\nrucio did show \"$DID\"\nrucio rule list --did \"$DID\" \n Use the  full dataset DID , including scope, not a filename or an XRootD URL. This is the dataset containing the example file  DAOD_SUSY15.27118280._000001.pool.root.1 ; the manifest will include all of its files. Still check its current replica and rule state before requesting another transfer. A dataset is a set of files; a container is a set of datasets. For a container, inspect its contents with  rucio did content list \"$CONTAINER_DID\" , choose its closed child datasets, and use a separate campaign for each. Do not close an official production dataset yourself; wait for completion or select a closed dataset. \n Check existing rules before creating another one. An existing rule that retains this dataset at NET2 for your whole campaign may be sufficient. Otherwise request  one copy  at this exact RSE, for example for 14 days: \n rucio rule add --copies 1 --rses \"$RSE\" --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" \n Record the returned rule ID. This schedules a site-to-site transfer; it does not download the dataset to Unity. The example lifetime is in seconds and must cover the transfer, queueing, processing, and retries. It is not a statement of NET2 quota or retention policy. \n read -r -p 'Rule ID to monitor: ' RULE_ID\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$RULE_ID\" > rule-id.txt\nrucio rule show \"$RULE_ID\" \n Continue when the rule is  OK  and all file locks are satisfied. A rule ID alone is not evidence that the transfer has finished. If it is stuck, use  rucio rule show --examine \"$RULE_ID\"  and contact the relevant support team with the rule ID. Do not submit repeated rules to retry the same transfer. You can leave the allocation while waiting and return in a later authenticated session. \n ATLAS membership permitting reads does not automatically grant your Rucio account quota or permission to place data at this RSE. If the request is rejected, ask the NET2 storage contact for the appropriate quota, group account, or approval procedure. Do not change accounts or request approval flags without knowing the site's policy. Files present without a protecting rule can later be deleted by Rucio. \n Older Rucio clients \n The commands above use the newer grouped CLI. If the client supplied by  lsetup rucio  does not recognize it, use the equivalent legacy command below. Run  one form , not both: \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Newer command \n Legacy equivalent \n \n \n \n \n rucio rse list --rses \"$RSE\" \n rucio list-rses --rses \"$RSE\" \n \n \n rucio did show \"$DID\" \n rucio stat \"$DID\" \n \n \n rucio did content list \"$CONTAINER_DID\" \n rucio list-content \"$CONTAINER_DID\" \n \n \n rucio rule list --did \"$DID\" \n rucio list-rules \"$DID\" \n \n \n rucio rule add --copies 1 --rses \"$RSE\" --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" \n rucio add-rule --lifetime 1209600 \"$DID\" 1 \"$RSE\" \n \n \n rucio rule show \"$RULE_ID\" \n rucio rule-info \"$RULE_ID\" \n \n \n rucio rule show --examine \"$RULE_ID\" \n rucio rule-info --examine \"$RULE_ID\" \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "4. Freeze a complete input manifest", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#manifest", "text": "4. Freeze a complete input manifest \n Still inside EL9 with  lsetup rucio  and a valid proxy, run: \n python make-manifest.py --did \"$DID\" --rse \"$RSE\" --output manifest.json \n The helper uses Rucio's Python client instead of parsing a printed table. It: \n \n Requires a closed, nonempty dataset and independently lists  all  its files. \n Queries available, directly readable  root://  replicas at  NET2_LOCALGROUPDISK , respecting the RSE's read-availability setting. \n Selects exactly one registered URL per file, sorts by file DID, and records scope, name, size, and catalogue checksums. \n Refuses duplicates, missing replicas, inconsistent metadata, or a changed dataset listing. A partial transfer cannot silently become a partial analysis. \n Publishes  manifest.json  only when complete, and refuses to overwrite an existing manifest. \n \n Inspect the snapshot and optionally export a simple URL list for other tools: \n python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nwith open('manifest.json') as stream:\n    manifest = json.load(stream)\nfiles = manifest['files']\nprint('Dataset:', manifest['did'])\nprint('RSE:', manifest['rse'])\nprint('Files:', len(files))\nprint('Total size: %.3f GB' % (sum(f['bytes'] for f in files) / 1e9))\nprint('First URL:', files[0]['url'])\nwith open('inputs.txt', 'x') as stream:\n    stream.writelines(f['url'] + '\\n' for f in files)\nPY \n The Slurm example uses  manifest.json , which preserves more information than  inputs.txt . Catalogue availability is checked at preparation time; worker reads can still fail later, which is why output validation and retries remain necessary. Stored catalogue checksums are provenance; selective ROOT reads do not recompute a whole-file checksum. \n Use the URLs Rucio returns.  Do not reconstruct hashed  /rucio/...  directories or replace hostnames with string substitutions.  root://xrootd.data.net2.mghpcc.org:1094//atlaslocalgroupdisk/...  is a NET2 access pattern, but the manifest must follow the registered read endpoints. If those endpoints are inaccessible from a worker, resolve that with NET2/Rucio configuration before scaling up. \n"}, {"title": "5. Make the proxy available to workers", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#worker-proxy", "text": "5. Make the proxy available to workers \n The grid chapter's  /tmp  proxy belongs to its node/container. A batch worker cannot access it merely because its path was exported. Copy the valid proxy to a  private shared-home directory , outside the campaign. Run this in the authenticated EL9 session, from the campaign directory: \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nPROXY_DIR=\"$HOME/$(dirname \"$PROXY_REL\")\"\numask 077\nmkdir -p \"$PROXY_DIR\"\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\" \"$HOME/.globus/unity-atlas-proxies\" \"$PROXY_DIR\"\n\nif PROXY_NEW=$(mktemp \"$PROXY_DIR/.x509up.XXXXXXXX\"); then\n    if cp -- \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" \"$PROXY_NEW\" \\\n        && chmod 600 \"$PROXY_NEW\" \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -exists \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -acexists atlas \\\n        && voms-proxy-info -file \"$PROXY_NEW\" -all; then\n        mv -f -- \"$PROXY_NEW\" \"$HOME/$PROXY_REL\"\n    else\n        rm -f -- \"$PROXY_NEW\"\n        echo 'Proxy was not published; fix authentication before submitting.' >&2\n    fi\nfi \n Inspect  both  the proxy and VOMS-attribute lifetimes. Each worker requires enough remaining validity for its requested wall time plus 15 minutes. The default two-hour job therefore needs more than 2 hours 15 minutes remaining  when it starts , after queueing. \n The batch wrapper passes the relative proxy path explicitly because it uses  --export=NONE . Inside EL9, the payload resolves it against that container's  $HOME , copies it into a private worker  /tmp  directory, and validates the copy. It needs no private key, password prompt, or Rucio dataset query. It preserves the grid CA certificate-directory setting while keeping grid-tool Python and library setup separate from the analysis release. \n For renewal, run  atlas_proxy  again in an authenticated EL9 session, then repeat the copy block above from the same campaign directory only after it succeeds. The  grid chapter  also gives the manual commands. The temporary-file rename updates the shared source atomically.  Queued tasks can use the replacement; already-running tasks keep their original copy.  Do not request a wall time longer than the credential can cover, and do not extend a running job beyond its validated proxy lifetime. \n Leave the container and allocation with  exit  twice. The remaining preparation and  sbatch  commands run in a  fresh Ubuntu login shell . \n"}, {"title": "6. Configure and freeze the analysis", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#configure", "text": "6. Configure and freeze the analysis \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,exact-version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nread -r -p 'Numeric branch (for example MuonsAuxDyn.pt): ' ATLAS_BRANCH\n\npython3 - \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" \"$ATLAS_BRANCH\" <<'PY'\nimport json\nimport sys\nconfig = dict(release=sys.argv[1], tree='CollectionTree', branch=sys.argv[2],\n              bins=100, xmin=0.0, xmax=200000.0)\nwith open('analysis-config.json', 'x') as stream:\n    json.dump(config, stream, indent=2)\n    stream.write('\\n')\nPY \n Choose the exact release that supports your input and analysis.  MuonsAuxDyn.pt  is an example, not a promise that every DAOD has that branch; inspect your file's schema and select an existing numeric scalar/vector branch. Adjust the tree name and histogram range in  analysis-config.json  before freezing it. The example range is suitable for a momentum expressed in MeV. \n The Python analysis opens the file, checks its size against the manifest, books a histogram and an event count, and runs the event loop over the selected branch. It checks the processed event count and reopens the output before publishing it. It does not treat a metadata-only file open as successful analysis. \n Freeze the input list, configuration, and code together: \n sha256sum manifest.json analysis-config.json make-manifest.py \\\n    atlas-dataset.sbatch run-dataset.sh analyse-file.py dataset_status.py \\\n    > campaign.sha256\nsha256sum --check --strict campaign.sha256 \n Do not edit these files or regenerate this checksum list while jobs are queued, running, or being retried. Both host and container check it. For a new analysis version, create a new campaign directory; keep the original manifest and results as provenance.  logs/  must already exist before submission. \n"}, {"title": "7. Run one task, then scale out", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#submit", "text": "7. Run one task, then scale out \n From the  Ubuntu login shell , submit a pilot for manifest index zero: \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nif PILOT_ID=$(sbatch --parsable --array=0 atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"); then\n    PILOT_ID=${PILOT_ID%%;*}\n    printf 'Pilot job: %s\\n' \"$PILOT_ID\"\nfi \n The defaults are one CPU, 4 GB, and two hours per task. Wait for completion and inspect: \n squeue --jobs=\"$PILOT_ID\"\nsacct --jobs=\"$PILOT_ID\" --format=JobID,State,ExitCode,Elapsed,MaxRSS\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-dataset-${PILOT_ID}_0.out\"\ntail -n 50 \"logs/atlas-dataset-${PILOT_ID}_0.err\"\npython3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" \n The status tool reports  1/N  complete after a successful pilot and returns nonzero while the whole dataset is unfinished. Inspect  results/task-000000/result.root  and  receipt.json , and require Slurm  COMPLETED  with exit code  0:0 . If configuration or analysis code needs changing, make a fresh campaign before launching the dataset. Adjust resources based on the pilot's time and memory, not on the bulk-transfer rate alone. \n Get the file count and inspect the cluster's array limits: \n NFILES=$(python3 -c 'import json; print(len(json.load(open(\"manifest.json\"))[\"files\"]))')\nscontrol show config | grep -E 'MaxArraySize|SchedulerParameters'\nprintf 'Dataset has %s tasks\\n' \"$NFILES\" \n MaxArraySize  limits the highest array index, and site policy can also limit task counts and submitted jobs. If the dataset fits those limits, submit with at most  four simultaneous tasks  initially: \n if JOB_ID=$(sbatch --parsable --array=\"0-$((NFILES-1))%4\" \\\n    atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"); then\n    JOB_ID=${JOB_ID%%;*}\n    printf 'Dataset array: %s\\n' \"$JOB_ID\"\nfi \n The completed pilot result is validated and reused rather than counted twice. A task publishes to  results/task-INDEX/  only after its temporary output and receipt pass validation. Retries use the same manifest index. Interrupted temporary directories do not count as completed inputs. \n Use command-line options such as  --cpus-per-task=4 --mem=8G --time=01:00:00  to override resources. The sample enables ROOT threading only up to the CPU allocation. Increase  %4  gradually after measuring event throughput, memory, and the effect of concurrent readers. A fast single-file transfer does not establish the capacity available to every simultaneous job. \n Datasets larger than one array \n The optional third script argument is a  manifest offset . For example, a second window of 500 files uses local array indices  0-499  and offset  500 , which process manifest indices  500-999 : \n # Example only: requires at least 1000 files and a site limit permitting 500 tasks.\nsbatch --array=0-499%4 atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\" 500 \n Submit only windows within the manifest, shorten the final window, and finish one window before submitting the next if you want a global concurrency limit of four.  %4  is a limit  per array , not across all your arrays. Global manifest indices remain stable even though Slurm indices restart at zero. \n"}, {"title": "8. Check completeness, retry, and merge", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#results", "text": "8. Check completeness, retry, and merge \n Monitor arrays with  squeue  and  sacct  as above. After the relevant arrays have stopped, validate the published results: \n python3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" --retry-list retry-1.txt \n The helper checks every expected input, its code/configuration hashes, event counts, and the output's size and SHA-256. It returns nonzero for unfinished or invalid results and writes their manifest indices to  retry-1.txt . It refuses to overwrite old lists; use  retry-2.txt  for a later pass. A Slurm success flag or the mere existence of a ROOT file is insufficient. \n Inspect the failures, renew the shared proxy if needed, and fix operational causes before retrying. For a dataset using a single zero-offset array: \n RETRY=$(cat retry-1.txt)\nif [[ -n \"$RETRY\" ]]; then\n    sbatch --array=\"${RETRY}%4\" atlas-dataset.sbatch \"$USER_PI\" \"$PROXY_REL\"\nfi \n For offset windows, the retry list contains  global  manifest indices, including files in future windows that have not run yet. First select only indices belonging to the window you are retrying, then subtract its offset to obtain local Slurm indices and pass that offset again. For example, global index  507  is  --array=7  with offset  500 . Keep retry arrays within the same site limits. \n A published result that fails validation is deliberately not overwritten. With its tasks stopped, preserve it under a separate  quarantine/  directory before retrying that index. Do not delete a valid result to force a rerun; changed analysis code belongs in a new campaign. \n Only when every file has a valid result should you produce the merge list: \n python3 dataset_status.py --campaign \"$PWD\" --outputs outputs.txt \n If anything is incomplete, no new merge list is written. The list contains exactly one result per input, in manifest order. Do not collect outputs with a broad wildcard over original jobs, retries, and temporary directories. \n For the example histograms, merge  inside an allocated EL9 session with the same release . Revalidate the complete manifest and merge those exact outputs, avoiding shell argument-length limits. Publish the merged file only after success: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/jobs/net2-susy15-v1\"\nATLAS_RELEASE=$(python3 -c 'import json; print(json.load(open(\"analysis-config.json\"))[\"release\"])')\nasetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport os\nimport tempfile\nimport ROOT\nfrom dataset_status import load_campaign, inspect_campaign\n\ncampaign = load_campaign('.')\noutputs, failures = inspect_campaign(campaign)\nif failures:\n    raise RuntimeError('Dataset incomplete; do not merge')\ntarget = Path('merged.root')\nif target.exists():\n    raise RuntimeError('merged.root already exists; preserve the previous merge')\nwith tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix='.merge-', dir='.') as directory:\n    temporary = Path(directory) / 'merged.root'\n    merger = ROOT.TFileMerger()\n    if not merger.OutputFile(str(temporary), 'RECREATE'):\n        raise RuntimeError('Cannot create merge output')\n    for output in outputs:\n        if not merger.AddFile(str(output)):\n            raise RuntimeError(f'Cannot add {output}')\n    if not merger.Merge():\n        raise RuntimeError('Merge failed')\n    del merger\n    check = ROOT.TFile.Open(str(temporary), 'READ')\n    if not check or check.IsZombie() or not check.Get('example'):\n        raise RuntimeError('Merged histogram is missing or unreadable')\n    check.Close()\n    os.link(temporary, target)  # Atomic publication; refuses an existing target.\nprint('Merged', len(outputs), 'validated inputs into', target)\nPY \n This merges the example's histogram outputs,  not the original xAOD/DAOD files . Use your analysis framework's merge and metadata checks for other output formats. Preserve  manifest.json , configuration, code, receipts, and job IDs alongside the final result. Move durable results to your  /project/${USER_PI}/${USER}  area according to Unity storage policy. \n When all queued/running tasks and retries have finished, destroy the campaign's shared proxy from an EL9 session with  lsetup rucio : \n PROXY_REL=\".globus/unity-atlas-proxies/$(basename \"$PWD\")/x509up\"\nvoms-proxy-destroy -file \"$HOME/$PROXY_REL\" \n Keep the Rucio rule alive through processing and recovery. In an authenticated Rucio session, reload its ID with  RULE_ID=$(cat rule-id.txt) . If you own it and need more time, use  rucio rule update --lifetime 1209600 \"$RULE_ID\"  (legacy:  rucio update-rule --lifetime 1209600 \"$RULE_ID\" ), subject to policy. Do not delete rules owned by someone else or remove replicas directly from dCache. \n"}, {"title": "9. Adapt the example to your analysis", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#your-analysis", "text": "9. Adapt the example to your analysis \n Replace the histogram computation in  analyse-file.py  before freezing a new campaign. The selected input is  campaign.manifest['files'][index]['url'] ; pass that URL to ROOT, uproot, or your ATLAS analysis executable. Keep the following behavior: \n \n A read failure produces a nonzero exit; never silently skip a file. \n Output is written into the supplied temporary  staging  directory and validated before publication. \n The receipt identifies the input and exact configuration/code, and records a meaningful completeness check for your analysis. After event selections, distinguish events processed from events selected. \n Any extra scripts, configuration, or compiled-build identity are included in  campaign.sha256  and the receipt's provenance checks. The example's  INPUTS  list is in  dataset_status.py . \n \n The starting granularity is one file per task. If files are tiny compared with container/release startup time, group several files into a task in a later version and record/check every member. If one file exceeds the credential or wall-time budget, use explicit nonoverlapping event ranges and validate total coverage. Keep these partitions fixed for retries. \n Direct XRootD reads avoid a second full copy in Unity storage. If measurements show that repeated scans benefit from staging, download into job-local scratch, verify the catalogue checksum, and account for local capacity and cleanup. Keep dataset discovery out of each worker: catalogue queries are done once when preparing the manifest. \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n Rucio rejects the rule \n Exact RSE name, account quota, write permission, and site approval policy. \n \n \n Rule remains replicating or stuck \n Inspect the rule and transfer errors; do not start from an incomplete URL list. \n \n \n Manifest creation fails \n Closed DATASET rather than container, all files available, direct ROOT protocol, and readable RSE status. Existing manifests are never replaced. \n \n \n Worker cannot find the proxy \n Copy it into private shared home and pass the relative path; the interactive  /tmp  filename is not shared. \n \n \n Proxy expires too soon \n Renew and atomically republish the shared source, then retry; running tasks keep their original snapshot. \n \n \n campaign.sha256  fails \n A frozen input changed or is missing. Restore it or create a new campaign rather than mixing versions. \n \n \n ROOT branch/type error \n Inspect the actual DAOD schema and use a supported numeric branch and compatible release. \n \n \n Some tasks time out or run out of memory \n Use their logs and accounting to adjust resources; do not mark them successful. \n \n \n Existing published result is invalid \n Stop its tasks, preserve the directory in quarantine, investigate, then retry. \n \n \n Array rejected \n Check index/task/submission limits; use bounded windows with explicit offsets. \n \n \n All jobs completed, but the status tool reports missing inputs \n Check failed/retried tasks and manifest indices; do not merge until complete. \n \n \n \n If an instruction does not work,  open an issue  with the command, error, client/release versions, node hostname, and relevant task or rule ID. Omit proxy contents, private keys, and tokens. \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "running-on-datasets/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Rucio:  replication rules ,  current CLI examples ,  DID client , and  replica client . \n Rucio CLI definitions:  rules  and  replicas . \n Unity:  batch jobs  and  storage . \n Slurm:  job arrays  and  sbatch . \n ROOT:  RDataFrame  and  TFileMerger . \n \n Next:   Using Pixi  covers personal tools and project package environments. \n"}, {"title": "Using Pixi", "url": "using-pixi/index.html", "text": "Pixi installs software in your own directories and runs commands with the dependencies supplied by a project. No administrator privileges are needed. This chapter covers installing Pixi, managing personal command-line tools, and using an  existing  Pixi project. \n Complete the  environment setup , including your work/project directories and  .bashrc  configuration , first. The examples use your saved  ATLAS_WORKDIR , derived from  USER_PI  and Unity  $USER , and the allocation/container helpers. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Use \n Command \n Where it applies \n \n \n \n \n Personal command-line tools \n pixi global \n Tools available to your account from any directory. \n \n \n A project's supplied dependencies and commands \n pixi install  and  pixi run \n The project identified by its Pixi manifest. \n \n \n \n 1. Install Pixi in your user area \n For the EL9 workflows in this guide, use a  fresh container before running  asetup . Independent Pixi projects can run directly on Unity's Ubuntu hosts if they support that environment. Install and resolve packages in the operating-system environment where you intend to run them. To use EL9, start from the Unity login node: \n unity_cpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu compute node:\n# If your settings or helpers are missing, run source ~/.bashrc here.\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n In your chosen compute-node environment (EL9 for the sequence above), run the official installer  once : \n curl -fsSL https://pixi.sh/install.sh | PIXI_NO_PATH_UPDATE=1 bash\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\npixi --version \n The default executable is  ~/.pixi/bin/pixi .  PIXI_NO_PATH_UPDATE=1  leaves shell-file editing to you. Ensure your existing Unity  ~/.bashrc  contains the following line  once ; it is also included in the environment chapter's  complete shell snippet : \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\" \n The export in the installation command makes Pixi available immediately. Future shells load the saved setting. If a new container resets  PATH , run that export inside it, or invoke  \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\"  directly. Check that the container exposes the same persistent home directory; a new allocation should not require another installation. \n To update the Pixi executable later, use  pixi self-update . This is separate from updating the packages it manages. See the  official installation instructions  for alternative locations and platforms. \n 2. Manage personal tools with pixi global \n Here,  global means available throughout your own account , not installed for everyone on Unity. Pixi normally creates a separate environment for each requested tool and exposes its executable through  ~/.pixi/bin . \n For example, install the text-search tool ripgrep from conda-forge: \n pixi global install --channel conda-forge ripgrep\nrg --version \n The package is named  ripgrep , while its command is  rg . You can now run  rg  from any directory without  pixi run  or an activation step. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Task \n Command \n \n \n \n \n List your global tools/environments \n pixi global list \n \n \n Update the installed ripgrep environment \n pixi global update ripgrep \n \n \n Remove that environment and its exposed command \n pixi global uninstall ripgrep \n \n \n \n Use global installation for utilities you want across projects. Installing a global tool does not add it to a project's manifest. The  global-tools guide  covers additional options. \n 3. Install an existing project's dependencies \n A Pixi-enabled repository supplies a  manifest , usually  pixi.toml  or a  pyproject.toml  containing Pixi configuration. It often also supplies  pixi.lock , which records the resolved package versions. You do not need to create or edit either file just to run that project. \n Place the repository in your work area. For a project already checked out under  PROJECT_NAME , run: \n # In the runtime required by the project; replace PROJECT_NAME with its directory.\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\npixi install \n pixi install  prepares the default environment, normally under the project's  .pixi/envs/  directory. It uses an up-to-date lock file; if the lock is missing or needs updating, it can resolve dependencies and write the lock. Downloads and environments consume your storage quota, so keep substantial project environments in your work area. \n When the project provides a lock file and you want to require it unchanged, use: \n pixi install --locked \n --locked  fails if the lock is missing or inconsistent with the manifest. Follow the project's instructions to resolve that situation. Keep the manifest and lock together when sharing a project; the installed  .pixi  directory can be recreated from them. \n 4. Run the project's code \n Prefix a command with  pixi run  to execute it in the project's environment. For a repository whose manifest includes Python and which supplies an  analysis.py  script: \n # In the project's directory, after pixi install:\npixi run python -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)'\npixi run python analysis.py \n Replace  analysis.py  with the command documented by your project. The printed Python path should identify the project's environment. No shell activation is required, and your parent shell remains unchanged when the command finishes.  pixi run  can install missing dependencies automatically, but an explicit  pixi install  makes the setup step clear. \n Projects can also provide named tasks. List them with  pixi task list ; if the project defines a task named  test , run it with  pixi run test . \n For a run using the supplied lock: \n pixi run --locked python analysis.py \n To select a manifest explicitly, use  --manifest-path  with either command: \n pixi install --manifest-path \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME/pixi.toml\"\npixi run --manifest-path \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME/pixi.toml\" python --version \n The manifest selects the environment; follow the project's working-directory instructions for code that opens relative paths. Keep builds, event processing, and training inside your Slurm allocation. Pixi does not allocate CPUs, GPUs, or additional wall time. \n For notebooks,  Using JupyterLab  shows how to launch the server from this environment and connect from your browser through SSH. \n 5. Use Pixi alongside ATLAS \n Continue to use  asetup  for an ATLAS release and its Python/ROOT stack. Use Pixi for projects and supporting tools that supply their own dependencies. Installing another Python or ROOT globally can change which executable your shell finds; use project environments for those packages. \n Separate package environments do not automatically remove every inherited variable. An existing ATLAS  PYTHONPATH  or  LD_LIBRARY_PATH  can interfere with an independent Pixi program. Start those workflows in a fresh shell or container before  asetup , unless the project documents a combined setup. \n The  Codex Agents chapter  provides such a combined workflow: Codex inherits the ATLAS release, while a launcher runs the Rucio/AMI clients in a separate Pixi environment with an explicitly cleaned environment. It reuses the Pixi installation from this chapter. \n Learn how to create and modify environments \n For a guided introduction to creating a Pixi project, adding dependencies, and maintaining its environment, follow  The Carpentries: Introduction to Pixi . This manual focuses on installing tools and running environments supplied by an analysis or service. \n References \n \n Pixi:  installation ,  global tools ,  pixi install ,  pixi run , and  lock files . \n \n Next:   Using VS Code  covers interactive editing and ATLAS Python execution on Unity. \n"}, {"title": "1. Install Pixi in your user area", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#installation", "text": "1. Install Pixi in your user area \n For the EL9 workflows in this guide, use a  fresh container before running  asetup . Independent Pixi projects can run directly on Unity's Ubuntu hosts if they support that environment. Install and resolve packages in the operating-system environment where you intend to run them. To use EL9, start from the Unity login node: \n unity_cpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu compute node:\n# If your settings or helpers are missing, run source ~/.bashrc here.\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n In your chosen compute-node environment (EL9 for the sequence above), run the official installer  once : \n curl -fsSL https://pixi.sh/install.sh | PIXI_NO_PATH_UPDATE=1 bash\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\npixi --version \n The default executable is  ~/.pixi/bin/pixi .  PIXI_NO_PATH_UPDATE=1  leaves shell-file editing to you. Ensure your existing Unity  ~/.bashrc  contains the following line  once ; it is also included in the environment chapter's  complete shell snippet : \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\" \n The export in the installation command makes Pixi available immediately. Future shells load the saved setting. If a new container resets  PATH , run that export inside it, or invoke  \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\"  directly. Check that the container exposes the same persistent home directory; a new allocation should not require another installation. \n To update the Pixi executable later, use  pixi self-update . This is separate from updating the packages it manages. See the  official installation instructions  for alternative locations and platforms. \n"}, {"title": "2. Manage personal tools with pixi global", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#global-tools", "text": "2. Manage personal tools with pixi global \n Here,  global means available throughout your own account , not installed for everyone on Unity. Pixi normally creates a separate environment for each requested tool and exposes its executable through  ~/.pixi/bin . \n For example, install the text-search tool ripgrep from conda-forge: \n pixi global install --channel conda-forge ripgrep\nrg --version \n The package is named  ripgrep , while its command is  rg . You can now run  rg  from any directory without  pixi run  or an activation step. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Task \n Command \n \n \n \n \n List your global tools/environments \n pixi global list \n \n \n Update the installed ripgrep environment \n pixi global update ripgrep \n \n \n Remove that environment and its exposed command \n pixi global uninstall ripgrep \n \n \n \n Use global installation for utilities you want across projects. Installing a global tool does not add it to a project's manifest. The  global-tools guide  covers additional options. \n"}, {"title": "3. Install an existing project's dependencies", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#projects", "text": "3. Install an existing project's dependencies \n A Pixi-enabled repository supplies a  manifest , usually  pixi.toml  or a  pyproject.toml  containing Pixi configuration. It often also supplies  pixi.lock , which records the resolved package versions. You do not need to create or edit either file just to run that project. \n Place the repository in your work area. For a project already checked out under  PROJECT_NAME , run: \n # In the runtime required by the project; replace PROJECT_NAME with its directory.\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\npixi install \n pixi install  prepares the default environment, normally under the project's  .pixi/envs/  directory. It uses an up-to-date lock file; if the lock is missing or needs updating, it can resolve dependencies and write the lock. Downloads and environments consume your storage quota, so keep substantial project environments in your work area. \n When the project provides a lock file and you want to require it unchanged, use: \n pixi install --locked \n --locked  fails if the lock is missing or inconsistent with the manifest. Follow the project's instructions to resolve that situation. Keep the manifest and lock together when sharing a project; the installed  .pixi  directory can be recreated from them. \n"}, {"title": "4. Run the project's code", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#run", "text": "4. Run the project's code \n Prefix a command with  pixi run  to execute it in the project's environment. For a repository whose manifest includes Python and which supplies an  analysis.py  script: \n # In the project's directory, after pixi install:\npixi run python -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)'\npixi run python analysis.py \n Replace  analysis.py  with the command documented by your project. The printed Python path should identify the project's environment. No shell activation is required, and your parent shell remains unchanged when the command finishes.  pixi run  can install missing dependencies automatically, but an explicit  pixi install  makes the setup step clear. \n Projects can also provide named tasks. List them with  pixi task list ; if the project defines a task named  test , run it with  pixi run test . \n For a run using the supplied lock: \n pixi run --locked python analysis.py \n To select a manifest explicitly, use  --manifest-path  with either command: \n pixi install --manifest-path \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME/pixi.toml\"\npixi run --manifest-path \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME/pixi.toml\" python --version \n The manifest selects the environment; follow the project's working-directory instructions for code that opens relative paths. Keep builds, event processing, and training inside your Slurm allocation. Pixi does not allocate CPUs, GPUs, or additional wall time. \n For notebooks,  Using JupyterLab  shows how to launch the server from this environment and connect from your browser through SSH. \n"}, {"title": "5. Use Pixi alongside ATLAS", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#atlas", "text": "5. Use Pixi alongside ATLAS \n Continue to use  asetup  for an ATLAS release and its Python/ROOT stack. Use Pixi for projects and supporting tools that supply their own dependencies. Installing another Python or ROOT globally can change which executable your shell finds; use project environments for those packages. \n Separate package environments do not automatically remove every inherited variable. An existing ATLAS  PYTHONPATH  or  LD_LIBRARY_PATH  can interfere with an independent Pixi program. Start those workflows in a fresh shell or container before  asetup , unless the project documents a combined setup. \n The  Codex Agents chapter  provides such a combined workflow: Codex inherits the ATLAS release, while a launcher runs the Rucio/AMI clients in a separate Pixi environment with an explicitly cleaned environment. It reuses the Pixi installation from this chapter. \n"}, {"title": "Learn how to create and modify environments", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#learn-more", "text": "Learn how to create and modify environments \n For a guided introduction to creating a Pixi project, adding dependencies, and maintaining its environment, follow  The Carpentries: Introduction to Pixi . This manual focuses on installing tools and running environments supplied by an analysis or service. \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "using-pixi/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Pixi:  installation ,  global tools ,  pixi install ,  pixi run , and  lock files . \n \n Next:   Using VS Code  covers interactive editing and ATLAS Python execution on Unity. \n"}, {"title": "Using VS Code", "url": "using-vscode/index.html", "text": "VS Code can edit files on Unity and run Python with ATLAS software inside EL9. The key is  where the remote VS Code backend starts : opening an EL9 shell in an Ubuntu VS Code terminal changes that terminal, but does not move the Python extension, debugger, or notebook kernel into the container. \n This chapter includes the Unity connection steps and three ways to work. For integrated ATLAS Python execution, start with the  container tunnel  method; it starts VS Code from the environment you have already prepared. \n Choose a connection method \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Method \n Where the VS Code backend runs \n ATLAS Python integration \n \n \n \n \n A. Ordinary Remote SSH \n Ubuntu on your allocated compute node \n Edit files and run ATLAS commands in a container terminal. Run/debug buttons need a container backend; a separate remote Jupyter server is another option for notebooks. \n \n \n B. Remote SSH into EL9 \n EL9, entered by an SSH startup command \n Python execution and debugging in the container. Uses Unity's SSH route, with an additional launcher. Start with a CPU allocation. \n \n \n C. Remote Tunnels inside EL9 \n EL9, launched from your configured ATLAS shell \n Python execution and debugging in the container. Preferred for GPUs because it starts directly in the allocated GPU shell. Requires access to Microsoft's tunnel service. \n \n \n \n All three methods use a  Slurm compute allocation . A remote connection does not reserve resources. Do not run analysis or a persistent analysis backend on a login node. \n Configure your local computer \n Install  VS Code  and Microsoft's  Remote – SSH  extension. You need a Unity account and an SSH key registered with Unity, as for a normal terminal connection. The following assumes that  ssh unity  will use that key without an interactive password prompt. \n Edit your  local  SSH configuration:  ~/.ssh/config  on Linux/macOS or  %USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\config  on Windows. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  and the key path with your Unity username and actual private key. Use the same username/key for both entries. \n Host unity\n    HostName login.unityhpc.org\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost *.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity \n ProxyJump  reaches the compute node through Unity's login service. Test the login connection in a local terminal: \n ssh unity \n If Windows OpenSSH reports  Corrupted MAC on input , Unity documents adding  MACs hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com  to the affected SSH host entries. Apply that workaround only for that error. \n Complete the  one-time directory setup  and  Bash configuration  first. This chapter assumes  USER_PI ,  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" , and the  unity_cpu ,  unity_gpu ,  setupATLAS , and  atlas_gpu  helpers are already defined. In a Unity terminal,  $USER  is your Unity username; your local SSH configuration still needs that literal username.  Do not automatically run  salloc , enter a container, or select an ATLAS release every time  .bashrc  is read.  SSH and VS Code also start noninteractive shells; keep these actions in commands you call explicitly. \n Request a compute session \n In the Unity login shell, request a single compute node. For example, for CPU work: \n unity_cpu \n Or, for a GPU session, use this allocation instead: \n unity_gpu \n Wait for the allocation. Unity's interactive setup puts you in a shell on the allocated node. If that shell has not loaded your configuration, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. Record the hostname and job ID, then enter your existing analysis checkout under the work area configured in  .bashrc . Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name throughout this chapter: \n hostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nprintf 'Project folder: %s\\n' \"$(pwd -P)\" \n Keep this allocation shell open. To protect it from a local network interruption, follow the  environment chapter's  tmux  instructions , starting  tmux  on the login node before requesting the allocation. It does not extend the job's time limit. \n The examples below use  job  123456  and  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu  as placeholders. Substitute the values from your allocation. Refresh them every time you start a new job. \n A. Ordinary Remote SSH \n \n In local VS Code, open the Command Palette and run  Remote-SSH: Connect to Host… . \n Enter the full hostname of your  allocated compute node , for example  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu . Select  Linux  if prompted. \n In the remote window, use  File → Open Folder…  and paste the expanded  Project folder  path printed above. The status bar should identify the compute-node SSH connection. \n Open an integrated terminal and check  hostname -f . To obtain an explicit shell in the existing job, use Unity's documented command with your job ID: \n \n srun --overlap --pty --jobid=123456 /bin/bash \n For a self-contained Pixi project that runs on Ubuntu, follow  Using Pixi  to install its environment in this allocated shell. Install Microsoft's  Python  extension on the remote target and use  Python: Select Interpreter  to select the project's Pixi Python. If it is not detected, enter the path printed by  pixi run python -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)'  from the project directory. See  Pixi's VS Code integration  for details. The ATLAS release workflow below uses the Python selected by  asetup  instead. \n Enter EL9 and select ATLAS software \n For a CPU session, run in the allocated Ubuntu shell: \n module load apptainer/latest\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For a GPU session, use the helper  instead . It loads Apptainer, enables  --nv , and forwards Slurm's device selection: \n atlas_gpu \n These helpers are defined in the environment chapter. Unity normally exposes  /work  through ALRB, so an additional  -m /work  is not needed for the standard workflow. Use the  mount guidance  if a required filesystem is missing. \n Now,  inside EL9 , select the exact release used by your analysis: \n cat /etc/os-release\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nasetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Enter the same exact  project,version  selected in  Load analysis software . If you have a compiled analysis package, also source its generated setup script after  asetup , for example  source /absolute/path/to/build/PLATFORM/setup.sh . On a GPU allocation, check  nvidia-smi ; if the utility is not on  PATH , try  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi , as described in the  GPU troubleshooting notes . \n You can now run analysis commands in this terminal.  The VS Code backend is still on Ubuntu.  Selecting a Python binary on CVMFS does not by itself provide EL9 or the environment established by  asetup . Continue with B or C for integrated execution. \n B. Remote SSH directly into EL9 \n ATLAS documents using SSH  RemoteCommand  to enter a container  before VS Code starts its backend . The adaptation below selects your ATLAS release at that stage too. No SSH daemon inside the container is required. \n Use a  CPU allocation , with only one running job of yours on that node. Unity's SSH service must place the session in the allocation; the launcher's job check detects stale or ambiguous configuration but does not replace that site-level behavior. For GPU work, use method C: a new SSH session may have a different Slurm step/device view from the original GPU shell. \n Install the launchers \n In your Unity Ubuntu shell, download both companion scripts to the same directory: \n mkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/bin\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/vscode/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh \\\n  --output \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/vscode/atlas-vscode-shell.sh \\\n  --output \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-shell.sh\"\nchmod u+x \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh\" \n The  host launcher  loads Apptainer and explicitly binds CVMFS,  /work , and your home directory. The  inside-container launcher  sets up ATLAS and then passes SSH's input stream to Bash for VS Code's bootstrap. Setup output goes to stderr; setup commands cannot consume that input stream. \n Save this allocation's settings \n First run the normal EL9 setup above once and record the  resolved EL9 container image path printed by ALRB . Use that actual path, which may be a container directory on CVMFS, rather than guessing a container tag. Exit the container back to the  original CPU allocation's Ubuntu shell , keeping the allocation alive. \n Fill in the image below and enter the same  project,version  again in the host shell: variables set inside the container do not propagate back to it. Set  ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP  to your absolute generated build setup path if needed, or leave it empty. This writes a private, allocation-specific configuration file: \n ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE='/cvmfs/REPLACE_WITH_THE_RESOLVED_EL9_IMAGE_PATH'\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE=\"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP=''\nATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/vscode-job-${SLURM_JOB_ID}.sh\"\n(\n    umask 077\n    {\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_JOB_ID=%q\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_NODE=%q\\n' \"$(hostname -s)\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_CPUS=%q\\n' \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_USER_PI=%q\\n' \"$USER_PI\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_USER=%q\\n' \"$USER\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_WORKDIR=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP\"\n    } > \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG\"\n)\nprintf 'Configuration: %s\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG\"\nprintf 'VS Code server directory: %s\\n' \\\n  \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/.vscode-el9-ssh/job-${SLURM_JOB_ID}\" \n The configuration records the expanded work path and your  USER_PI / USER  values. The launcher forwards them into the clean container environment without depending on an interactive  .bashrc  being read during SSH startup. \n Connect through a dedicated SSH alias \n Add this entry to your  local  SSH configuration. Replace the hostname and username with your allocation details, and replace  PASTE_ALLOCATION_CONFIG_PATH  with the complete  Configuration  path printed above. Local SSH configuration does not expand Unity's  $USER_PI  or  $USER ; paste the expanded remote path. \n Host unity-atlas-el9\n    HostName cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity\n    RemoteCommand ~/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh PASTE_ALLOCATION_CONFIG_PATH\n    RequestTTY yes \n Use paths without spaces in this SSH entry. Keep  RemoteCommand  on this dedicated alias, so normal Unity SSH access is unaffected. Do not put  srun --pty  in  RemoteCommand : VS Code needs its bootstrap input intact, and the backend must survive the bootstrap shell exiting. \n In  local VS Code User Settings (JSON) , merge these settings with your existing configuration. Replace  PASTE_SERVER_INSTALL_PATH  with the complete  VS Code server directory  printed above; this JSON value must be a literal remote path, not an expression containing  $USER_PI  or  $USER : \n {\n  \"remote.SSH.enableRemoteCommand\": true,\n  \"remote.SSH.remoteServerListenOnSocket\": false,\n  \"remote.SSH.lockfilesInTmp\": true,\n  \"remote.SSH.serverInstallPath\": {\n    \"unity-atlas-el9\": \"PASTE_SERVER_INSTALL_PATH\"\n  }\n} \n The separate server directory prevents reuse of the Ubuntu backend or an earlier allocation's environment. Refresh its job ID with the SSH entry. ATLAS notes that  RemoteCommand  support is experimental; the socket setting can be necessary for it. \n First test the alias from a local terminal with  ssh unity-atlas-el9 . At its prompt, check  /etc/os-release  and run the Python/ROOT check above, then  exit . Next run  Remote-SSH: Connect to Host… → unity-atlas-el9 , open your project folder, and follow the  integrated Python checks . If VS Code bootstrap or reconnection fails, inspect  Output → Remote - SSH  and try method C. \n C. Start a VS Code tunnel inside EL9 \n This method starts Microsoft's standalone CLI  inside the already configured ATLAS environment , so its remote backend and extensions run in EL9. It avoids the SSH startup wrapper. The desktop editor connects using  Remote – Tunnels ; a browser client is also available. \n Use the  original compute shell created by your allocation , especially for a GPU job. Follow  Enter EL9 and select ATLAS software , including  --nv  for GPUs,  asetup , and any local build setup. Confirm the Python/ROOT check succeeds before continuing. \n Inside that configured EL9 shell, install the standalone CLI in your writable work directory. The download below is for  x86-64  nodes: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nVSCODE_ROOT=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/.vscode-atlas\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$VSCODE_ROOT\" \"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  'https://code.visualstudio.com/sha/download?build=stable&os=cli-alpine-x64' \\\n  --output \"$VSCODE_ROOT/vscode_cli.tar.gz\"\ntar -xf \"$VSCODE_ROOT/vscode_cli.tar.gz\" -C \"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin\"\n\n# Save this interpreter path for the VS Code selection step.\npython -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)' \n Install once; on later sessions reuse the CLI and redefine  VSCODE_ROOT . Start the tunnel in the foreground, replacing  123456  with your current job ID: \n VSCODE_SESSION=\"$VSCODE_ROOT/sessions/job-123456\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$VSCODE_SESSION\"\n\"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin/code\" \\\n  --cli-data-dir \"$VSCODE_SESSION/cli\" \\\n  tunnel --server-data-dir \"$VSCODE_SESSION/server\" \\\n  --name unity-atlas-123456 \n Use the  explicit standalone CLI path . A  code  command supplied by an existing Remote SSH terminal may instead be a shim for that existing session. Keep both state directories separate from other sessions: the CLI cache can otherwise reuse an already running server. Use a new session directory and tunnel name for simultaneous environments or a changed ATLAS release. \n \n Follow the CLI's device-login instructions and review the VS Code Server license prompt. Sign in with the GitHub or Microsoft identity you will use in your editor. \n In local VS Code, install Microsoft's  Remote – Tunnels  extension. \n Run  Remote-Tunnels: Connect to Tunnel… , sign in with the same identity, and select  unity-atlas-123456 . \n Open the expanded project-folder path printed in the allocation step and continue below. Alternatively, open the  vscode.dev  URL printed by the CLI to use the browser editor. \n \n The compute node needs outbound access to Microsoft's tunnel service; if that service is unavailable from Unity, use the SSH method. Keep the allocation and foreground tunnel running. Stop the tunnel with  Ctrl+C  when finished. Do not install a persistent tunnel service on a compute node. \n Configure and test integrated Python \n These steps apply to methods B and C. In the connected window, install Microsoft's  Python ,  Python Debugger , and  Pylance  extensions  on the remote target  when prompted. Installing only the local extensions is insufficient. \n In a new remote integrated terminal, without entering another container, check: \n cat /etc/os-release\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Run  Python: Select Interpreter → Enter interpreter path…  and select the full path printed by  sys.executable . The selected interpreter needs the ATLAS environment as well as EL9. If terminal startup files overwrite  PATH ,  PYTHONPATH , or library paths, they can also override the backend's inherited environment; correct those settings and restart the backend. \n For an Athena development build, ATLAS also documents generated  ide_python  and  env.txt  settings for its Python tools. Those are build-specific additions; do not assume every AnalysisBase installation provides them. \n Run and debug a small analysis check \n Create  check_atlas.py  in your project, or  download this example : \n \"\"\"Run with VS Code's Run button, debugger, and notebook kernel.\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nprint(Path(\"/etc/os-release\").read_text())\nprint(\"Python:\", sys.executable)\nprint(\"ATLAS project:\", os.environ.get(\"AtlasProject\"))\nprint(\"ATLAS version:\", os.environ.get(\"AtlasVersion\"))\nprint(\"ROOT:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:\", os.environ.get(\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"))\nhistogram = ROOT.TH1D(\"check\", \"VS Code ATLAS check\", 10, 0, 10)\nhistogram.Fill(4.0)  # Put a breakpoint here.\nprint(\"Entries:\", histogram.GetEntries())\nassert histogram.GetEntries() == 1 \n Use  Run Python File in Terminal  from the editor's Run button. Then put a breakpoint on  histogram.Fill(4.0)  and run  Python Debugger: Debug Python File , or press  F5  and choose the Python debugger. Confirm that execution stops at the breakpoint and finishes with one histogram entry. Also test an import from your own analysis package if you have one. \n For a reusable F5 configuration, save this as  .vscode/launch.json  in the project ( download ): \n {\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ATLAS: current Python file\",\n      \"type\": \"debugpy\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${file}\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\",\n      \"justMyCode\": false\n    }\n  ]\n} \n This tests Python debugging. Stepping into compiled C++ libraries requires a separate C++ debugger configuration and suitable debug symbols. \n Notebooks and the Python Interactive window \n Install  Jupyter  and  Jupyter Notebook Renderers  on the remote target. Open a notebook and use the top-right  Select Kernel  menu to choose the same ATLAS Python environment. Kernel selection is separate from  Python: Select Interpreter . For a  .py  file with  # %%  cells, select the corresponding kernel in the Python Interactive window too. \n The chosen Python must have a compatible  ipykernel : \n python -c 'import ipykernel; print(ipykernel.__version__)' \n If that fails, arrange a writable, compatible kernel environment for that release before proceeding; do not install packages into read-only CVMFS or replace the release's ROOT/Python stack indiscriminately. Run the contents of  check_atlas.py  in a notebook cell to confirm that the  kernel , not only a terminal, is using EL9 and ATLAS. \n Alternative: connect only the notebook kernel \n For a browser interface with a project-managed Pixi environment, follow the complete  Using JupyterLab  workflow. The instructions below connect a local VS Code notebook to an ATLAS-configured Jupyter server. \n If you only need notebooks, a  local VS Code window  can connect to a  Jupyter server started inside EL9 . Allocate a compute node and enter the configured container as above. This requires a working  jupyter  and  ipykernel  in that environment. After  asetup , start: \n jupyter lab --no-browser --ip=127.0.0.1 --port=8888 \\\n  --ServerApp.port_retries=0 \n Keep its authentication enabled. In a  local terminal , forward that port through the allocated node, using the actual hostname: \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8888:127.0.0.1:8888 cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu \n The fixed-port and loopback settings match  Using JupyterLab , which also explains choosing another port and reconnecting. If port 8888 is occupied on the node, choose another server port and update the tunnel's remote port. \n In a  local VS Code window , install Microsoft's  Jupyter  extension locally and open your notebook. Choose  Select Kernel → Select Another Kernel… → Existing Jupyter Server , and enter the authenticated  http://127.0.0.1:8888/...  URL printed by Jupyter, including its token. Select the ATLAS kernel and run the Python/ROOT check above in a cell. The notebook file is opened and saved locally; code executes remotely, and input/output paths in that code refer to Unity storage. Keep both the Jupyter process and SSH forward alive. This provides notebook/cell execution, but does not move ordinary Python Run/F5 or language services into EL9. \n End a session and troubleshoot \n Close the remote VS Code connection when finished, stop any tunnel/Jupyter process, and release the allocation by exiting its original shell or using  scancel 123456  with the correct job ID. Closing VS Code alone does not necessarily cancel the allocation. \n When changing ATLAS releases, stop the previous backend and reconnect after setting up the new environment. For SSH,  Remote-SSH: Kill VS Code Server on Host…  is available; refresh the allocation configuration, hostname, and dedicated server directory as needed. For tunnels, stop the old foreground process and use a fresh session directory/name. Running  asetup  only in an already open terminal does not reconfigure the other VS Code processes. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n Check \n \n \n \n \n Compute-node SSH is rejected \n The allocation must still be running, and the hostname, username, key, and  ProxyJump  must match it. \n \n \n Terminal is EL9 but Run/F5 cannot import ROOT \n Method A changes only the terminal. With B/C, check the selected interpreter and whether an older backend or startup file replaced the environment. \n \n \n RemoteCommand bootstrap hangs or fails \n Test the SSH alias in a terminal, inspect Remote - SSH output, and check that startup files print no extra stdout. Use the supplied stdin-preserving launcher. \n \n \n Launcher rejects the saved job \n Recreate the configuration for the current node/job; the CPU launcher expects exactly one running job of yours on that node. \n \n \n Reconnect reaches an old environment \n Stop the old backend and use the allocation-specific server/state paths. \n \n \n Notebook import fails while Run works \n Select the notebook kernel separately; check its Python path and compatible  ipykernel . \n \n \n Tunnel cannot sign in or connect \n Check the device-login identity, outbound service access, and that the allocation and foreground CLI are still alive. \n \n \n GPU command or framework fails \n Use  --nv  and the original allocated GPU shell. Confirm Slurm's device selection with the intended framework;  nvidia-smi  alone does not validate framework compatibility or assigned-device mapping. \n \n \n \n References \n \n Unity:  SSH configuration ,  VS Code ,  interactive jobs , and  FAQ . \n ATLAS:  VS Code remote usage with SSH  and  Python in VS Code . \n Microsoft:  Remote SSH ,  Remote Tunnels ,  Python environments ,  Python debugging , and  Jupyter kernel management . \n Microsoft implementation details:  CLI arguments ,  CLI state/cache , and  server startup/reuse . \n DCCN:  VS Code on an HPC cluster , including a tunnel launched inside a Singularity container. \n Slurm:  SSH adoption into a job  and  GPU resource management . \n \n Next:   Using JupyterLab  runs notebooks from your own Pixi environment through a browser. \n"}, {"title": "Choose a connection method", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#choose-a-connection-method", "text": "Choose a connection method \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Method \n Where the VS Code backend runs \n ATLAS Python integration \n \n \n \n \n A. Ordinary Remote SSH \n Ubuntu on your allocated compute node \n Edit files and run ATLAS commands in a container terminal. Run/debug buttons need a container backend; a separate remote Jupyter server is another option for notebooks. \n \n \n B. Remote SSH into EL9 \n EL9, entered by an SSH startup command \n Python execution and debugging in the container. Uses Unity's SSH route, with an additional launcher. Start with a CPU allocation. \n \n \n C. Remote Tunnels inside EL9 \n EL9, launched from your configured ATLAS shell \n Python execution and debugging in the container. Preferred for GPUs because it starts directly in the allocated GPU shell. Requires access to Microsoft's tunnel service. \n \n \n \n All three methods use a  Slurm compute allocation . A remote connection does not reserve resources. Do not run analysis or a persistent analysis backend on a login node. \n"}, {"title": "Configure your local computer", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#configure-your-local-computer", "text": "Configure your local computer \n Install  VS Code  and Microsoft's  Remote – SSH  extension. You need a Unity account and an SSH key registered with Unity, as for a normal terminal connection. The following assumes that  ssh unity  will use that key without an interactive password prompt. \n Edit your  local  SSH configuration:  ~/.ssh/config  on Linux/macOS or  %USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\config  on Windows. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  and the key path with your Unity username and actual private key. Use the same username/key for both entries. \n Host unity\n    HostName login.unityhpc.org\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost *.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity \n ProxyJump  reaches the compute node through Unity's login service. Test the login connection in a local terminal: \n ssh unity \n If Windows OpenSSH reports  Corrupted MAC on input , Unity documents adding  MACs hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com  to the affected SSH host entries. Apply that workaround only for that error. \n Complete the  one-time directory setup  and  Bash configuration  first. This chapter assumes  USER_PI ,  ATLAS_WORKDIR=\"/work/${USER_PI}/${USER}/atlas\" , and the  unity_cpu ,  unity_gpu ,  setupATLAS , and  atlas_gpu  helpers are already defined. In a Unity terminal,  $USER  is your Unity username; your local SSH configuration still needs that literal username.  Do not automatically run  salloc , enter a container, or select an ATLAS release every time  .bashrc  is read.  SSH and VS Code also start noninteractive shells; keep these actions in commands you call explicitly. \n"}, {"title": "Request a compute session", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#allocation", "text": "Request a compute session \n In the Unity login shell, request a single compute node. For example, for CPU work: \n unity_cpu \n Or, for a GPU session, use this allocation instead: \n unity_gpu \n Wait for the allocation. Unity's interactive setup puts you in a shell on the allocated node. If that shell has not loaded your configuration, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. Record the hostname and job ID, then enter your existing analysis checkout under the work area configured in  .bashrc . Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name throughout this chapter: \n hostname -f\nprintf 'Job: %s\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\"\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nprintf 'Project folder: %s\\n' \"$(pwd -P)\" \n Keep this allocation shell open. To protect it from a local network interruption, follow the  environment chapter's  tmux  instructions , starting  tmux  on the login node before requesting the allocation. It does not extend the job's time limit. \n The examples below use  job  123456  and  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu  as placeholders. Substitute the values from your allocation. Refresh them every time you start a new job. \n"}, {"title": "A. Ordinary Remote SSH", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#ordinary-ssh", "text": "A. Ordinary Remote SSH \n \n In local VS Code, open the Command Palette and run  Remote-SSH: Connect to Host… . \n Enter the full hostname of your  allocated compute node , for example  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu . Select  Linux  if prompted. \n In the remote window, use  File → Open Folder…  and paste the expanded  Project folder  path printed above. The status bar should identify the compute-node SSH connection. \n Open an integrated terminal and check  hostname -f . To obtain an explicit shell in the existing job, use Unity's documented command with your job ID: \n \n srun --overlap --pty --jobid=123456 /bin/bash \n For a self-contained Pixi project that runs on Ubuntu, follow  Using Pixi  to install its environment in this allocated shell. Install Microsoft's  Python  extension on the remote target and use  Python: Select Interpreter  to select the project's Pixi Python. If it is not detected, enter the path printed by  pixi run python -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)'  from the project directory. See  Pixi's VS Code integration  for details. The ATLAS release workflow below uses the Python selected by  asetup  instead. \n Enter EL9 and select ATLAS software \n For a CPU session, run in the allocated Ubuntu shell: \n module load apptainer/latest\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For a GPU session, use the helper  instead . It loads Apptainer, enables  --nv , and forwards Slurm's device selection: \n atlas_gpu \n These helpers are defined in the environment chapter. Unity normally exposes  /work  through ALRB, so an additional  -m /work  is not needed for the standard workflow. Use the  mount guidance  if a required filesystem is missing. \n Now,  inside EL9 , select the exact release used by your analysis: \n cat /etc/os-release\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nasetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Enter the same exact  project,version  selected in  Load analysis software . If you have a compiled analysis package, also source its generated setup script after  asetup , for example  source /absolute/path/to/build/PLATFORM/setup.sh . On a GPU allocation, check  nvidia-smi ; if the utility is not on  PATH , try  /usr/bin/nvidia-smi , as described in the  GPU troubleshooting notes . \n You can now run analysis commands in this terminal.  The VS Code backend is still on Ubuntu.  Selecting a Python binary on CVMFS does not by itself provide EL9 or the environment established by  asetup . Continue with B or C for integrated execution. \n"}, {"title": "B. Remote SSH directly into EL9", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#container-ssh", "text": "B. Remote SSH directly into EL9 \n ATLAS documents using SSH  RemoteCommand  to enter a container  before VS Code starts its backend . The adaptation below selects your ATLAS release at that stage too. No SSH daemon inside the container is required. \n Use a  CPU allocation , with only one running job of yours on that node. Unity's SSH service must place the session in the allocation; the launcher's job check detects stale or ambiguous configuration but does not replace that site-level behavior. For GPU work, use method C: a new SSH session may have a different Slurm step/device view from the original GPU shell. \n Install the launchers \n In your Unity Ubuntu shell, download both companion scripts to the same directory: \n mkdir -p \"$HOME/.local/bin\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/vscode/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh \\\n  --output \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/vscode/atlas-vscode-shell.sh \\\n  --output \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-shell.sh\"\nchmod u+x \"$HOME/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh\" \n The  host launcher  loads Apptainer and explicitly binds CVMFS,  /work , and your home directory. The  inside-container launcher  sets up ATLAS and then passes SSH's input stream to Bash for VS Code's bootstrap. Setup output goes to stderr; setup commands cannot consume that input stream. \n Save this allocation's settings \n First run the normal EL9 setup above once and record the  resolved EL9 container image path printed by ALRB . Use that actual path, which may be a container directory on CVMFS, rather than guessing a container tag. Exit the container back to the  original CPU allocation's Ubuntu shell , keeping the allocation alive. \n Fill in the image below and enter the same  project,version  again in the host shell: variables set inside the container do not propagate back to it. Set  ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP  to your absolute generated build setup path if needed, or leave it empty. This writes a private, allocation-specific configuration file: \n ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE='/cvmfs/REPLACE_WITH_THE_RESOLVED_EL9_IMAGE_PATH'\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE=\"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP=''\nATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/vscode-job-${SLURM_JOB_ID}.sh\"\n(\n    umask 077\n    {\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_JOB_ID=%q\\n' \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_NODE=%q\\n' \"$(hostname -s)\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_CPUS=%q\\n' \"${SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK:-1}\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_USER_PI=%q\\n' \"$USER_PI\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_USER=%q\\n' \"$USER\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_IMAGE\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_RELEASE\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_WORKDIR=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\n        printf 'ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP=%q\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_BUILD_SETUP\"\n    } > \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG\"\n)\nprintf 'Configuration: %s\\n' \"$ATLAS_VSCODE_CONFIG\"\nprintf 'VS Code server directory: %s\\n' \\\n  \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/.vscode-el9-ssh/job-${SLURM_JOB_ID}\" \n The configuration records the expanded work path and your  USER_PI / USER  values. The launcher forwards them into the clean container environment without depending on an interactive  .bashrc  being read during SSH startup. \n Connect through a dedicated SSH alias \n Add this entry to your  local  SSH configuration. Replace the hostname and username with your allocation details, and replace  PASTE_ALLOCATION_CONFIG_PATH  with the complete  Configuration  path printed above. Local SSH configuration does not expand Unity's  $USER_PI  or  $USER ; paste the expanded remote path. \n Host unity-atlas-el9\n    HostName cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity\n    RemoteCommand ~/.local/bin/atlas-vscode-ssh.sh PASTE_ALLOCATION_CONFIG_PATH\n    RequestTTY yes \n Use paths without spaces in this SSH entry. Keep  RemoteCommand  on this dedicated alias, so normal Unity SSH access is unaffected. Do not put  srun --pty  in  RemoteCommand : VS Code needs its bootstrap input intact, and the backend must survive the bootstrap shell exiting. \n In  local VS Code User Settings (JSON) , merge these settings with your existing configuration. Replace  PASTE_SERVER_INSTALL_PATH  with the complete  VS Code server directory  printed above; this JSON value must be a literal remote path, not an expression containing  $USER_PI  or  $USER : \n {\n  \"remote.SSH.enableRemoteCommand\": true,\n  \"remote.SSH.remoteServerListenOnSocket\": false,\n  \"remote.SSH.lockfilesInTmp\": true,\n  \"remote.SSH.serverInstallPath\": {\n    \"unity-atlas-el9\": \"PASTE_SERVER_INSTALL_PATH\"\n  }\n} \n The separate server directory prevents reuse of the Ubuntu backend or an earlier allocation's environment. Refresh its job ID with the SSH entry. ATLAS notes that  RemoteCommand  support is experimental; the socket setting can be necessary for it. \n First test the alias from a local terminal with  ssh unity-atlas-el9 . At its prompt, check  /etc/os-release  and run the Python/ROOT check above, then  exit . Next run  Remote-SSH: Connect to Host… → unity-atlas-el9 , open your project folder, and follow the  integrated Python checks . If VS Code bootstrap or reconnection fails, inspect  Output → Remote - SSH  and try method C. \n"}, {"title": "C. Start a VS Code tunnel inside EL9", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#container-tunnel", "text": "C. Start a VS Code tunnel inside EL9 \n This method starts Microsoft's standalone CLI  inside the already configured ATLAS environment , so its remote backend and extensions run in EL9. It avoids the SSH startup wrapper. The desktop editor connects using  Remote – Tunnels ; a browser client is also available. \n Use the  original compute shell created by your allocation , especially for a GPU job. Follow  Enter EL9 and select ATLAS software , including  --nv  for GPUs,  asetup , and any local build setup. Confirm the Python/ROOT check succeeds before continuing. \n Inside that configured EL9 shell, install the standalone CLI in your writable work directory. The download below is for  x86-64  nodes: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nVSCODE_ROOT=\"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/.vscode-atlas\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$VSCODE_ROOT\" \"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  'https://code.visualstudio.com/sha/download?build=stable&os=cli-alpine-x64' \\\n  --output \"$VSCODE_ROOT/vscode_cli.tar.gz\"\ntar -xf \"$VSCODE_ROOT/vscode_cli.tar.gz\" -C \"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin\"\n\n# Save this interpreter path for the VS Code selection step.\npython -c 'import sys; print(sys.executable)' \n Install once; on later sessions reuse the CLI and redefine  VSCODE_ROOT . Start the tunnel in the foreground, replacing  123456  with your current job ID: \n VSCODE_SESSION=\"$VSCODE_ROOT/sessions/job-123456\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$VSCODE_SESSION\"\n\"$VSCODE_ROOT/cli-bin/code\" \\\n  --cli-data-dir \"$VSCODE_SESSION/cli\" \\\n  tunnel --server-data-dir \"$VSCODE_SESSION/server\" \\\n  --name unity-atlas-123456 \n Use the  explicit standalone CLI path . A  code  command supplied by an existing Remote SSH terminal may instead be a shim for that existing session. Keep both state directories separate from other sessions: the CLI cache can otherwise reuse an already running server. Use a new session directory and tunnel name for simultaneous environments or a changed ATLAS release. \n \n Follow the CLI's device-login instructions and review the VS Code Server license prompt. Sign in with the GitHub or Microsoft identity you will use in your editor. \n In local VS Code, install Microsoft's  Remote – Tunnels  extension. \n Run  Remote-Tunnels: Connect to Tunnel… , sign in with the same identity, and select  unity-atlas-123456 . \n Open the expanded project-folder path printed in the allocation step and continue below. Alternatively, open the  vscode.dev  URL printed by the CLI to use the browser editor. \n \n The compute node needs outbound access to Microsoft's tunnel service; if that service is unavailable from Unity, use the SSH method. Keep the allocation and foreground tunnel running. Stop the tunnel with  Ctrl+C  when finished. Do not install a persistent tunnel service on a compute node. \n"}, {"title": "Configure and test integrated Python", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#integrated-python", "text": "Configure and test integrated Python \n These steps apply to methods B and C. In the connected window, install Microsoft's  Python ,  Python Debugger , and  Pylance  extensions  on the remote target  when prompted. Installing only the local extensions is insufficient. \n In a new remote integrated terminal, without entering another container, check: \n cat /etc/os-release\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())' \n Run  Python: Select Interpreter → Enter interpreter path…  and select the full path printed by  sys.executable . The selected interpreter needs the ATLAS environment as well as EL9. If terminal startup files overwrite  PATH ,  PYTHONPATH , or library paths, they can also override the backend's inherited environment; correct those settings and restart the backend. \n For an Athena development build, ATLAS also documents generated  ide_python  and  env.txt  settings for its Python tools. Those are build-specific additions; do not assume every AnalysisBase installation provides them. \n Run and debug a small analysis check \n Create  check_atlas.py  in your project, or  download this example : \n \"\"\"Run with VS Code's Run button, debugger, and notebook kernel.\"\"\"\nimport os\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport ROOT\n\nROOT.gROOT.SetBatch(True)\nprint(Path(\"/etc/os-release\").read_text())\nprint(\"Python:\", sys.executable)\nprint(\"ATLAS project:\", os.environ.get(\"AtlasProject\"))\nprint(\"ATLAS version:\", os.environ.get(\"AtlasVersion\"))\nprint(\"ROOT:\", ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())\nprint(\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES:\", os.environ.get(\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"))\nhistogram = ROOT.TH1D(\"check\", \"VS Code ATLAS check\", 10, 0, 10)\nhistogram.Fill(4.0)  # Put a breakpoint here.\nprint(\"Entries:\", histogram.GetEntries())\nassert histogram.GetEntries() == 1 \n Use  Run Python File in Terminal  from the editor's Run button. Then put a breakpoint on  histogram.Fill(4.0)  and run  Python Debugger: Debug Python File , or press  F5  and choose the Python debugger. Confirm that execution stops at the breakpoint and finishes with one histogram entry. Also test an import from your own analysis package if you have one. \n For a reusable F5 configuration, save this as  .vscode/launch.json  in the project ( download ): \n {\n  \"version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"configurations\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ATLAS: current Python file\",\n      \"type\": \"debugpy\",\n      \"request\": \"launch\",\n      \"program\": \"${file}\",\n      \"console\": \"integratedTerminal\",\n      \"justMyCode\": false\n    }\n  ]\n} \n This tests Python debugging. Stepping into compiled C++ libraries requires a separate C++ debugger configuration and suitable debug symbols. \n Notebooks and the Python Interactive window \n Install  Jupyter  and  Jupyter Notebook Renderers  on the remote target. Open a notebook and use the top-right  Select Kernel  menu to choose the same ATLAS Python environment. Kernel selection is separate from  Python: Select Interpreter . For a  .py  file with  # %%  cells, select the corresponding kernel in the Python Interactive window too. \n The chosen Python must have a compatible  ipykernel : \n python -c 'import ipykernel; print(ipykernel.__version__)' \n If that fails, arrange a writable, compatible kernel environment for that release before proceeding; do not install packages into read-only CVMFS or replace the release's ROOT/Python stack indiscriminately. Run the contents of  check_atlas.py  in a notebook cell to confirm that the  kernel , not only a terminal, is using EL9 and ATLAS. \n Alternative: connect only the notebook kernel \n For a browser interface with a project-managed Pixi environment, follow the complete  Using JupyterLab  workflow. The instructions below connect a local VS Code notebook to an ATLAS-configured Jupyter server. \n If you only need notebooks, a  local VS Code window  can connect to a  Jupyter server started inside EL9 . Allocate a compute node and enter the configured container as above. This requires a working  jupyter  and  ipykernel  in that environment. After  asetup , start: \n jupyter lab --no-browser --ip=127.0.0.1 --port=8888 \\\n  --ServerApp.port_retries=0 \n Keep its authentication enabled. In a  local terminal , forward that port through the allocated node, using the actual hostname: \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8888:127.0.0.1:8888 cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu \n The fixed-port and loopback settings match  Using JupyterLab , which also explains choosing another port and reconnecting. If port 8888 is occupied on the node, choose another server port and update the tunnel's remote port. \n In a  local VS Code window , install Microsoft's  Jupyter  extension locally and open your notebook. Choose  Select Kernel → Select Another Kernel… → Existing Jupyter Server , and enter the authenticated  http://127.0.0.1:8888/...  URL printed by Jupyter, including its token. Select the ATLAS kernel and run the Python/ROOT check above in a cell. The notebook file is opened and saved locally; code executes remotely, and input/output paths in that code refer to Unity storage. Keep both the Jupyter process and SSH forward alive. This provides notebook/cell execution, but does not move ordinary Python Run/F5 or language services into EL9. \n"}, {"title": "End a session and troubleshoot", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#end-a-session-and-troubleshoot", "text": "End a session and troubleshoot \n Close the remote VS Code connection when finished, stop any tunnel/Jupyter process, and release the allocation by exiting its original shell or using  scancel 123456  with the correct job ID. Closing VS Code alone does not necessarily cancel the allocation. \n When changing ATLAS releases, stop the previous backend and reconnect after setting up the new environment. For SSH,  Remote-SSH: Kill VS Code Server on Host…  is available; refresh the allocation configuration, hostname, and dedicated server directory as needed. For tunnels, stop the old foreground process and use a fresh session directory/name. Running  asetup  only in an already open terminal does not reconfigure the other VS Code processes. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n Check \n \n \n \n \n Compute-node SSH is rejected \n The allocation must still be running, and the hostname, username, key, and  ProxyJump  must match it. \n \n \n Terminal is EL9 but Run/F5 cannot import ROOT \n Method A changes only the terminal. With B/C, check the selected interpreter and whether an older backend or startup file replaced the environment. \n \n \n RemoteCommand bootstrap hangs or fails \n Test the SSH alias in a terminal, inspect Remote - SSH output, and check that startup files print no extra stdout. Use the supplied stdin-preserving launcher. \n \n \n Launcher rejects the saved job \n Recreate the configuration for the current node/job; the CPU launcher expects exactly one running job of yours on that node. \n \n \n Reconnect reaches an old environment \n Stop the old backend and use the allocation-specific server/state paths. \n \n \n Notebook import fails while Run works \n Select the notebook kernel separately; check its Python path and compatible  ipykernel . \n \n \n Tunnel cannot sign in or connect \n Check the device-login identity, outbound service access, and that the allocation and foreground CLI are still alive. \n \n \n GPU command or framework fails \n Use  --nv  and the original allocated GPU shell. Confirm Slurm's device selection with the intended framework;  nvidia-smi  alone does not validate framework compatibility or assigned-device mapping. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "using-vscode/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Unity:  SSH configuration ,  VS Code ,  interactive jobs , and  FAQ . \n ATLAS:  VS Code remote usage with SSH  and  Python in VS Code . \n Microsoft:  Remote SSH ,  Remote Tunnels ,  Python environments ,  Python debugging , and  Jupyter kernel management . \n Microsoft implementation details:  CLI arguments ,  CLI state/cache , and  server startup/reuse . \n DCCN:  VS Code on an HPC cluster , including a tunnel launched inside a Singularity container. \n Slurm:  SSH adoption into a job  and  GPU resource management . \n \n Next:   Using JupyterLab  runs notebooks from your own Pixi environment through a browser. \n"}, {"title": "Using JupyterLab", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html", "text": "Run JupyterLab from your own terminal session on a Unity compute node, then connect from a browser on your computer through an SSH tunnel. Your project chooses the Pixi packages; you choose the Slurm resources and whether to run inside EL9. Notebooks, data access, and computation stay on Unity. \n This chapter assumes you have completed  Setting up the environment , including the one-time directories and  .bashrc  configuration , and  installed Pixi . It reuses  USER_PI , Unity  $USER ,  ATLAS_WORKDIR , and the allocation/container helpers. \n 1. Prepare the project and your SSH connection \n Use an existing project whose Pixi environment includes  jupyterlab ,  ipykernel , and the analysis packages  you need. For example, a HEP project might supply Uproot, Awkward Array, Hist, and plotting packages. The examples below use the project's default environment. See  Using Pixi  for installing a supplied manifest, or the  Carpentries introduction  for creating or changing one. \n JupyterLab supplies the interface and server; the  kernel  is the Python process that executes notebook cells. Both will run in the project's Pixi environment in this recipe. A global Jupyter installation is not needed. \n On your  local computer , use the SSH configuration from  Using VS Code . If you have not configured it yet, merge the following into  ~/.ssh/config  on Linux/macOS or  %USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\config  on Windows. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  and the private-key path with your Unity account and key; do not duplicate entries already present. \n Host unity\n    HostName login.unityhpc.org\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost *.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity \n The second entry reaches a compute node through the login service. It uses your literal Unity username, which may differ from your laptop's  $USER . Use an ordinary compute-node connection for this chapter, without the VS Code container  RemoteCommand  alias. \n In your first local terminal, connect: \n ssh unity \n If Windows OpenSSH reports  Corrupted MAC on input , Unity's  SSH guide  documents adding  MACs hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com  to the affected SSH host entries. \n 2. Allocate a compute node \n On the  Unity login node , record the login hostname, then start  tmux  before requesting resources. This lets the allocation and Jupyter process survive an SSH disconnection: \n hostname -f\ntmux new -s atlas-jupyter\n\n# Inside tmux, on the login node:\nunity_cpu \n The CPU helper requests four CPUs, 16 GB of memory, and two hours. Adjust the allocation to your workload using the  environment chapter . For a GPU session, use  unity_gpu   instead of   unity_cpu . \n When the allocation starts, you should be in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell . Use the  location checks in the environment chapter  if the shell remains on a login node. Record both values below; the compute hostname is the destination for your later SSH tunnel, while the job ID identifies the allocation: \n hostname -f\necho \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \n If the new shell has not loaded your directory settings or helpers, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. Start Jupyter in this allocated shell, not on the login node. \n 3. Choose Ubuntu or EL9 \n For a self-contained Pixi analysis that supports Unity's Ubuntu hosts, you can remain in the compute-node shell. If your project requires EL9, enter the container  before installing or starting its environment : \n # Ubuntu compute node, for a CPU session requiring EL9:\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For an allocated NVIDIA GPU node, use  cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"  followed by  atlas_gpu  instead. That helper enables the GPU passthrough described in the environment chapter. The Pixi environment must also contain a compatible GPU framework; reserving a GPU does not make ordinary notebook code use it. \n Inside EL9, check  cat /etc/os-release . This workflow uses Apptainer's normal shared host networking, so the SSH tunnel can reach Jupyter on the compute node's loopback address. Do not add a separate network namespace such as  --net  to this recipe. \n Keep an independent Pixi analysis separate from  asetup , as described in  Using Pixi alongside ATLAS . Start with a fresh shell/container before loading an ATLAS release. A Pixi Python environment does not automatically acquire the release's PyROOT or Athena bindings, and inherited ATLAS Python/library paths can conflict with its packages. If your notebook needs those bindings, it needs a compatible ATLAS kernel setup; see the  ATLAS notebook checks in Using VS Code . \n 4. Install the environment and start JupyterLab \n In the chosen  compute-node environment  (Ubuntu or EL9), enter the existing project. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\n\n# Restore the user-tool path if the container reset PATH.\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\npixi install\npixi run python -c 'import sys, jupyterlab, ipykernel; print(sys.executable)' \n Keep the printed Python path for the notebook check below. If an import fails, the project's environment needs the missing dependency. Update the manifest through the project's normal workflow; do not install into an unrelated Python. For a project with a supplied lock file, you can use  pixi install --locked  as explained in  Using Pixi . \n Give this environment's kernel a clear name. Run this once after installing the environment, and repeat if you recreate or move it: \n pixi run python -m ipykernel install --sys-prefix \\\n  --name unity-hep --display-name \"Python (HEP Pixi)\" \n --sys-prefix  places the kernel definition inside this Pixi environment, where this JupyterLab server can find it. It does not register a container-dependent interpreter for every other Jupyter server in your account. The definition records the interpreter path, so keep the project in its current location during the session. \n Now start the server in the foreground: \n pixi run jupyter lab --no-browser --ip=127.0.0.1 --port=8888 \\\n  --ServerApp.port_retries=0 \n The server listens only on the compute node's loopback interface.  port_retries=0  makes it stop if port 8888 is occupied, instead of silently choosing another port. In that case, select another port, such as 8890, here  and in the remote-port position of the tunnel below . \n Keep authentication enabled. With Jupyter's default token authentication, the terminal prints an HTTP URL containing a private token. Keep that terminal running; do not copy the token into a repository or share it. If you previously configured a Jupyter password, use that password when prompted instead. \n 5. Connect from your browser \n Open a  second terminal on your local computer . Replace  COMPUTE_NODE  with the full compute hostname recorded in step 2, for example  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu : \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8888:127.0.0.1:8888 COMPUTE_NODE \n This command uses the  ProxyJump  configuration above. It forwards your computer's port 8888 to port 8888 on the allocated node's loopback interface. The SSH destination must be the  compute node ; forwarding to the login node's loopback would reach the wrong machine. A quiet terminal is normal: leave the tunnel running. \n Open the  http://127.0.0.1:8888/lab?token=...  URL in your local browser, substituting the actual token printed by Jupyter. Use its HTTP URL, not the  file:///...  shortcut, which refers to a file on Unity. The SSH connection carries the browser traffic to the server. \n If port 8888 is already used on your  local computer , use a different local port while retaining the server's remote port: \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8889:127.0.0.1:8888 COMPUTE_NODE \n Then open  http://127.0.0.1:8889/lab?token=...  with the same token. In  -L , the first port is local and the last port is on the compute node. \n 6. Select and check the notebook kernel \n In JupyterLab, open or create a notebook and select  Python (HEP Pixi)  from the kernel menu. Check this even for an existing notebook: it may remember a different kernel. \n Run the following cell: \n import sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nprint(\"Python:\", sys.executable)\nprint(\"Working directory:\", Path.cwd()) \n The Python path should match the one printed by  pixi run python  in step 4. Test the analysis packages your project actually includes, for example: \n import uproot\nimport awkward as ak\nimport hist\n\nprint(\"Uproot:\", uproot.__version__)\nprint(\"Awkward:\", ak.__version__)\nprint(\"Hist:\", hist.__version__) \n The selected kernel has access to the packages installed in that Pixi environment and the files visible to its Unity process. Notebook paths refer to Unity storage, not your laptop. JupyterLab's file browser starts in the project directory; notebooks saved there are saved on Unity. Normal filesystem permissions and container mounts still apply. For a GPU project, also run its framework's device check in a cell. \n 7. Disconnect, reconnect, and finish \n Save the notebook before disconnecting. In the first terminal, detach from  tmux  with  Ctrl+B , then  D . You can then close the SSH connection and stop the local forwarding command with  Ctrl+C . The server and kernel can continue while their allocation is running. Closing a browser tab alone does not stop them or release resources. \n To return, reconnect to Unity and use  ssh loginX  to reach the same login node you recorded before starting  tmux , if necessary. Then run: \n tmux attach -t atlas-jupyter \n Recreate the local SSH tunnel and reopen the authenticated URL. If the same Jupyter server and kernel are still running, their in-memory state remains available. Once the Slurm time limit is reached, the job is cancelled, or the node fails, that state is lost; a new allocation requires a new server and tunnel destination. Saved notebooks and files remain, but saved cell output is not a checkpoint of Python variables. \n When finished, save your work, stop Jupyter with  Ctrl+C  in its terminal and confirm shutdown if prompted. Exit the container if you entered one, then exit the original allocated compute shell to release the allocation. Stop the local SSH tunnel too. From a login shell you can check remaining jobs with  squeue -u \"$USER\" . \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n jupyter  or  ipykernel  is missing \n Both must be in this project's Pixi environment; check imports through  pixi run python . \n \n \n The server reports its port is occupied \n Select a different server port and update the tunnel's remote port. Keep the fixed-port behavior. \n \n \n SSH reports  Address already in use \n Change the tunnel's local port and the browser URL, as in the 8889 example. \n \n \n Compute-node SSH fails \n Check the live allocation, full compute hostname, Unity username/key, and  ProxyJump  configuration. \n \n \n Browser connection is refused \n Keep both Jupyter and the local tunnel running; check that the tunnel targets the correct node and server port. \n \n \n Browser asks for authentication \n Use the current server's token or configured password. A restarted server may have a new token. \n \n \n Imports work in a terminal but fail in the notebook \n Select  Python (HEP Pixi)  and compare  sys.executable . Repeat kernel registration if the environment was recreated or moved. \n \n \n A package was added but the notebook still uses the old state \n Stop/restart the kernel after updating the environment; rerun the notebook cells. \n \n \n ATLAS bindings fail in Pixi Python \n Follow a project-supported ATLAS kernel setup; EL9 alone does not make independent Python environments interchangeable. \n \n \n \n References \n \n Unity:  SSH ,  compute-node SSH configuration , and  interactive jobs and tmux . \n Pixi:  JupyterLab integration  and  running commands . \n Jupyter:  authentication and token URLs ,  server options , and  kernel troubleshooting . \n IPython:  kernel registration . \n OpenSSH:  ssh  port forwarding . \n Apptainer:  network namespaces . \n \n Next:   Codex Agents  adds an agent with ATLAS software and authenticated dataset tools. \n"}, {"title": "1. Prepare the project and your SSH connection", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#prepare", "text": "1. Prepare the project and your SSH connection \n Use an existing project whose Pixi environment includes  jupyterlab ,  ipykernel , and the analysis packages  you need. For example, a HEP project might supply Uproot, Awkward Array, Hist, and plotting packages. The examples below use the project's default environment. See  Using Pixi  for installing a supplied manifest, or the  Carpentries introduction  for creating or changing one. \n JupyterLab supplies the interface and server; the  kernel  is the Python process that executes notebook cells. Both will run in the project's Pixi environment in this recipe. A global Jupyter installation is not needed. \n On your  local computer , use the SSH configuration from  Using VS Code . If you have not configured it yet, merge the following into  ~/.ssh/config  on Linux/macOS or  %USERPROFILE%\\.ssh\\config  on Windows. Replace  YOUR_USERNAME  and the private-key path with your Unity account and key; do not duplicate entries already present. \n Host unity\n    HostName login.unityhpc.org\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n\nHost *.unity.rc.umass.edu\n    User YOUR_USERNAME\n    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\n    ProxyJump unity \n The second entry reaches a compute node through the login service. It uses your literal Unity username, which may differ from your laptop's  $USER . Use an ordinary compute-node connection for this chapter, without the VS Code container  RemoteCommand  alias. \n In your first local terminal, connect: \n ssh unity \n If Windows OpenSSH reports  Corrupted MAC on input , Unity's  SSH guide  documents adding  MACs hmac-sha2-512-etm@openssh.com  to the affected SSH host entries. \n"}, {"title": "2. Allocate a compute node", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#allocation", "text": "2. Allocate a compute node \n On the  Unity login node , record the login hostname, then start  tmux  before requesting resources. This lets the allocation and Jupyter process survive an SSH disconnection: \n hostname -f\ntmux new -s atlas-jupyter\n\n# Inside tmux, on the login node:\nunity_cpu \n The CPU helper requests four CPUs, 16 GB of memory, and two hours. Adjust the allocation to your workload using the  environment chapter . For a GPU session, use  unity_gpu   instead of   unity_cpu . \n When the allocation starts, you should be in the  Ubuntu compute-node shell . Use the  location checks in the environment chapter  if the shell remains on a login node. Record both values below; the compute hostname is the destination for your later SSH tunnel, while the job ID identifies the allocation: \n hostname -f\necho \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" \n If the new shell has not loaded your directory settings or helpers, run  source ~/.bashrc  there. Start Jupyter in this allocated shell, not on the login node. \n"}, {"title": "3. Choose Ubuntu or EL9", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#runtime", "text": "3. Choose Ubuntu or EL9 \n For a self-contained Pixi analysis that supports Unity's Ubuntu hosts, you can remain in the compute-node shell. If your project requires EL9, enter the container  before installing or starting its environment : \n # Ubuntu compute node, for a CPU session requiring EL9:\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For an allocated NVIDIA GPU node, use  cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"  followed by  atlas_gpu  instead. That helper enables the GPU passthrough described in the environment chapter. The Pixi environment must also contain a compatible GPU framework; reserving a GPU does not make ordinary notebook code use it. \n Inside EL9, check  cat /etc/os-release . This workflow uses Apptainer's normal shared host networking, so the SSH tunnel can reach Jupyter on the compute node's loopback address. Do not add a separate network namespace such as  --net  to this recipe. \n Keep an independent Pixi analysis separate from  asetup , as described in  Using Pixi alongside ATLAS . Start with a fresh shell/container before loading an ATLAS release. A Pixi Python environment does not automatically acquire the release's PyROOT or Athena bindings, and inherited ATLAS Python/library paths can conflict with its packages. If your notebook needs those bindings, it needs a compatible ATLAS kernel setup; see the  ATLAS notebook checks in Using VS Code . \n"}, {"title": "4. Install the environment and start JupyterLab", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#start", "text": "4. Install the environment and start JupyterLab \n In the chosen  compute-node environment  (Ubuntu or EL9), enter the existing project. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\n\n# Restore the user-tool path if the container reset PATH.\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\"\npixi install\npixi run python -c 'import sys, jupyterlab, ipykernel; print(sys.executable)' \n Keep the printed Python path for the notebook check below. If an import fails, the project's environment needs the missing dependency. Update the manifest through the project's normal workflow; do not install into an unrelated Python. For a project with a supplied lock file, you can use  pixi install --locked  as explained in  Using Pixi . \n Give this environment's kernel a clear name. Run this once after installing the environment, and repeat if you recreate or move it: \n pixi run python -m ipykernel install --sys-prefix \\\n  --name unity-hep --display-name \"Python (HEP Pixi)\" \n --sys-prefix  places the kernel definition inside this Pixi environment, where this JupyterLab server can find it. It does not register a container-dependent interpreter for every other Jupyter server in your account. The definition records the interpreter path, so keep the project in its current location during the session. \n Now start the server in the foreground: \n pixi run jupyter lab --no-browser --ip=127.0.0.1 --port=8888 \\\n  --ServerApp.port_retries=0 \n The server listens only on the compute node's loopback interface.  port_retries=0  makes it stop if port 8888 is occupied, instead of silently choosing another port. In that case, select another port, such as 8890, here  and in the remote-port position of the tunnel below . \n Keep authentication enabled. With Jupyter's default token authentication, the terminal prints an HTTP URL containing a private token. Keep that terminal running; do not copy the token into a repository or share it. If you previously configured a Jupyter password, use that password when prompted instead. \n"}, {"title": "5. Connect from your browser", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#connect", "text": "5. Connect from your browser \n Open a  second terminal on your local computer . Replace  COMPUTE_NODE  with the full compute hostname recorded in step 2, for example  cpu001.unity.rc.umass.edu : \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8888:127.0.0.1:8888 COMPUTE_NODE \n This command uses the  ProxyJump  configuration above. It forwards your computer's port 8888 to port 8888 on the allocated node's loopback interface. The SSH destination must be the  compute node ; forwarding to the login node's loopback would reach the wrong machine. A quiet terminal is normal: leave the tunnel running. \n Open the  http://127.0.0.1:8888/lab?token=...  URL in your local browser, substituting the actual token printed by Jupyter. Use its HTTP URL, not the  file:///...  shortcut, which refers to a file on Unity. The SSH connection carries the browser traffic to the server. \n If port 8888 is already used on your  local computer , use a different local port while retaining the server's remote port: \n ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes \\\n  -L 127.0.0.1:8889:127.0.0.1:8888 COMPUTE_NODE \n Then open  http://127.0.0.1:8889/lab?token=...  with the same token. In  -L , the first port is local and the last port is on the compute node. \n"}, {"title": "6. Select and check the notebook kernel", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#kernel", "text": "6. Select and check the notebook kernel \n In JupyterLab, open or create a notebook and select  Python (HEP Pixi)  from the kernel menu. Check this even for an existing notebook: it may remember a different kernel. \n Run the following cell: \n import sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nprint(\"Python:\", sys.executable)\nprint(\"Working directory:\", Path.cwd()) \n The Python path should match the one printed by  pixi run python  in step 4. Test the analysis packages your project actually includes, for example: \n import uproot\nimport awkward as ak\nimport hist\n\nprint(\"Uproot:\", uproot.__version__)\nprint(\"Awkward:\", ak.__version__)\nprint(\"Hist:\", hist.__version__) \n The selected kernel has access to the packages installed in that Pixi environment and the files visible to its Unity process. Notebook paths refer to Unity storage, not your laptop. JupyterLab's file browser starts in the project directory; notebooks saved there are saved on Unity. Normal filesystem permissions and container mounts still apply. For a GPU project, also run its framework's device check in a cell. \n"}, {"title": "7. Disconnect, reconnect, and finish", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#sessions", "text": "7. Disconnect, reconnect, and finish \n Save the notebook before disconnecting. In the first terminal, detach from  tmux  with  Ctrl+B , then  D . You can then close the SSH connection and stop the local forwarding command with  Ctrl+C . The server and kernel can continue while their allocation is running. Closing a browser tab alone does not stop them or release resources. \n To return, reconnect to Unity and use  ssh loginX  to reach the same login node you recorded before starting  tmux , if necessary. Then run: \n tmux attach -t atlas-jupyter \n Recreate the local SSH tunnel and reopen the authenticated URL. If the same Jupyter server and kernel are still running, their in-memory state remains available. Once the Slurm time limit is reached, the job is cancelled, or the node fails, that state is lost; a new allocation requires a new server and tunnel destination. Saved notebooks and files remain, but saved cell output is not a checkpoint of Python variables. \n When finished, save your work, stop Jupyter with  Ctrl+C  in its terminal and confirm shutdown if prompted. Exit the container if you entered one, then exit the original allocated compute shell to release the allocation. Stop the local SSH tunnel too. From a login shell you can check remaining jobs with  squeue -u \"$USER\" . \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n jupyter  or  ipykernel  is missing \n Both must be in this project's Pixi environment; check imports through  pixi run python . \n \n \n The server reports its port is occupied \n Select a different server port and update the tunnel's remote port. Keep the fixed-port behavior. \n \n \n SSH reports  Address already in use \n Change the tunnel's local port and the browser URL, as in the 8889 example. \n \n \n Compute-node SSH fails \n Check the live allocation, full compute hostname, Unity username/key, and  ProxyJump  configuration. \n \n \n Browser connection is refused \n Keep both Jupyter and the local tunnel running; check that the tunnel targets the correct node and server port. \n \n \n Browser asks for authentication \n Use the current server's token or configured password. A restarted server may have a new token. \n \n \n Imports work in a terminal but fail in the notebook \n Select  Python (HEP Pixi)  and compare  sys.executable . Repeat kernel registration if the environment was recreated or moved. \n \n \n A package was added but the notebook still uses the old state \n Stop/restart the kernel after updating the environment; rerun the notebook cells. \n \n \n ATLAS bindings fail in Pixi Python \n Follow a project-supported ATLAS kernel setup; EL9 alone does not make independent Python environments interchangeable. \n \n \n \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "using-jupyterlab/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n Unity:  SSH ,  compute-node SSH configuration , and  interactive jobs and tmux . \n Pixi:  JupyterLab integration  and  running commands . \n Jupyter:  authentication and token URLs ,  server options , and  kernel troubleshooting . \n IPython:  kernel registration . \n OpenSSH:  ssh  port forwarding . \n Apptainer:  network namespaces . \n \n Next:   Codex Agents  adds an agent with ATLAS software and authenticated dataset tools. \n"}, {"title": "Codex Agents", "url": "codex-agents/index.html", "text": "Run Codex  inside the allocated EL9 container , after selecting your ATLAS release. Its shell commands can then compile and run your analysis with the same software you use interactively. Add two per-user stdio MCP servers so Codex can query Rucio and AMI using your own grid identity. \n This workflow needs no facility-wide credential broker and no change to Unity login. You authenticate to Codex separately from ATLAS, create a short-lived VOMS proxy yourself, and let Codex start the MCP processes when needed. \n 1. What runs where \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Environment and purpose \n \n \n \n \n Codex CLI \n Runs in EL9 on your allocated compute node, in your analysis directory. Its shell commands inherit the ATLAS release environment. \n \n \n Rucio and AMI MCP servers \n Child processes started by Codex in the same container, under your Unix account. They communicate over stdin/stdout; there is no listening HTTP port. \n \n \n MCP Python environment \n A separate Pixi environment containing the clients and grid dependencies. It does not replace the Python used by your analysis. \n \n \n VOMS proxy \n A short-lived file created by you before starting Codex; both MCP servers use it to authenticate as you. \n \n \n \n You need a Unity account and group work directory, an OpenAI sign-in method enabled for Codex, and an active ATLAS grid identity. Complete  Grid certificates and authentication  to obtain and register a certificate and install its encrypted private key on Unity. A Unity login, CERN password, or Codex login does not replace the grid credential. \n Complete  Setting up the environment  first, including the one-time creation of your work/project directories and the  .bashrc  configuration , then  install Pixi . This chapter uses your saved  USER_PI  and  ATLAS_WORKDIR , Unity  $USER , and the  unity_cpu ,  unity_gpu ,  setupATLAS , and  atlas_gpu  commands. \n The compute node needs outbound connectivity to the sign-in/model service and ATLAS endpoints; installation additionally needs access to the package sources. This chapter starts with  dataset discovery and metadata queries : Rucio MCP is explicitly launched in read-only mode. \n 2. Allocate resources and enter EL9 \n From your computer, connect using the  local SSH configuration from Using VS Code : \n ssh unity \n For a session that can survive an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas-codex  on the login node first and record its hostname, following the  session guidance . Then request resources: \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu \n The default helper requests four CPUs, 16 GB of memory, and two hours. Once Unity gives you a compute-node shell, check  hostname -f  and  echo \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" , then start the container. If this new shell has not loaded your configuration, run  source ~/.bashrc  first. \n # Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For GPU analysis, use this alternative sequence.  unity_gpu  requests one GPU with the same CPU, memory, and time limits;  atlas_gpu  loads Apptainer and supplies the GPU passthrough options from the  environment chapter : \n # Unity login node\nunity_gpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu GPU compute node\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\natlas_gpu \n /work  is normally visible inside the container without an additional  -m /work . If a required path is missing, follow the  mount guidance . \n Inside EL9, explicitly enter your analysis work area and check the environment: \n # Inside EL9\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\nprintf 'Container home: %s\\n' \"$HOME\" \n This chapter targets  x86_64  nodes. On the first visit, install the tools in this fresh container before running  asetup . On later visits, reuse the installation and go to  Prepare a proxy , then  Load ATLAS and start Codex . \n 3. Install Codex and sign in \n Run the official standalone installer  inside EL9 . It does not require Node.js or a Python environment: \n curl -fsSL https://chatgpt.com/codex/install.sh | sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\ncodex --version \n The installer currently uses  ~/.local/bin . Keep the installation in a persistent directory visible in future containers. If the launcher changes the apparent home directory, use the paths visible  inside EL9  consistently. \n Use device authentication on the remote node: \n codex login --device-auth\ncodex login status \n Open the printed URL on your own computer and enter the one-time code. This requires device-code authentication to be enabled in the relevant ChatGPT account/workspace settings. If it is unavailable, use the alternatives in the  official authentication guide , or ask the workspace administrator to enable the supported flow. You do not need a browser running on the compute node. \n Codex normally retains the login in its configured credential store, so a new allocation does not imply a new login. Keep its configuration and credentials private; do not put them in your analysis repository. This authentication is only for Codex—ATLAS authentication comes below. \n 4. Install isolated MCP tools \n Use a dedicated Pixi environment for the MCP servers. In particular,  do not  pip install  them into the Python selected by  asetup , and do not activate their environment in your analysis shell. Their dependency versions serve the catalog clients, while analysis code must keep the release's Python and ROOT libraries. \n This section assumes Pixi is installed as described in  Using Pixi . Check that the installation is visible inside EL9: \n \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\" --version \n The workflow uses a supplied manifest, following the earlier chapter's  existing-project instructions . Download the  manifest  and  environment launcher  to the same private directory: \n MCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$MCP_DIR\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/codex/pixi.toml \\\n  --output \"$MCP_DIR/pixi.toml\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/codex/atlas-mcp-env.sh \\\n  --output \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nchmod u+x \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n The manifest uses conda-forge packages, including pyAMI through the AMI package's dependencies, and pins the MCP versions: \n [workspace]\nname = \"atlas-mcp\"\nchannels = [\"conda-forge\"]\nplatforms = [\"linux-64\"]\n\n[dependencies]\npython = \"3.11.*\"\nrucio-mcp = \"==0.10.0\"\nami-mcp = \"==0.3.1\"\nvoms = \"*\"\nvoms-lsc = \"*\"\nca-policy-lcg = \"*\" \n Install before the first MCP handshake, so Codex does not need to wait for dependency resolution and downloads: \n env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$(id -un)\" PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LANG=C.UTF-8 \\\n  \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\" install --manifest-path \"$MCP_DIR/pixi.toml\" \n Keep the generated  pixi.lock  with the manifest; the launcher uses  --locked  to prevent silent re-resolution at server startup. Pixi generates this lock file during installation. If home quota is limited, choose a private persistent work directory for  MCP_DIR   before downloading and installing . Use that directory in every subsequent  MCP_DIR  assignment and in the absolute launcher paths below. \n The launcher starts Pixi with an explicit clean environment and forwards only the relevant proxy/account settings. This keeps ATLAS  PYTHONPATH ,  PYTHONHOME , and  LD_LIBRARY_PATH  out of the MCP clients. Pixi then activates its own grid CA environment. Codex's parent shell remains unchanged. Do not add  pixi run --clean-env  to this launcher: that additional filter can remove the explicitly forwarded grid variables. \n If Pixi is installed elsewhere, set  ATLAS_MCP_PIXI  to its absolute executable path, and substitute that path in the installation command. The launcher accepts this override. \n Check that the installed packages import and their command-line interfaces work: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" python -c \\\n  'import sys, importlib.metadata as m, pyAMI.client, rucio.client; print(sys.version); print(\"rucio-mcp\", m.version(\"rucio-mcp\")); print(\"ami-mcp\", m.version(\"ami-mcp\"))'\n\"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-init --help \n These are installation checks, not authenticated service tests. \n 5. Prepare a personal grid proxy \n Perform authentication yourself in the terminal  before starting Codex . The MCP servers must not stop during their stdin/stdout protocol to ask for a private-key passphrase. \n Use the certificate and encrypted private key installed in  Grid certificates and authentication :  ~/.globus/usercert.pem  and  ~/.globus/userkey.pem . Install that chapter's recommended  atlas_proxy  helper  once as well. Here it runs VOMS commands through the isolated MCP launcher, which supplies its own grid tools and CA bundle; no additional  lsetup rucio  is needed. \n Check the paths  inside EL9 ; the container may expose your home at a different path than the Ubuntu host. The helper passes the certificate and key paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key . If your files have different names or locations, adjust those paths in your installed  atlas-proxy.sh . \n printf 'HOME=%s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n Set the launcher path and your  Rucio account name , which may differ from your Unity username: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nMCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" \n If you already created a valid, readable proxy in this same container using the  grid-authentication chapter , keep its existing  X509_USER_PROXY  and  ATLAS_PROXY_DIR  values. Check its lifetime with the command below and skip the creation block while it remains valid: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft \n Otherwise, create the proxy with the same helper used elsewhere in this guide, passing the isolated launcher as its second argument: \n atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n The helper creates a private directory and supplies an explicit  -out  filename. Stop if proxy creation fails. Keep the exported  X509_USER_PROXY ; do not replace it with the output of  voms-proxy-info -path  after a failure, which can leave it empty. See  grid-authentication troubleshooting  for both credential discovery and invalid output-path errors. \n The passphrase prompt unlocks your grid private key; it is not asking for your Unity or Codex password. The last command must report a positive remaining lifetime. A requested 12-hour lifetime is a request, not a guarantee—inspect the actual expiry. \n The proxy stays on this node/container and is passed to the clients by  filename , not by putting its contents into the conversation or configuration. Recreate it after moving to another node or a container with a different  /tmp . The original certificate/key and the short-lived proxy are different files. \n The MCP  rucio_whoami  check below will verify the actual service-side account. The server's  --site atlas  selects its bundled ATLAS configuration. \n 6. Give Codex the MCP configuration \n Print the launcher's absolute path: \n printf '%s\\n' \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nmkdir -p \"$HOME/.codex\" \n Merge the following into your existing  ~/.codex/config.toml  inside EL9  ( download the example ). Replace each complete  command  value with the absolute launcher path printed above. TOML command paths are literal: do not put  $HOME  in them. If you already have any of these tables, edit them instead of adding duplicate tables. Put the top-level settings before the first table header. \n # Merge into your existing ~/.codex/config.toml; do not replace it blindly.\n# Top-level settings must appear before any [table] headers.\napproval_policy = \"on-request\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\nallow_login_shell = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"all\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[mcp_servers.atlas-rucio]\ncommand = \"/ABSOLUTE/HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nargs = [\"rucio-mcp\", \"serve\", \"--site\", \"atlas\", \"--auth-type\", \"x509\", \"--read-only\"]\nenv_vars = [\"X509_USER_PROXY\", \"RUCIO_ACCOUNT\", \"ATLAS_MCP_PIXI\", \"ATLAS_PMGXSEC_PATH\"]\nstartup_timeout_sec = 120\ntool_timeout_sec = 120\n\n[mcp_servers.atlas-ami]\ncommand = \"/ABSOLUTE/HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nargs = [\"ami-mcp\", \"serve\"]\nenv_vars = [\"X509_USER_PROXY\", \"ATLAS_MCP_PIXI\", \"ATLAS_PMGXSEC_PATH\"]\nstartup_timeout_sec = 120\ntool_timeout_sec = 120 \n The settings serve two different environments: \n \n allow_login_shell = false  and  inherit = \"all\"  preserve the exported ATLAS setup when Codex runs analysis commands. The explicit default-exclusion setting filters variable names containing  KEY ,  SECRET , or  TOKEN ; it does not prevent processes from reading files accessible to your account. \n Each MCP server's  env_vars  forwards the  current  proxy path at startup. A new allocation can use a new path without rewriting the TOML. The wrapper isolates the server's Python environment. \n --read-only  blocks Rucio MCP write operations. AMI's standard tools are oriented toward metadata queries; AMI's own role permissions remain authoritative. These settings do not impose a separate read-only restriction on Codex's general shell. \n \n These user-level settings apply to other Codex sessions using the same configuration. Codex also supports project-scoped  .codex/config.toml  in trusted projects if you prefer to scope the ATLAS-specific settings; keep personal absolute paths out of shared commits. \n Verify the entries: \n codex mcp list\ncodex mcp get atlas-rucio\ncodex mcp get atlas-ami \n These commands confirm registration, not successful grid access.  Do not run  codex mcp login  for these stdio servers ; that command is for compatible HTTP/OAuth servers. Their authentication is the VOMS proxy you just created. \n Codex will launch both commands itself. You do not need to keep two extra terminals open running  rucio-mcp serve  and  ami-mcp serve , start them in the background, or expose a network port. A manually started stdio server waiting silently for protocol input is not an interactive command prompt. \n 7. Load ATLAS and start Codex \n In the same EL9 shell, enter your existing analysis checkout. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name, as in the Pixi and JupyterLab chapters. Select the full  project,version  release string described in  Load analysis software , then source any generated local build setup: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi\n# If needed, use your real generated setup path:\n# source /absolute/path/to/build/PLATFORM/setup.sh \n Continue only after the release and build setup succeed.  Then start Codex from that project directory: \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())'\ncodex --sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request \n Set the thread count to fit your actual allocation. Run  nvidia-smi  and a small framework-specific check before GPU work. Starting Codex on Ubuntu and later entering a container in one subprocess will not relocate the running agent and its other processes. \n The default workspace is the directory where you start Codex. If a necessary output directory lies elsewhere, add that particular directory with  --add-dir /absolute/output/path . Keep heavy builds, event loops, and training within the Slurm allocation. \n Inside Codex, use  /mcp  to inspect the active connections. Start with this small validation request: \n \n Verify that you are on the allocated compute node in EL9. Report the working directory, Python executable, ATLAS project/version, and ROOT version using a non-login shell. Run a small PyROOT histogram check. Then use atlas-rucio to call rucio_whoami and report my Rucio account. Do not print the environment or any credential contents. \n \n For AMI, use a real dataset already relevant to your analysis: \n \n Use atlas-ami to retrieve metadata and provenance for DATASET_NAME. Make a bounded query to the remote AMI service and report the result or exact error. Do not substitute a local PMG cross-section lookup for this authentication test. \n \n Replace  DATASET_NAME  before sending it. A PMG file lookup on CVMFS can succeed without authenticating to AMI, so it is not sufficient for this test. Once both checks work, ask for a bounded discovery task, then for analysis code using the returned dataset names. Large downloads, replication-rule changes, and production submissions should be separate explicit tasks. \n Codex's shell sandbox and the MCP processes have separate execution paths. Shell network restrictions do not themselves restrict MCP network traffic; there is no need to enable unrestricted shell networking solely for the catalog clients. Their service credentials and server capabilities control those calls. \n 8. Give the agent project context \n Add instructions to your project's  AGENTS.md ; merge with any existing file. A starter is available as  AGENTS.md.example . Tell Codex which release is already active, how to build/test the project, which inputs to use, and how many resources the job has. The model does not automatically know the contents of our ChatGPT conversation. \n A short  PROJECT_CONTEXT.md  is useful for changing details: \n # Project context\n\n- Goal: describe the analysis task.\n- Release: exact project, release, and platform used by the analysis.\n- Build setup: absolute path, or none.\n- Input data: exact paths or dataset names; nominate a small test sample.\n- Outputs: the intended writable project/output directory.\n- Resources: allocated CPUs, memory, GPUs, and wall time.\n- Validation: the command and expected result for a small successful test.\n- Current status: completed work, known problems, and the next task. \n Keep credentials out of these files. For example, ask: “Read AGENTS.md and PROJECT_CONTEXT.md, inspect the existing analysis, implement the next step, and run the small validation sample.” \n 9. Renew, resume, and finish \n If the proxy expires, leave Codex and renew it  in the same terminal, at the same path : \n atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" && codex resume --last \n The helper validates the replacement before publishing it; a failed renewal leaves the previous proxy intact and does not resume Codex. Restarting Codex also restarts its stdio servers, which avoids keeping a client authenticated with an expired credential. The  env_vars  values are read when the servers start. Use  codex resume  to choose a saved conversation instead of automatically selecting the last one. \n If the allocation ended, first obtain a  new allocation , enter EL9, create a new proxy, re-export your Rucio account, and select the same ATLAS release/build. Then resume from the project directory. Resuming restores conversation history, not the old process, container, GPU allocation, or software environment. \n With  tmux , detach using  Ctrl+B , then  D , and reconnect to the same login node before  tmux attach -t atlas-codex . The Slurm time limit still applies. \n When finished, exit Codex, remove the session proxy, and then leave the container and allocation: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-destroy -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\"\nrmdir \"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\"\nunset X509_USER_PROXY ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\nexit\n# Back in the allocation's Ubuntu shell: exit again to release it. \n Destroying a local proxy file removes that copy; it does not revoke every token already issued using it. The workflow relies on short-lived credentials and ending the associated client processes. \n Short sequence for later sessions \n After obtaining resources and entering a fresh EL9 container, return to the same checkout. If you have not yet created a proxy in this container, use: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nMCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\nexport RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n Continue only after successful proxy creation and a positive remaining lifetime. If you already created a valid proxy in this container, reuse it as described in  Prepare a personal grid proxy . Then select your release: \n read -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi\n# Source your local build setup here if needed. \n Once the proxy, release, and local build setup are ready, resume: \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())'\ncodex resume \n Installation, MCP registration, and Codex login normally persist. The allocation, container, software setup, and proxy do not. Optional  .bashrc  additions should define helpers or add the Codex executable directory to  PATH ; do not automatically start Codex, mint proxies, or enter containers at login. \n Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n codex: command not found  inside EL9 \n Add the actual installation directory to  PATH  inside the container. Check whether the host and container expose different home paths. \n \n \n Device login is unavailable \n Check account/workspace device-code settings and the official alternative authentication flows. Codex login is independent of VOMS authentication. \n \n \n ROOT works in your shell but fails in Codex \n Start Codex after  asetup ; check non-login shell settings and inherited Python/library paths. Do not activate the MCP environment in the analysis shell. \n \n \n Pixi cannot resolve/install \n Confirm x86_64/EL9, package-source connectivity, quota, and the downloaded manifest. Preserve the error; do not solve it by installing into the ATLAS release. \n \n \n MCP initialization fails \n Check the absolute executable path, executable permission, installed lock file, forwarded proxy/account variables, and  /mcp  error details. No startup banner may be printed to stdout by the launcher. \n \n \n VOMS reports  No credentials found! \n Check  HOME  and the certificate/key filenames inside EL9. Pass their readable paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key , as in  Prepare a personal grid proxy . A  .globus  directory in your current working directory is not necessarily  $HOME/.globus . If the error persists, inspect any preceding credential-loading error. \n \n \n VOMS reports  Error creating proxy certificate: : Invalid argument \n Check whether  X509_USER_PROXY  is empty or names an invalid output path. Create a private directory, export a nonempty filename, and pass it with  -out ; see  grid-authentication troubleshooting . \n \n \n VOMS cannot load the key or reports a bad passphrase \n Check key permissions, file format, and the grid-key passphrase. For later authentication failures, also check certificate expiry and ATLAS registration. \n \n \n Rucio reports a wrong/unknown account \n Use the Rucio account associated with your grid identity, not automatically your Unity username. \n \n \n Proxy renewed but calls still fail \n Check remaining lifetime and restart Codex so both stdio clients are recreated. A new node/container needs a newly accessible proxy path. \n \n \n TLS validation reports expired CRLs \n Refresh the writable Pixi CA bundle using the command below, then retry. Do not disable TLS verification. \n \n \n Linux sandbox fails inside Apptainer \n The host may restrict nested namespaces/bubblewrap. Record the exact error and ask Unity support for the supported configuration. An Apptainer shell with home/work mounts is not itself a replacement for Codex's file-access sandbox. \n \n \n \n For an expired-CRL error, refresh the isolated tool environment's certificate revocation lists: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" sh -c '\"$X509_CERT_DIR/refresh_crls.sh\"' \n References \n \n OpenAI:  Codex CLI ,  authentication ,  MCP configuration ,  configuration reference , and  CLI commands . \n OpenAI:  permissions ,  sandbox requirements , and  AGENTS.md instructions . \n Rucio MCP , its  configuration , and  conda-forge package recipe . \n AMI MCP , its  configuration , and  conda-forge package recipe . \n Pixi installation  and  lock files . \n Unity:  interactive jobs  and  SSH . \n \n"}, {"title": "1. What runs where", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#overview", "text": "1. What runs where \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Component \n Environment and purpose \n \n \n \n \n Codex CLI \n Runs in EL9 on your allocated compute node, in your analysis directory. Its shell commands inherit the ATLAS release environment. \n \n \n Rucio and AMI MCP servers \n Child processes started by Codex in the same container, under your Unix account. They communicate over stdin/stdout; there is no listening HTTP port. \n \n \n MCP Python environment \n A separate Pixi environment containing the clients and grid dependencies. It does not replace the Python used by your analysis. \n \n \n VOMS proxy \n A short-lived file created by you before starting Codex; both MCP servers use it to authenticate as you. \n \n \n \n You need a Unity account and group work directory, an OpenAI sign-in method enabled for Codex, and an active ATLAS grid identity. Complete  Grid certificates and authentication  to obtain and register a certificate and install its encrypted private key on Unity. A Unity login, CERN password, or Codex login does not replace the grid credential. \n Complete  Setting up the environment  first, including the one-time creation of your work/project directories and the  .bashrc  configuration , then  install Pixi . This chapter uses your saved  USER_PI  and  ATLAS_WORKDIR , Unity  $USER , and the  unity_cpu ,  unity_gpu ,  setupATLAS , and  atlas_gpu  commands. \n The compute node needs outbound connectivity to the sign-in/model service and ATLAS endpoints; installation additionally needs access to the package sources. This chapter starts with  dataset discovery and metadata queries : Rucio MCP is explicitly launched in read-only mode. \n"}, {"title": "2. Allocate resources and enter EL9", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#container", "text": "2. Allocate resources and enter EL9 \n From your computer, connect using the  local SSH configuration from Using VS Code : \n ssh unity \n For a session that can survive an SSH disconnect, start  tmux new -s atlas-codex  on the login node first and record its hostname, following the  session guidance . Then request resources: \n # Unity login node\nunity_cpu \n The default helper requests four CPUs, 16 GB of memory, and two hours. Once Unity gives you a compute-node shell, check  hostname -f  and  echo \"$SLURM_JOB_ID\" , then start the container. If this new shell has not loaded your configuration, run  source ~/.bashrc  first. \n # Ubuntu compute node\nmodule load apptainer/latest\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\nsetupATLAS -c el9 \n For GPU analysis, use this alternative sequence.  unity_gpu  requests one GPU with the same CPU, memory, and time limits;  atlas_gpu  loads Apptainer and supplies the GPU passthrough options from the  environment chapter : \n # Unity login node\nunity_gpu\n\n# After allocation, on the Ubuntu GPU compute node\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\natlas_gpu \n /work  is normally visible inside the container without an additional  -m /work . If a required path is missing, follow the  mount guidance . \n Inside EL9, explicitly enter your analysis work area and check the environment: \n # Inside EL9\ncd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR\"\ncat /etc/os-release\nuname -m\nprintf 'Container home: %s\\n' \"$HOME\" \n This chapter targets  x86_64  nodes. On the first visit, install the tools in this fresh container before running  asetup . On later visits, reuse the installation and go to  Prepare a proxy , then  Load ATLAS and start Codex . \n"}, {"title": "3. Install Codex and sign in", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#codex-install", "text": "3. Install Codex and sign in \n Run the official standalone installer  inside EL9 . It does not require Node.js or a Python environment: \n curl -fsSL https://chatgpt.com/codex/install.sh | sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\ncodex --version \n The installer currently uses  ~/.local/bin . Keep the installation in a persistent directory visible in future containers. If the launcher changes the apparent home directory, use the paths visible  inside EL9  consistently. \n Use device authentication on the remote node: \n codex login --device-auth\ncodex login status \n Open the printed URL on your own computer and enter the one-time code. This requires device-code authentication to be enabled in the relevant ChatGPT account/workspace settings. If it is unavailable, use the alternatives in the  official authentication guide , or ask the workspace administrator to enable the supported flow. You do not need a browser running on the compute node. \n Codex normally retains the login in its configured credential store, so a new allocation does not imply a new login. Keep its configuration and credentials private; do not put them in your analysis repository. This authentication is only for Codex—ATLAS authentication comes below. \n"}, {"title": "4. Install isolated MCP tools", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#mcp-install", "text": "4. Install isolated MCP tools \n Use a dedicated Pixi environment for the MCP servers. In particular,  do not  pip install  them into the Python selected by  asetup , and do not activate their environment in your analysis shell. Their dependency versions serve the catalog clients, while analysis code must keep the release's Python and ROOT libraries. \n This section assumes Pixi is installed as described in  Using Pixi . Check that the installation is visible inside EL9: \n \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\" --version \n The workflow uses a supplied manifest, following the earlier chapter's  existing-project instructions . Download the  manifest  and  environment launcher  to the same private directory: \n MCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\ninstall -d -m 700 \"$MCP_DIR\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/codex/pixi.toml \\\n  --output \"$MCP_DIR/pixi.toml\"\ncurl --fail --location \\\n  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rafaellopesdesa/unity_atlas/main/examples/codex/atlas-mcp-env.sh \\\n  --output \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nchmod u+x \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n The manifest uses conda-forge packages, including pyAMI through the AMI package's dependencies, and pins the MCP versions: \n [workspace]\nname = \"atlas-mcp\"\nchannels = [\"conda-forge\"]\nplatforms = [\"linux-64\"]\n\n[dependencies]\npython = \"3.11.*\"\nrucio-mcp = \"==0.10.0\"\nami-mcp = \"==0.3.1\"\nvoms = \"*\"\nvoms-lsc = \"*\"\nca-policy-lcg = \"*\" \n Install before the first MCP handshake, so Codex does not need to wait for dependency resolution and downloads: \n env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$(id -un)\" PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LANG=C.UTF-8 \\\n  \"$HOME/.pixi/bin/pixi\" install --manifest-path \"$MCP_DIR/pixi.toml\" \n Keep the generated  pixi.lock  with the manifest; the launcher uses  --locked  to prevent silent re-resolution at server startup. Pixi generates this lock file during installation. If home quota is limited, choose a private persistent work directory for  MCP_DIR   before downloading and installing . Use that directory in every subsequent  MCP_DIR  assignment and in the absolute launcher paths below. \n The launcher starts Pixi with an explicit clean environment and forwards only the relevant proxy/account settings. This keeps ATLAS  PYTHONPATH ,  PYTHONHOME , and  LD_LIBRARY_PATH  out of the MCP clients. Pixi then activates its own grid CA environment. Codex's parent shell remains unchanged. Do not add  pixi run --clean-env  to this launcher: that additional filter can remove the explicitly forwarded grid variables. \n If Pixi is installed elsewhere, set  ATLAS_MCP_PIXI  to its absolute executable path, and substitute that path in the installation command. The launcher accepts this override. \n Check that the installed packages import and their command-line interfaces work: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" python -c \\\n  'import sys, importlib.metadata as m, pyAMI.client, rucio.client; print(sys.version); print(\"rucio-mcp\", m.version(\"rucio-mcp\")); print(\"ami-mcp\", m.version(\"ami-mcp\"))'\n\"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-init --help \n These are installation checks, not authenticated service tests. \n"}, {"title": "5. Prepare a personal grid proxy", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#grid-auth", "text": "5. Prepare a personal grid proxy \n Perform authentication yourself in the terminal  before starting Codex . The MCP servers must not stop during their stdin/stdout protocol to ask for a private-key passphrase. \n Use the certificate and encrypted private key installed in  Grid certificates and authentication :  ~/.globus/usercert.pem  and  ~/.globus/userkey.pem . Install that chapter's recommended  atlas_proxy  helper  once as well. Here it runs VOMS commands through the isolated MCP launcher, which supplies its own grid tools and CA bundle; no additional  lsetup rucio  is needed. \n Check the paths  inside EL9 ; the container may expose your home at a different path than the Ubuntu host. The helper passes the certificate and key paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key . If your files have different names or locations, adjust those paths in your installed  atlas-proxy.sh . \n printf 'HOME=%s\\n' \"$HOME\"\nls -l \"$HOME/.globus/usercert.pem\" \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\"\nchmod 700 \"$HOME/.globus\"\nchmod 600 \"$HOME/.globus/userkey.pem\" \n Set the launcher path and your  Rucio account name , which may differ from your Unity username: \n export RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nMCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\" \n If you already created a valid, readable proxy in this same container using the  grid-authentication chapter , keep its existing  X509_USER_PROXY  and  ATLAS_PROXY_DIR  values. Check its lifetime with the command below and skip the creation block while it remains valid: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-info -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\" -timeleft \n Otherwise, create the proxy with the same helper used elsewhere in this guide, passing the isolated launcher as its second argument: \n atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n The helper creates a private directory and supplies an explicit  -out  filename. Stop if proxy creation fails. Keep the exported  X509_USER_PROXY ; do not replace it with the output of  voms-proxy-info -path  after a failure, which can leave it empty. See  grid-authentication troubleshooting  for both credential discovery and invalid output-path errors. \n The passphrase prompt unlocks your grid private key; it is not asking for your Unity or Codex password. The last command must report a positive remaining lifetime. A requested 12-hour lifetime is a request, not a guarantee—inspect the actual expiry. \n The proxy stays on this node/container and is passed to the clients by  filename , not by putting its contents into the conversation or configuration. Recreate it after moving to another node or a container with a different  /tmp . The original certificate/key and the short-lived proxy are different files. \n The MCP  rucio_whoami  check below will verify the actual service-side account. The server's  --site atlas  selects its bundled ATLAS configuration. \n"}, {"title": "6. Give Codex the MCP configuration", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#configuration", "text": "6. Give Codex the MCP configuration \n Print the launcher's absolute path: \n printf '%s\\n' \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nmkdir -p \"$HOME/.codex\" \n Merge the following into your existing  ~/.codex/config.toml  inside EL9  ( download the example ). Replace each complete  command  value with the absolute launcher path printed above. TOML command paths are literal: do not put  $HOME  in them. If you already have any of these tables, edit them instead of adding duplicate tables. Put the top-level settings before the first table header. \n # Merge into your existing ~/.codex/config.toml; do not replace it blindly.\n# Top-level settings must appear before any [table] headers.\napproval_policy = \"on-request\"\nsandbox_mode = \"workspace-write\"\nallow_login_shell = false\n\n[shell_environment_policy]\ninherit = \"all\"\nignore_default_excludes = false\n\n[mcp_servers.atlas-rucio]\ncommand = \"/ABSOLUTE/HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nargs = [\"rucio-mcp\", \"serve\", \"--site\", \"atlas\", \"--auth-type\", \"x509\", \"--read-only\"]\nenv_vars = [\"X509_USER_PROXY\", \"RUCIO_ACCOUNT\", \"ATLAS_MCP_PIXI\", \"ATLAS_PMGXSEC_PATH\"]\nstartup_timeout_sec = 120\ntool_timeout_sec = 120\n\n[mcp_servers.atlas-ami]\ncommand = \"/ABSOLUTE/HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp/atlas-mcp-env.sh\"\nargs = [\"ami-mcp\", \"serve\"]\nenv_vars = [\"X509_USER_PROXY\", \"ATLAS_MCP_PIXI\", \"ATLAS_PMGXSEC_PATH\"]\nstartup_timeout_sec = 120\ntool_timeout_sec = 120 \n The settings serve two different environments: \n \n allow_login_shell = false  and  inherit = \"all\"  preserve the exported ATLAS setup when Codex runs analysis commands. The explicit default-exclusion setting filters variable names containing  KEY ,  SECRET , or  TOKEN ; it does not prevent processes from reading files accessible to your account. \n Each MCP server's  env_vars  forwards the  current  proxy path at startup. A new allocation can use a new path without rewriting the TOML. The wrapper isolates the server's Python environment. \n --read-only  blocks Rucio MCP write operations. AMI's standard tools are oriented toward metadata queries; AMI's own role permissions remain authoritative. These settings do not impose a separate read-only restriction on Codex's general shell. \n \n These user-level settings apply to other Codex sessions using the same configuration. Codex also supports project-scoped  .codex/config.toml  in trusted projects if you prefer to scope the ATLAS-specific settings; keep personal absolute paths out of shared commits. \n Verify the entries: \n codex mcp list\ncodex mcp get atlas-rucio\ncodex mcp get atlas-ami \n These commands confirm registration, not successful grid access.  Do not run  codex mcp login  for these stdio servers ; that command is for compatible HTTP/OAuth servers. Their authentication is the VOMS proxy you just created. \n Codex will launch both commands itself. You do not need to keep two extra terminals open running  rucio-mcp serve  and  ami-mcp serve , start them in the background, or expose a network port. A manually started stdio server waiting silently for protocol input is not an interactive command prompt. \n"}, {"title": "7. Load ATLAS and start Codex", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#start", "text": "7. Load ATLAS and start Codex \n In the same EL9 shell, enter your existing analysis checkout. Replace  PROJECT_NAME  with its directory name, as in the Pixi and JupyterLab chapters. Select the full  project,version  release string described in  Load analysis software , then source any generated local build setup: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nread -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi\n# If needed, use your real generated setup path:\n# source /absolute/path/to/build/PLATFORM/setup.sh \n Continue only after the release and build setup succeed.  Then start Codex from that project directory: \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\npython -c 'import sys, ROOT; print(sys.executable); print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())'\ncodex --sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request \n Set the thread count to fit your actual allocation. Run  nvidia-smi  and a small framework-specific check before GPU work. Starting Codex on Ubuntu and later entering a container in one subprocess will not relocate the running agent and its other processes. \n The default workspace is the directory where you start Codex. If a necessary output directory lies elsewhere, add that particular directory with  --add-dir /absolute/output/path . Keep heavy builds, event loops, and training within the Slurm allocation. \n Inside Codex, use  /mcp  to inspect the active connections. Start with this small validation request: \n \n Verify that you are on the allocated compute node in EL9. Report the working directory, Python executable, ATLAS project/version, and ROOT version using a non-login shell. Run a small PyROOT histogram check. Then use atlas-rucio to call rucio_whoami and report my Rucio account. Do not print the environment or any credential contents. \n \n For AMI, use a real dataset already relevant to your analysis: \n \n Use atlas-ami to retrieve metadata and provenance for DATASET_NAME. Make a bounded query to the remote AMI service and report the result or exact error. Do not substitute a local PMG cross-section lookup for this authentication test. \n \n Replace  DATASET_NAME  before sending it. A PMG file lookup on CVMFS can succeed without authenticating to AMI, so it is not sufficient for this test. Once both checks work, ask for a bounded discovery task, then for analysis code using the returned dataset names. Large downloads, replication-rule changes, and production submissions should be separate explicit tasks. \n Codex's shell sandbox and the MCP processes have separate execution paths. Shell network restrictions do not themselves restrict MCP network traffic; there is no need to enable unrestricted shell networking solely for the catalog clients. Their service credentials and server capabilities control those calls. \n"}, {"title": "8. Give the agent project context", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#project-context", "text": "8. Give the agent project context \n Add instructions to your project's  AGENTS.md ; merge with any existing file. A starter is available as  AGENTS.md.example . Tell Codex which release is already active, how to build/test the project, which inputs to use, and how many resources the job has. The model does not automatically know the contents of our ChatGPT conversation. \n A short  PROJECT_CONTEXT.md  is useful for changing details: \n # Project context\n\n- Goal: describe the analysis task.\n- Release: exact project, release, and platform used by the analysis.\n- Build setup: absolute path, or none.\n- Input data: exact paths or dataset names; nominate a small test sample.\n- Outputs: the intended writable project/output directory.\n- Resources: allocated CPUs, memory, GPUs, and wall time.\n- Validation: the command and expected result for a small successful test.\n- Current status: completed work, known problems, and the next task. \n Keep credentials out of these files. For example, ask: “Read AGENTS.md and PROJECT_CONTEXT.md, inspect the existing analysis, implement the next step, and run the small validation sample.” \n"}, {"title": "9. Renew, resume, and finish", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#sessions", "text": "9. Renew, resume, and finish \n If the proxy expires, leave Codex and renew it  in the same terminal, at the same path : \n atlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" && codex resume --last \n The helper validates the replacement before publishing it; a failed renewal leaves the previous proxy intact and does not resume Codex. Restarting Codex also restarts its stdio servers, which avoids keeping a client authenticated with an expired credential. The  env_vars  values are read when the servers start. Use  codex resume  to choose a saved conversation instead of automatically selecting the last one. \n If the allocation ended, first obtain a  new allocation , enter EL9, create a new proxy, re-export your Rucio account, and select the same ATLAS release/build. Then resume from the project directory. Resuming restores conversation history, not the old process, container, GPU allocation, or software environment. \n With  tmux , detach using  Ctrl+B , then  D , and reconnect to the same login node before  tmux attach -t atlas-codex . The Slurm time limit still applies. \n When finished, exit Codex, remove the session proxy, and then leave the container and allocation: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" voms-proxy-destroy -file \"$X509_USER_PROXY\"\nrmdir \"$ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\"\nunset X509_USER_PROXY ATLAS_PROXY_DIR\nexit\n# Back in the allocation's Ubuntu shell: exit again to release it. \n Destroying a local proxy file removes that copy; it does not revoke every token already issued using it. The workflow relies on short-lived credentials and ending the associated client processes. \n Short sequence for later sessions \n After obtaining resources and entering a fresh EL9 container, return to the same checkout. If you have not yet created a proxy in this container, use: \n cd \"$ATLAS_WORKDIR/PROJECT_NAME\"\nMCP_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/unity-atlas-mcp\"\nexport RUCIO_ACCOUNT=YOUR_RUCIO_ACCOUNT\nsource \"$HOME/.config/unity-atlas/atlas-proxy.sh\"\natlas_proxy 12:00 \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" \n Continue only after successful proxy creation and a positive remaining lifetime. If you already created a valid proxy in this container, reuse it as described in  Prepare a personal grid proxy . Then select your release: \n read -r -p 'ATLAS project,version: ' ATLAS_RELEASE\nif [[ \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\" == *,* ]]; then\n    asetup \"$ATLAS_RELEASE\"\nelse\n    echo 'Enter a project and exact version, separated by a comma.'\nfi\n# Source your local build setup here if needed. \n Once the proxy, release, and local build setup are ready, resume: \n export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\"\nexport OMP_NUM_THREADS=4\npython -c 'import ROOT; print(ROOT.gROOT.GetVersion())'\ncodex resume \n Installation, MCP registration, and Codex login normally persist. The allocation, container, software setup, and proxy do not. Optional  .bashrc  additions should define helpers or add the Codex executable directory to  PATH ; do not automatically start Codex, mint proxies, or enter containers at login. \n"}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#troubleshooting", "text": "Troubleshooting \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Symptom \n What to check \n \n \n \n \n codex: command not found  inside EL9 \n Add the actual installation directory to  PATH  inside the container. Check whether the host and container expose different home paths. \n \n \n Device login is unavailable \n Check account/workspace device-code settings and the official alternative authentication flows. Codex login is independent of VOMS authentication. \n \n \n ROOT works in your shell but fails in Codex \n Start Codex after  asetup ; check non-login shell settings and inherited Python/library paths. Do not activate the MCP environment in the analysis shell. \n \n \n Pixi cannot resolve/install \n Confirm x86_64/EL9, package-source connectivity, quota, and the downloaded manifest. Preserve the error; do not solve it by installing into the ATLAS release. \n \n \n MCP initialization fails \n Check the absolute executable path, executable permission, installed lock file, forwarded proxy/account variables, and  /mcp  error details. No startup banner may be printed to stdout by the launcher. \n \n \n VOMS reports  No credentials found! \n Check  HOME  and the certificate/key filenames inside EL9. Pass their readable paths explicitly with  -cert  and  -key , as in  Prepare a personal grid proxy . A  .globus  directory in your current working directory is not necessarily  $HOME/.globus . If the error persists, inspect any preceding credential-loading error. \n \n \n VOMS reports  Error creating proxy certificate: : Invalid argument \n Check whether  X509_USER_PROXY  is empty or names an invalid output path. Create a private directory, export a nonempty filename, and pass it with  -out ; see  grid-authentication troubleshooting . \n \n \n VOMS cannot load the key or reports a bad passphrase \n Check key permissions, file format, and the grid-key passphrase. For later authentication failures, also check certificate expiry and ATLAS registration. \n \n \n Rucio reports a wrong/unknown account \n Use the Rucio account associated with your grid identity, not automatically your Unity username. \n \n \n Proxy renewed but calls still fail \n Check remaining lifetime and restart Codex so both stdio clients are recreated. A new node/container needs a newly accessible proxy path. \n \n \n TLS validation reports expired CRLs \n Refresh the writable Pixi CA bundle using the command below, then retry. Do not disable TLS verification. \n \n \n Linux sandbox fails inside Apptainer \n The host may restrict nested namespaces/bubblewrap. Record the exact error and ask Unity support for the supported configuration. An Apptainer shell with home/work mounts is not itself a replacement for Codex's file-access sandbox. \n \n \n \n For an expired-CRL error, refresh the isolated tool environment's certificate revocation lists: \n \"$MCP_DIR/atlas-mcp-env.sh\" sh -c '\"$X509_CERT_DIR/refresh_crls.sh\"' \n"}, {"title": "References", "url": "codex-agents/index.html#references", "text": "References \n \n OpenAI:  Codex CLI ,  authentication ,  MCP configuration ,  configuration reference , and  CLI commands . \n OpenAI:  permissions ,  sandbox requirements , and  AGENTS.md instructions . \n Rucio MCP , its  configuration , and  conda-forge package recipe . \n AMI MCP , its  configuration , and  conda-forge package recipe . \n Pixi installation  and  lock files . \n Unity:  interactive jobs  and  SSH . \n \n"}];
